{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is an irrational number?", "options": ["22/7", "3.1416", "√25", "√2"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option D. √2 is irrational because it cannot be expressed in the form p/q where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0, and its decimal expansion is non-terminating and non-recurring.", "rubric_keywords": ["irrational number", "non-terminating", "non-recurring", "square root"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The decimal expansion of a rational number is either terminating or non-terminating ___.", "options": ["recurring", "terminating", "irrational", "prime"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "recurring. Explanation: Every rational number has a decimal expansion that is either terminating or non-terminating repeating (recurring).", "rubric_keywords": ["decimal expansion", "recurring", "rational number"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express 0.6̄ in the form p/q, where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let x = 0.666... (1). Multiply by 10: 10x = 6.666... (2). Subtract (1) from (2): 9x = 6, so x = 6/9 = 2/3. Final answer: 2/3.", "rubric_keywords": ["p/q form", "algebraic method", "recurring decimal", "2/3"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Rationalize the denominator of 1 / (7 + 3√2).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Multiply numerator and denominator by conjugate (7 - 3√2). Denominator becomes (7)^2 - (3√2)^2 = 49 - 18 = 31. Result is (7 - 3√2) / 31.", "rubric_keywords": ["rationalizing factor", "conjugate", "denominator", "31"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Simplify: (2^24 × 3^12) / (6^12) and find the value of x if 2^(x-1) = 32.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Part 1: 6^12 = (2 × 3)^12 = 2^12 × 3^12. Expression becomes (2^24 × 3^12) / (2^12 × 3^12) = 2^(24-12) = 2^12 = 4096. Part 2: 2^(x-1) = 32 = 2^5. Therefore, x - 1 = 5, which gives x = 6.", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "simplification", "base", "exponent"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Simplify the following expression: 3√45 - √20 + 4√5", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "3√45 = 3√(9×5) = 3 × 3√5 = 9√5. √20 = √(4×5) = 2√5. 4√5 remains 4√5. Combining terms: 9√5 - 2√5 + 4√5 = (9 - 2 + 4)√5 = 11√5.", "rubric_keywords": ["surds", "simplification", "like terms", "11√5"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): √2 is an irrational number. Reason (R): The square root of every positive integer is irrational.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A is true but R is false. Explanation: √2 is indeed irrational, but the square root of every positive integer is not irrational (e.g., √4 = 2, which is rational).", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "irrational", "counterexample"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions: In a mathematics club activity, students are exploring real numbers. Priya represents √2 and √3 on a spiral. (i) What is the distance from origin to √3 on the number line spiral? (ii) Find the value of (256)^(0.16) × (256)^(0.09).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) The distance from the origin is √3 units. (ii) Using law of exponents a^m × a^n = a^(m+n): (256)^(0.16 + 0.09) = (256)^(0.25) = (256)^(1/4) = (4^4)^(1/4) = 4.", "rubric_keywords": ["spiral", "laws of exponents", "rational exponent", "4"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I (Number/Expression) with Column II (Classification):\nColumn I: 1. √9, 2. 0, 3. 22/7, 4. 2 - √3\nColumn II: A. Irrational number, B. Rational number (Integer), C. Rational number (Fraction), D. Whole number and Integer", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A", "1-D, 2-B, 3-C, 4-A", "1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (√9 = 3, which is an integer), 2-D (0 is whole and integer), 3-C (22/7 is rational fraction), 4-A (2 - √3 is irrational). Correct option code: 1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A.", "rubric_keywords": ["Column I", "Column II", "classification", "rational", "irrational"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find three rational numbers between 3 and 4.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Method 1: Since we need 3 numbers, let n = 3. Multiply and divide 3 and 4 by 3+1 = 4. 3 = 12/4 and 4 = 16/4. Three rational numbers are 13/4, 14/4 (or 7/2), and 15/4.", "rubric_keywords": ["rational numbers", "equivalent fractions", "mean method", "denominator"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a rational number?", "options": ["\\sqrt{2}", "\\pi", "0", "\\sqrt{5}"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C is correct because 0 can be written in the form p/q where p = 0 and q is any non-zero integer (e.g., 0/1). \\sqrt{2}, \\pi, and \\sqrt{5} are irrational numbers.", "rubric_keywords": ["rational number", "zero", "p/q form", "irrational"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the decimal expansion of 3/13 and state the kind of decimal expansion.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Performing long division for 3 \\div 13 gives 0.230769230769... which can be written as 0.\\overline{230769}. Since the remainder never becomes zero and a block of digits repeats continuously, the decimal expansion is non-terminating recurring.", "rubric_keywords": ["long division", "non-terminating recurring", "repeating block", "remainder"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Simplify the expression: 2^{2/3} \\cdot 2^{1/3}", "options": ["2", "4", "2^{1/3}", "2^{3/2}"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A is correct. Using the law of exponents a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}, we get 2^{2/3 + 1/3} = 2^{3/3} = 2^1 = 2.", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "addition of fractions", "base", "power"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Rationalize the denominator of \\frac{1}{7 + 4\\sqrt{3}}.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is (7 - 4\\sqrt{3}). We get \\frac{1}{7 + 4\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\frac{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}}{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}}{(7)^2 - (4\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\frac{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}}{49 - 48} = \\frac{7 - 4\\sqrt{3}}{1} = 7 - 4\\sqrt{3}.", "rubric_keywords": ["conjugate", "denominator", "rationalize", "algebraic identity"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Every real number is either a rational number or an ___ number.", "options": ["irrational", "integer", "fraction", "prime"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "irrational. Explanation: The collection of all rational and irrational numbers together make up the collection of real numbers.", "rubric_keywords": ["irrational number", "real numbers", "collection"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Represent \\sqrt{9.3} geometrically on the number line.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Steps of construction: 1. Draw a line segment AB = 9.3 units. 2. Extend AB to C such that BC = 1 unit, so AC = 10.3 units. 3. Find the midpoint O of AC. 4. Draw a semicircle with centre O and radius OC (or OA). 5. Draw a perpendicular line from B intersecting the semicircle at point D. 6. Taking B as the centre and BD as radius, draw an arc intersecting the number line at E. The distance BE represents \\sqrt{9.3}.", "rubric_keywords": ["geometric construction", "semicircle", "perpendicular", "number line", "midpoint"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is an irrational number between 1 and 2?", "options": ["1.5", "\\sqrt{2}", "\\frac{3}{2}", "1.414"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B is correct. \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.4142135... which is non-terminating non-recurring and lies between 1 and 2. 1.5 and 3/2 are rational numbers, and 1.414 is a terminating decimal (hence rational).", "rubric_keywords": ["irrational number", "non-terminating non-recurring", "between 1 and 2"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express 0.\\overline{6} in the form p/q, where p and q are integers and q \\neq 0.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let x = 0.6666... --- (1). Since one digit is repeating, multiply both sides by 10: 10x = 6.6666... --- (2). Subtract equation (1) from equation (2): 10x - x = 6.6666... - 0.6666... \\Rightarrow 9x = 6 \\Rightarrow x = 6/9 = 2/3.", "rubric_keywords": ["algebraic method", "multiplying by 10", "subtracting equations", "p/q form"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the laws of exponents in Column I with their simplified form in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. a^m \\cdot a^n\n2. (a^m)^n\n3. \\frac{a^m}{a^n}\n4. a^m \\cdot b^m\n\nColumn II:\nA. a^{m-n}\nB. a^{mn}\nC. (ab)^m\nD. a^{m+n}", "options": ["1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-D, 2-C, 3-A, 4-B", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct pairing: 1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C. Explanation: 1. a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}, 2. (a^m)^n = a^{mn}, 3. a^m/a^n = a^{m-n}, 4. a^m \\cdot b^m = (ab)^m.", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "matching", "algebraic identities"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find four rational numbers between 3 and 4.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To find four rational numbers, we can write 3 and 4 with denominator 4 + 1 = 5. Thus, 3 = 15/5 and 4 = 20/5. Four rational numbers between them are 16/5, 17/5, 18/5, and 19/5.", "rubric_keywords": ["rational numbers", "denominator", "equivalent fractions", "between two numbers"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a rational number?", "options": ["√2", "π", "0/5", "√3"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "0/5 is a rational number because it can be expressed in the form p/q where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0 (q = 5). The others are irrational numbers.", "rubric_keywords": ["rational number", "p/q form", "denominator non-zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The decimal representation of a rational number is either terminating or non-terminating ___ decimal.", "options": ["repeating", "non-repeating", "terminating", "irrational"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "repeating. Explanation: Rational numbers have decimal expansions that either terminate or are non-terminating and recurring/repeating.", "rubric_keywords": ["terminating", "repeating", "decimal expansion"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express 0.6̄ in the form p/q, where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let x = 0.666... (Equation 1). Multiply by 10 on both sides: 10x = 6.666... (Equation 2). Subtract Equation 1 from Equation 2: 9x = 6, which gives x = 6/9 = 2/3. Final Answer: 2/3.", "rubric_keywords": ["let x", "multiply by 10", "subtract", "2/3"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Rationalize the denominator of 1 / (2 + √3).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate (2 - √3). Expression: [1 * (2 - √3)] / [(2 + √3)(2 - √3)] = (2 - √3) / (2^2 - (√3)^2) = (2 - √3) / (4 - 3) = 2 - √3. Final Answer: 2 - √3.", "rubric_keywords": ["rationalize", "conjugate", "denominator", "2 - √3"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "If x = 3 + 2√2, find the value of x + (1/x).", "options": ["3", "6", "2√2", "3√2"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given x = 3 + 2√2. Then 1/x = 1 / (3 + 2√2) = 3 - 2√2. Therefore, x + (1/x) = (3 + 2√2) + (3 - 2√2) = 6.", "rubric_keywords": ["reciprocal", "conjugate", "6"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I with Column II: Column I: 1. √9, 2. √5, 3. 7/8, 4. 0.333... ; Column II: A. Irrational number, B. Rational number (terminating), C. Rational number (non-terminating repeating), D. Natural number / Rational number.", "options": ["1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-D (√9 = 3 which is a natural and rational number), 2-A (√5 is non-terminating non-recurring, hence irrational), 3-B (7/8 = 0.875, terminating decimal), 4-C (0.333... is non-terminating repeating).", "rubric_keywords": ["classification", "rational", "irrational", "terminating"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The sum of two rational numbers is always rational. Reason (R): Rational numbers are closed under addition.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. The closure property states that the sum of any two rational numbers always results in a rational number.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "closure property", "addition"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Represent √9.3 geometrically on the number line and write the steps of construction.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Steps: 1. Draw line segment AB = 9.3 units. 2. Extend AB to C such that BC = 1 unit. 3. Find midpoint O of AC. 4. Draw semicircle with center O and radius OA. 5. Draw perpendicular from B to intersect semicircle at D. BD equals √9.3 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["geometry", "semicircle", "perpendicular", "construction steps"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find two rational numbers between 1/3 and 1/2.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Make denominators equal by taking LCM of 3 and 2, which is 6. 1/3 = 2/6 and 1/2 = 3/6. Multiplying numerator and denominator by 3 gives 6/18 and 9/18. Two rational numbers between them are 7/18 and 8/18.", "rubric_keywords": ["LCM", "denominators", "equivalent fractions", "rational numbers"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Simplify the following expression completely using laws of exponents: (x^a / x^b)^(a+b) × (x^b / x^c)^(b+c) × (x^c / x^a)^(c+a).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Using laws of exponents (x^m / x^n = x^(m-n) and (x^m)^n = x^(mn)), the expression simplifies to x^((a-b)(a+b)) × x^((b-c)(b+c)) × x^((c-a)(c+a)) = x^(a^2 - b^2) × x^(b^2 - c^2) × x^(c^2 - a^2) = x^(a^2 - b^2 + b^2 - c^2 + c^2 - a^2) = x^0 = 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "algebraic identity", "simplification", "1"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a rational number between 3 and 4?", "options": ["3.5", "\\sqrt{10}", "\\pi", "3.101101110..."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "3.5 can be expressed as 7/2, which is a rational number. \\sqrt{10}, \\pi, and the non-terminating non-recurring decimal are irrational numbers.", "rubric_keywords": ["rational number", "terminating decimal", "fraction"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Every rational number is a ___ number.", "options": ["real", "integer", "natural", "irrational"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "real. Every rational and irrational number forms the collection of real numbers.", "rubric_keywords": ["real number", "rational number", "collection"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The decimal expansion of 1/11 is:", "options": ["0.09", "0.0909...", "0.9", "0.099..."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "0.0909... or 0.overline{09}. Division of 1 by 11 yields a repeating non-terminating decimal expansion.", "rubric_keywords": ["decimal expansion", "repeating", "non-terminating"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express 0.666... in the form p/q, where p and q are integers and q != 0.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let x = 0.666... (1). Multiply by 10: 10x = 6.666... (2). Subtract (1) from (2): 9x = 6 => x = 6/9 = 2/3.", "rubric_keywords": ["equation", "subtract", "fraction"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Rationalize the denominator of 1 / (7 + 3\\sqrt{2}).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate 7 - 3\\sqrt{2}. [1 / (7 + 3\\sqrt{2})] * [(7 - 3\\sqrt{2}) / (7 - 3\\sqrt{2})] = (7 - 3\\sqrt{2}) / [7^2 - (3\\sqrt{2})^2] = (7 - 3\\sqrt{2}) / (49 - 18) = (7 - 3\\sqrt{2}) / 31.", "rubric_keywords": ["rationalize", "conjugate", "denominator"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Simplify: 2^(2/3) * 2^(1/5)", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Using the law of exponents a^m * a^n = a^(m+n), we get 2^(2/3 + 1/5). Finding the common denominator: 2/3 + 1/5 = (10+3)/15 = 13/15. Thus, the simplified form is 2^(13/15).", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "addition of fractions", "base"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the numbers in Column I with their correct classification in Column II.\nColumn I:\n1. \\sqrt{25}\n2. \\sqrt{23}\n3. 0.3796\n4. 7.352818...\nColumn II:\nA. Irrational number\nB. Rational number (terminating)\nC. Rational number (integer)\nD. Rational number (non-terminating recurring)", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-C: \\sqrt{25} = 5 which is an integer (rational). 2-A: \\sqrt{23} cannot be simplified to a rational number, so it is irrational. 3-B: 0.3796 terminates, hence rational. 4-D: 7.352818... is non-terminating and recurring, hence rational.", "rubric_keywords": ["classification", "rational", "irrational"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): \\sqrt{3} is an irrational number.\nReason (R): The square root of any positive integer that is not a perfect square is an irrational number.", "options": ["A) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "B) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "C) A is true but R is false", "D) A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both Assertion and Reason are true, and Reason correctly explains why \\sqrt{3} is irrational because 3 is not a perfect square.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "perfect square"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Represent \\sqrt{9.3} geometrically on the number line and explain the steps of construction.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Steps: 1. Draw a line segment AB = 9.3 units and extend B by 1 unit to point C such that BC = 1. 2. Find the midpoint O of AC. 3. Draw a semicircle with centre O and radius OA. 4. Draw a perpendicular line from B intersecting the semicircle at D. Then BD = \\sqrt{9.3}. 5. With B as centre and radius BD, draw an arc intersecting the number line to locate \\sqrt{9.3}.", "rubric_keywords": ["geometric representation", "semicircle", "perpendicular", "radius"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions below:\nReal numbers comprise both rational and irrational numbers. A student takes two numbers (3 + \\sqrt{5}) and (3 - \\sqrt{5}).\n(i) Find the sum of these two numbers and state whether it is rational or irrational.\n(ii) Find the product of these two numbers and state whether it is rational or irrational.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Sum = (3 + \\sqrt{5}) + (3 - \\sqrt{5}) = 6, which is a rational number. (ii) Product = (3 + \\sqrt{5})(3 - \\sqrt{5}) = 3^2 - (\\sqrt{5})^2 = 9 - 5 = 4, which is a rational number.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum", "product", "rational number", "conjugate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is an irrational number?", "options": ["\\sqrt{4}", "\\sqrt{9}", "\\sqrt{7}", "\\sqrt{16}"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C (\\sqrt{7}) is correct because 7 is not a perfect square, making its square root non-terminating and non-recurring (irrational). \\sqrt{4} = 2, \\sqrt{9} = 3, and \\sqrt{16} = 4 are rational numbers.", "rubric_keywords": ["irrational number", "non-perfect square", "square root"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The decimal expansion of a rational number is either terminating or ___.", "options": ["non-terminating recurring", "non-terminating non-recurring", "terminating", "irrational"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "non-terminating recurring. Explanation: Every rational number has a decimal expansion that is either terminating or non-terminating repeating (recurring).", "rubric_keywords": ["decimal expansion", "rational number", "non-terminating recurring"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "What is the value of $(3 + \\sqrt{3})(3 - \\sqrt{3})$?", "options": ["6", "9", "3", "12"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A (6) is correct. Using the algebraic identity $(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2$, we get $(3)^2 - (\\sqrt{3})^2 = 9 - 3 = 6$.", "rubric_keywords": ["algebraic identity", "difference of squares", "simplification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express the decimal $0.\\overline{6}$ in the form \\frac{p}{q}, where p and q are integers and q \\neq 0.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let x = 0.666... (Equation 1). Multiply by 10 since 1 digit is repeating: 10x = 6.666... (Equation 2). Subtracting Equation 1 from Equation 2 gives 9x = 6, so x = 6/9 = 2/3. Final answer: 2/3.", "rubric_keywords": ["recurring decimal", "p/q form", "equations", "subtraction"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Rationalise the denominator of \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{6}}.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}: \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{6}} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}}{(\\sqrt{7})^2 - (\\sqrt{6})^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}}{7 - 6} = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}.", "rubric_keywords": ["rationalise denominator", "conjugate", "square root property"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Evaluate the expression using laws of exponents: $2^{\\frac{2}{3}} \\cdot 2^{\\frac{1}{3}}$.", "options": [], "variables": {"base": 2, "exp1": "2/3", "exp2": "1/3"}, "answer_key": "Using the laws of exponents $a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}$, we add the fractional exponents: $\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{3}{3} = 1$. Thus, $2^{\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}} = 2^1 = 2$. Final Answer: 2.", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "addition of fractions", "base and power"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Represent $\\sqrt{9.3}$ geometrically on the number line with detailed steps of construction.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Steps of Construction: 1. Draw a line segment AB = 9.3 units. 2. Extend AB to point C such that BC = 1 unit. 3. Find the midpoint O of AC. 4. With O as center and OA as radius, draw a semicircle. 5. Draw a perpendicular line from B intersecting the semicircle at point D; length BD = \\sqrt{9.3}. 6. Taking B as origin, draw an arc with radius BD intersecting the number line at point E, which represents $\\sqrt{9.3}$.", "rubric_keywords": ["geometric representation", "semicircle", "perpendicular", "number line"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The sum of a rational number and an irrational number is always an irrational number. Reason (R): The sum, difference, product and quotient of two irrational numbers is always an irrational number.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C is correct. Assertion (A) is true because the sum of any rational and irrational number is always irrational. Reason (R) is false because operations between two irrational numbers can sometimes result in a rational number (e.g., \\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 2).", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "rational and irrational", "counterexample"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I with Column II:\nColumn I: 1. 0, 2. -5, 3. \\sqrt{23}, 4. 3.1416\nColumn II: A. Integer but not natural number, B. Whole number and integer, C. Irrational number, D. Rational number terminating.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D is correct. Explanation: 0 is a whole number and integer (1-B). -5 is an integer but not a natural number (2-A). \\sqrt{23} is non-terminating non-recurring, hence irrational (3-C). 3.1416 has a terminating decimal expansion, hence a rational number (4-D).", "rubric_keywords": ["classification", "integers", "whole numbers", "irrational"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and solve:\nTwo students are simplifying radical expressions. Amit simplifies $\\sqrt{45} - 3\\sqrt{20} + 4\\sqrt{5}$ and Priya simplifies $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}}$.\n(i) Simplify Amit's expression.\n(ii) Rationalize the denominator of Priya's expression.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Amit's expression: $\\sqrt{45} = 3\\sqrt{5}$, $3\\sqrt{20} = 3(2\\sqrt{5}) = 6\\sqrt{5}$. Thus, $3\\sqrt{5} - 6\\sqrt{5} + 4\\sqrt{5} = (3 - 6 + 4)\\sqrt{5} = \\sqrt{5}$. (ii) Priya's expression: $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{2}} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2})}{3 - 2} = 2(3 - \\sqrt{6}) = 6 - 2\\sqrt{6}$.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "simplifying radicals", "rationalisation", "like surds"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is an irrational number?", "options": ["\\(\\sqrt{4}\\)", "\\(\\sqrt{9}\\)", "\\(\\sqrt{7}\\)", "\\(\\frac{22}{7}\\)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C: \\(\\sqrt{7}\\) is an irrational number because 7 is not a perfect square, making its decimal expansion non-terminating and non-recurring. \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2\\) and \\(\\sqrt{9} = 3\\) are rational numbers, and \\(\\frac{22}{7}\\) is expressed as the ratio of two integers.", "rubric_keywords": ["irrational number", "non-terminating non-recurring", "perfect square"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The decimal expansion of a rational number is either terminating or non-terminating ___.", "options": ["recurring", "terminating", "irrational", "integer"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "recurring. Explanation: By definition, the decimal expansion of rational numbers is either terminating or non-terminating recurring (repeating), whereas irrational numbers have non-terminating non-recurring decimal expansions.", "rubric_keywords": ["non-terminating recurring", "rational number", "decimal expansion"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{6}} = \\sqrt{7} + k\\), then the value of \\(k\\) is:", "options": ["\\(\\sqrt{6}\\)", "-\\(\\sqrt{6}\\)", "6", "7"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A: Rationalize the denominator of \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{6}}\\) by multiplying numerator and denominator by its conjugate \\((\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6})\\): \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}}{7 - 6} = \\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{6}\\). Comparing with \\(\\sqrt{7} + k\\), we get \\(k = \\sqrt{6}\\).", "rubric_keywords": ["rationalizing denominator", "conjugate", "square root"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express \\(0.47\\bar{9}\\) in the form \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\), where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are integers and \\(q \\neq 0\\).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let \\(x = 0.47999... = 0.47\\bar{9}\\) ... (1)\nStep 2: Multiply both sides by 10 since one digit is non-repeating before repetition: \\(10x = 4.7999...\\) ... (2)\nStep 3: Multiply equation (2) by 10 to shift past the repeating digit: \\(100x = 47.999...\\) ... (3)\nStep 4: Subtract equation (2) from equation (3): \\(90x = 47.999... - 4.7999... = 43.2 = \\frac{432}{10}\\) \\(\\Rightarrow x = \\frac{432}{900} = \\frac{12}{25}\\). Final Answer: \\(\\frac{12}{25}\\).", "rubric_keywords": ["recurring decimal", "p/q form", "equations", "subtraction"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Simplify the following expression: \\((3 + \\sqrt{3})(2 + \\sqrt{2})\\)", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Use the distributive property to expand the product: \\(3(2 + \\sqrt{2}) + \\sqrt{3}(2 + \\sqrt{2})\\).\nStep 2: Multiply each term: \\(= 6 + 3\\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{6}\\).\nStep 3: Since all terms are unlike surds, they cannot be combined further. Final Answer: \\(6 + 3\\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{6}\\).", "rubric_keywords": ["distributive property", "surds", "simplification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Evaluate: \\((64)^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\times (125)^{-\\frac{1}{3}} \\div (32)^{\\frac{1}{5}}\\)", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Express each base in prime exponential form: \\(64 = 2^6\\), \\(125 = 5^3\\), \\(32 = 2^5\\).\nStep 2: Substitute into the expression: \\((2^6)^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\times (5^3)^{-\\frac{1}{3}} \\div (2^5)^{\\frac{1}{5}}\\).\nStep 3: Apply laws of exponents \\((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\\): \\(= 2^3 \\times 5^{-1} \\div 2^1 = 8 \\times \\frac{1}{5} \\div 2\\).\nStep 4: Compute final value: \\(= \\frac{8}{5} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{4}{5}\\). Final Answer: \\(\\frac{4}{5}\\) or \\(0.8\\).", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "prime factorization", "powers"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The sum of a rational number and an irrational number is always an irrational number.\nReason (R): The set of real numbers is closed under addition.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: Both (A) and (R) are true statements. The sum of a rational and irrational number is always irrational, and real numbers are closed under addition, but statement (R) is a broad algebraic property of real numbers and does not directly explain why the specific sum of a rational and irrational number is irrational.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion and Reason", "rational and irrational", "closure property"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) if \\(\\frac{5 + 2\\sqrt{3}}{7 + 4\\sqrt{3}} = a + b\\sqrt{3}\\).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Rationalize the denominator of LHS by multiplying numerator and denominator by the conjugate \\((7 - 4\\sqrt{3})\\).\nStep 2: Numerator: \\((5 + 2\\sqrt{3})(7 - 4\\sqrt{3}) = 35 - 20\\sqrt{3} + 14\\sqrt{3} - 8(3) = 35 - 24 - 6\\sqrt{3} = 11 - 6\\sqrt{3}\\). Wait, recalculating: \\(35 - 24 = 11\\). Let's check: \\(35 - 8(3) = 35 - 24 = 11\\). Ah, let's verify: \\(5 \\times 7 = 35\\); \\(5 \\times (-4\\sqrt{3}) = -20\\sqrt{3}\\); \\(2\\sqrt{3} \\times 7 = 14\\sqrt{3}\\); \\(2\\sqrt{3} \\times (-4\\sqrt{3}) = -8 \\times 3 = -24\\). Total = \\(35 - 24 - 20\\sqrt{3} + 14\\sqrt{3} = 11 - 6\\sqrt{3}\\).\nStep 3: Denominator: \\(7^2 - (4\\sqrt{3})^2 = 49 - 48 = 1\\).\nStep 4: LHS becomes \\(11 - 6\\sqrt{3}\\).\nStep 5: Equate with \\(a + b\\sqrt{3}\\): \\(11 - 6\\sqrt{3} = a + b\\sqrt{3}\\).\nStep 6: Comparing coefficients, \\(a = 11\\) and \\(b = -6\\). Final Answer: \\(a = 11, b = -6\\).", "rubric_keywords": ["rationalizing denominator", "conjugate", "comparing coefficients", "surds"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case study and answer the questions below:\nStudents in a math lab were given rectangular plots of land for a project. Plot A has a length of \\((3 + \\sqrt{5})\\) meters and a breadth of \\((3 - \\sqrt{5})\\) meters.\n(i) Find the perimeter of Plot A.\n(ii) Find the area of Plot A and state whether it is a rational or irrational number.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Perimeter = \\(2 \\times (\\text{length} + \\text{breadth}) = 2 \\times ((3 + \\sqrt{5}) + (3 - \\sqrt{5})) = 2 \\times (6) = 12\\) meters.\nStep 2: Area = \\(\\text{length} \\times \\text{breadth} = (3 + \\sqrt{5})(3 - \\sqrt{5}) = 3^2 - (\\sqrt{5})^2 = 9 - 5 = 4\\) square meters.\nStep 3: Classification: The area is 4, which is a rational number.", "rubric_keywords": ["perimeter", "area", "algebraic identity", "rational number"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the laws of exponents in Column I with their simplified expressions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. \\(a^m \\cdot a^n\\)\n2. \\((a^m)^n\\)\n3. \\(\\frac{a^m}{a^n}\\)\n4. \\(a^m \\cdot b^m\\)\n\nColumn II:\nA. \\(a^{m-n}\\)\nB. \\((ab)^m\\)\nC. \\(a^{m+n}\\)\nD. \\(a^{mn}\\)", "options": ["1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Matching: 1-C (\\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\)), 2-D (\\((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\\)), 3-A (\\(\\frac{a^m}{a^n} = a^{m-n}\\)), 4-B (\\(a^m \\cdot b^m = (ab)^m\\)).", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "matching", "powers"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a rational number between 3 and 4?", "options": ["\\frac{7}{2}", "\\sqrt{10}", "\\sqrt{11}", "\\pi"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The correct option is \\frac{7}{2}. Explanation: \\frac{7}{2} = 3.5, which lies strictly between 3 and 4. \\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16, but \\frac{7}{2} is a terminating decimal rational number directly situated at the midpoint.", "rubric_keywords": ["rational number", "midpoint", "terminating decimal", "3.5"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The decimal expansion of a rational number is either terminating or non-terminating ___.", "options": ["recurring", "non-recurring", "terminating", "irrational"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "recurring. Explanation: By definition, rational numbers can be expressed as p/q where q \\neq 0. Their decimal expansion is always either terminating or non-terminating repeating (recurring).", "rubric_keywords": ["decimal expansion", "recurring", "rational number"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "What is the value of $(256)^{-\\frac{1}{4}}$?", "options": ["4", "\\frac{1}{4}", "16", "\\frac{1}{16}"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The correct option is \\frac{1}{4}. Explanation: We know that $256 = 4^4$. Therefore, $(256)^{-\\frac{1}{4}} = (4^4)^{-\\frac{1}{4}} = 4^{4 \\times (-\\frac{1}{4})} = 4^{-1} = \\frac{1}{4}$.", "rubric_keywords": ["exponent", "laws of exponents", "negative exponent", "1/4"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{2}} $.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is $(\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{2})$. Step 2: $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7} + \\sqrt{2}} \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{7} - \n\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^2 - (\\sqrt{2})^2}$. Step 3: Simplify the denominator: $7 - 2 = 5$. Final Answer: $\\frac{\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{2}}{5}$.", "rubric_keywords": ["rationalize", "conjugate", "denominator", "sqrt(7) - sqrt(2)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the expressions in Column I with their simplified values in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. $a^m \\cdot a^n$\n2. $(a^m)^n$\n3. $a^m / a^n$\nColumn II:\nA. $a^{m-n}$\nB. $a^{m+n}$\nC. $a^{mn}$\nD. $a^{m/n}$", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Match: 1-B ($a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}$), 2-C ($(a^m)^n = a^{mn}$), 3-A ($a^m / a^n = a^{m-n}$). Explanation: These are the standard fundamental laws of exponents for real numbers.", "rubric_keywords": ["laws of exponents", "matching", "product rule", "power rule", "quotient rule"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Every rational number is a real number.\nReason (R): The collection of all real numbers consists of rational and irrational numbers.\nOptions:\n(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true but R is false.\n(d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a)", "(b)", "(c)", "(d)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The correct option is (a). Explanation: Real numbers are made up of rational and irrational numbers. Therefore, every rational number is indeed a real number, and the reason correctly defines the composition of the real number system.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "real numbers", "rational numbers", "irrational numbers"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Express $0.35$ (with bar on 35, i.e., $0.\\overline{35}$) in the form $p/q$, where $p$ and $q$ are integers and $q \\neq 0$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let $x = 0.353535...$ --- (1). Step 2: Since there are two repeating digits, multiply both sides by $100$: $100x = 35.353535...$ --- (2). Step 3: Subtract equation (1) from equation (2): $100x - x = 35.3535... - 0.3535... \\implies 99x = 35$. Step 4: Solve for $x$: $x = \\frac{35}{99}$. Final Answer: $\\frac{35}{99}$.", "rubric_keywords": ["recurring decimal", "p/q form", "algebraic method", "35/99"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Evaluate: $64^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\times 125^{\\frac{1}{3}}$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Express 64 and 125 in exponential form: $64 = 8^2$ (or $2^6$) and $125 = 5^3$. Step 2: Substitute into the expression: $(8^2)^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\times (5^3)^{\\frac{1}{3}}$. Step 3: Simplify using laws of exponents: $8^{2 \\times \\frac{1}{2}} \\times 5^{3 \\times \\frac{1}{3}} = 8^1 \\times 5^1 = 8 \\times 5 = 40$. Final Answer: $40$.", "rubric_keywords": ["evaluate", "exponent", "64^1/2", "125^1/3", "40"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Visualize the representation of $3.765$ on the number line, through successive magnification, up to 4 decimal places.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Observe that $3.765$ lies between 3 and 4. Divide the segment between 3 and 4 into 10 equal parts and locate the interval [3.7, 3.8].\nStep 2: Magnify the interval [3.7, 3.8] into 10 equal parts ($3.71, 3.72, ..., 3.80$). $3.765$ lies between 3.76 and 3.77.\nStep 3: Magnify the interval [3.76, 3.77] into 10 equal parts ($3.761, 3.762, ..., 3.770$). $3.765$ lies between 3.765 and 3.766.\nStep 4: Pinpoint the exact location of $3.765$ on the third magnification step.\nExplanation includes step-by-step successive magnification details and visual plotting description.", "rubric_keywords": ["successive magnification", "number line", "visualization", "decimal representation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Number Systems", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions:\nIn a geometry lab, students are asked to construct square root spirals. A student marks point $O$ as the origin and draws a line segment $OA_1$ of unit length 1 cm. Then, a perpendicular line segment $A_1A_2$ of unit length 1 cm is drawn on $OA_1$, forming right-angled triangle $\\triangle OA_1A_2$.\n(i) What is the length of $OA_2$?\n(ii) If the process is continued by drawing $A_2A_3$ perpendicular to $OA_2$ of length 1 cm, find the length of $OA_3$.\n(iii) Generalize the length of $OA_n$ for any natural number $n$.\n(iv) State whether the length of $OA_5$ is a rational or irrational number.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Solutions:\n(i) By Pythagoras theorem, $OA_2 = \\sqrt{OA_1^2 + A_1A_2^2} = \\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{2}$ cm.\n(ii) In right triangle $\\triangle OA_2A_3$, $OA_3 = \\sqrt{OA_2^2 + A_2A_3^2} = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{2 + 1} = \\sqrt{3}$ cm.\n(iii) By induction/pattern, the length of $OA_n = \\sqrt{n}$.\n(iv) The length of $OA_5$ is $\\sqrt{5}$, which is an irrational number.", "rubric_keywords": ["square root spiral", "Pythagoras theorem", "irrational number", "sqrt(n)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the degree of the polynomial $5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2$?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "7"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C. The degree of a polynomial in one variable is the highest power of the variable in the polynomial. Here, the highest power of $x$ is 3.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree", "highest power", "3"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The zero of the linear polynomial $p(x) = 2x + 5$ is ___.", "options": ["-5/2", "5/2", "2/5", "-2/5"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To find the zero of the polynomial, set $p(x) = 0$, so $2x + 5 = 0 \\implies 2x = -5 \\implies x = -5/2$. Missing term: -5/2.", "rubric_keywords": ["zero", "linear polynomial", "-5/2"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If $x + 1$ is a factor of the polynomial $x^3 + x^2 + x + k$, then the value of $k$ is:", "options": ["0", "1", "-1", "2"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By Factor Theorem, if $x + 1$ is a factor, then $p(-1) = 0$. Substituting $x = -1$ into $p(x) = x^3 + x^2 + x + k$: $(-1)^3 + (-1)^2 + (-1) + k = 0 \\implies -1 + 1 - 1 + k = 0 \\implies -1 + k = 0 \\implies k = 1$. Hence, option B.", "rubric_keywords": ["Factor Theorem", "k = 1", "p(-1) = 0"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the value of the polynomial $5x - 4x^2 + 3$ at (i) $x = 0$ (ii) $x = -1$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let $p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3$. (i) At $x = 0$, $p(0) = 5(0) - 4(0)^2 + 3 = 3$. (ii) At $x = -1$, $p(-1) = 5(-1) - 4(-1)^2 + 3 = -5 - 4(1) + 3 = -5 - 4 + 3 = -6$.", "rubric_keywords": ["value of polynomial", "p(0) = 3", "p(-1) = -6"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the remainder when $x^3 - a^2x + 6x - a$ is divided by $x - a$ using the Remainder Theorem.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let $p(x) = x^3 - a^2x + 6x - a$. By the Remainder Theorem, when $p(x)$ is divided by $x - a$, the remainder is $p(a)$. Substituting $x = a$: $p(a) = (a)^3 - a^2(a) + 6(a) - a = a^3 - a^3 + 6a - a = 5a$. Thus, the remainder is $5a$.", "rubric_keywords": ["Remainder Theorem", "p(a)", "5a"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The degree of a non-zero constant polynomial is zero.\nReason (R): A constant polynomial contains no variables with non-zero exponents.\nChoose the correct option: (a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A constant polynomial like $p(x) = c$ ($c \\neq 0$) can be written as $c \\cdot x^0$, so its degree is 0. Both assertion and reason are true, and reason correctly explains why the degree is zero. Hence, option (a).", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "constant polynomial", "degree zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Factorise the cubic polynomial $x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120$ completely.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let $p(x) = x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120$. Factors of the constant term $-120$ include $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 5$, etc. Step 2: Check for $x = 1$: $p(1) = 1^3 - 23(1)^2 + 142(1) - 120 = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 143 - 143 = 0$. Since $p(1) = 0$, $(x - 1)$ is a factor. Step 3: Divide $p(x)$ by $(x - 1)$ to find the quadratic quotient: $x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120 = (x - 1)(x^2 - 22x + 120)$. Step 4: Factorise the quadratic $x^2 - 22x + 120$: find two numbers whose product is 120 and sum is $-22$, which are $-10$ and $-12$. So, $x^2 - 22x + 120 = (x - 10)(x - 12)$. Final Answer: $(x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12)$.", "rubric_keywords": ["cubic polynomial", "factor theorem", "x - 1", "quadratic factor", "(x-1)(x-10)(x-12)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the algebraic identities in Column I with their correct expansions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. $(x + y)^3$\n2. $(x - y)^3$\n3. $x^3 + y^3$\n4. $x^3 - y^3$\n\nColumn II:\nA. $(x - y)(x^2 + xy + y^2)$\nB. $x^3 + y^3 + 3xy(x + y)$\nC. $(x + y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)$\nD. $x^3 - y^3 - 3xy(x - y)$", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A", "1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1 matches with B: $(x+y)^3 = x^3 + y^3 + 3xy(x+y)$. 2 matches with D: $(x-y)^3 = x^3 - y^3 - 3xy(x-y)$. 3 matches with C: $x^3 + y^3 = (x+y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)$. 4 matches with A: $x^3 - y^3 = (x-y)(x^2 + xy + y^2)$. Correct choice is 1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A.", "rubric_keywords": ["identities", "cube formulas", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write the coefficient of $x^2$ in each of the following polynomials: (i) $2 + x^2 + x$ (ii) $\\frac{\\pi}{2}x^2 + x$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) In $2 + x^2 + x$, the term containing $x^2$ is $1 \\cdot x^2$, so the coefficient of $x^2$ is $1$. (ii) In $\\frac{\\pi}{2}x^2 + x$, the term containing $x^2$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}x^2$, so the coefficient of $x^2$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.", "rubric_keywords": ["coefficient", "x^2", "1", "pi/2"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions below:\nA school gardener wants to fence a rectangular garden plot whose area is given by the polynomial $p(x) = 2x^2 + 9x + 10$, where $x$ represents the width in meters.\n(i) Factorise the polynomial $p(x)$ to find linear expressions representing the length and width of the garden.\n(ii) If the width $x = 4$ meters, find the actual area and perimeter of the garden.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) To factorise $2x^2 + 9x + 10$, split the middle term: $2x^2 + 5x + 4x + 10 = x(2x + 5) + 2(2x + 5) = (2x + 5)(x + 2)$. Thus, dimensions are $(2x + 5)$ and $(x + 2)$. (ii) Given width $x = 4$ m. Length = $2(4) + 5 = 13$ m, Width = $4 + 2 = 6$ m. Area = $13 \\times 6 = 78\\text{ m}^2$. Perimeter = $2(13 + 6) = 2(19) = 38\\text{ m}$.", "rubric_keywords": ["case-based", "factorisation", "area", "perimeter"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the degree of a zero polynomial?", "options": ["0", "1", "Not defined", "Any natural number"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The degree of a non-zero constant polynomial is zero, but the degree of the zero polynomial is not defined.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree", "zero polynomial", "not defined"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the zero of the polynomial p(x) = 2x + 5.", "options": ["5/2", "-5/2", "2/5", "-2/5"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To find the zero of p(x), set p(x) = 0. Therefore, 2x + 5 = 0 => 2x = -5 => x = -5/2.", "rubric_keywords": ["zero of polynomial", "linear equation", "-5/2"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Check whether x + 2 is a factor of x^3 + 3x^2 + 5x + 6.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let p(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 5x + 6 and g(x) = x + 2. The zero of g(x) is -2. By the Factor Theorem, x + 2 is a factor of p(x) if p(-2) = 0. Evaluating p(-2): (-2)^3 + 3(-2)^2 + 5(-2) + 6 = -8 + 3(4) - 10 + 6 = -8 + 12 - 10 + 6 = 0. Since p(-2) = 0, x + 2 is a factor of p(x).", "rubric_keywords": ["Factor Theorem", "remainder zero", "p(-2)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Evaluate the product 104 × 96 without direct multiplication, using suitable algebraic identities.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Express 104 and 96 as (100 + 4) and (100 - 4) respectively. Using the identity (x + y)(x - y) = x^2 - y^2, we get (100 + 4)(100 - 4) = 100^2 - 4^2 = 10000 - 16 = 9984.", "rubric_keywords": ["algebraic identity", "difference of squares", "9984"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Factorise the cubic polynomial: x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Find factors of the constant term -120, which are ±1, ±2, ±3, etc. By trial, let p(x) = x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120. For x = 1, p(1) = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 0. Thus, (x - 1) is a factor. Step 2: Divide p(x) by (x - 1) or use synthetic division to get the quadratic quotient: x^2 - 22x + 120. Step 3: Factorise the quadratic expression x^2 - 22x + 120 by splitting the middle term: x^2 - 12x - 10x + 120 = x(x - 12) - 10(x - 12) = (x - 10)(x - 12). Step 4: Combine all factors: (x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12).", "rubric_keywords": ["cubic polynomial", "factor theorem", "splitting middle term", "(x-1)(x-10)(x-12)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the value of k if x - 1 is a factor of the polynomial p(x) = 4x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + k.", "options": [], "variables": {"coef1": 4, "coef2": 3, "coef3": -4}, "answer_key": "Since x - 1 is a factor of p(x), by the Factor Theorem, p(1) must be equal to 0. Substitute x = 1 into p(x): 4(1)^3 + 3(1)^2 - 4(1) + k = 0 => 4 + 3 - 4 + k = 0 => 3 + k = 0 => k = -3.", "rubric_keywords": ["Factor Theorem", "p(1) = 0", "k = -3"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A polynomial of degree 3 is called a ___ polynomial.", "options": ["Linear", "Quadratic", "Cubic", "Constant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Cubic. Explanation: Polynomials of degree 1, 2, and 3 are called linear, quadratic, and cubic polynomials respectively.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree 3", "cubic"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the polynomials in Column I with their respective degrees in Column II:\nColumn I: 1. 5x^3 + 4x^2 + x, 2. 7x + √3, 3. 4, 4. 2x^2 - 7\nColumn II: A. Degree 0, B. Degree 1, C. Degree 2, D. Degree 3", "options": ["1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-D: 5x^3 + 4x^2 + x has highest power 3 (Degree 3). 2-B: 7x + √3 has highest power 1 (Degree 1). 3-A: 4 is a constant polynomial with no variable, meaning 4x^0 (Degree 0). 4-C: 2x^2 - 7 has highest power 2 (Degree 2). Correct option is 1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree of polynomial", "highest power", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The degree of the polynomial (x-1)(x-2)(x-3) is 3.\nReason (R): The degree of the product of two polynomials is equal to the product of their degrees.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(A) is true because expanding (x-1)(x-2)(x-3) yields x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6, which has degree 3. (R) is false because the degree of the product of two polynomials is equal to the sum of their degrees, not their product. Thus, (A) is true but (R) is false.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion Reason", "degree of product", "sum of degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A school management committee wants to design a rectangular playground whose area is represented by the polynomial p(x) = 25x^2 - 35x + 12. \n(i) What are the possible expressions for the length and breadth of the playground?\n(ii) If x = 2 meters, what will be the numerical area of the playground?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) To find the length and breadth, we factorise the quadratic polynomial p(x) = 25x^2 - 35x + 12 by splitting the middle term. Product of coefficients = 25 × 12 = 300. Two numbers whose product is 300 and sum is -35 are -20 and -15. \n25x^2 - 20x - 15x + 12 = 5x(5x - 4) - 3(5x - 4) = (5x - 3)(5x - 4). \nThus, the possible expressions for length and breadth are (5x - 3) metres and (5x - 4) metres (or vice versa). \n(ii) For x = 2, Area = p(2) = 25(2)^2 - 35(2) + 12 = 25(4) - 70 + 12 = 100 - 70 + 12 = 42 square metres.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "splitting middle term", "area polynomial", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the degree of the polynomial 5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "0"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The degree of a polynomial in one variable is the highest power of the variable in the polynomial. In 5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2, the highest power of x is 3. Hence, the degree is 3. Correct Option: Option C.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree", "highest power", "polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the zero of the polynomial p(x) = 3x - 6.", "options": ["3", "-3", "2", "-2"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To find the zero of the polynomial, set p(x) = 0. 3x - 6 = 0 => 3x = 6 => x = 2. Correct Option: Option C.", "rubric_keywords": ["zero of polynomial", "linear equation", "root"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The degree of a non-zero constant polynomial is ___.", "options": ["0", "1", "Not defined", "Infinity"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Exact missing word: 0. Explanation: A non-zero constant polynomial can be expressed as p(x) = c = c * x^0, where c is a non-zero real number. Thus, its degree is 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["constant polynomial", "degree", "zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the value of the polynomial p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3 at x = 2.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Substitute x = 2 in p(x): p(2) = 5(2) - 4(2)^2 + 3 = 10 - 4(4) + 3 = 10 - 16 + 3 = -3. Final Answer: -3.", "rubric_keywords": ["value of polynomial", "substitution", "evaluation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Verify whether x + 1 is a factor of p(x) = x^3 + x^2 + x + 1 using the Factor Theorem.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "According to the Factor Theorem, x + 1 is a factor of p(x) if p(-1) = 0. Substituting x = -1: p(-1) = (-1)^3 + (-1)^2 + (-1) + 1 = -1 + 1 - 1 + 1 = 0. Since p(-1) = 0, x + 1 is indeed a factor of p(x).", "rubric_keywords": ["factor theorem", "remainder", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the remainder when p(x) = x^3 - ax^2 + 6x - a is divided by x - a using the Remainder Theorem.", "options": [], "variables": {"a": "constant"}, "answer_key": "By the Remainder Theorem, when p(x) is divided by x - a, the remainder is given by p(a). Substituting x = a: p(a) = (a)^3 - a(a)^2 + 6(a) - a = a^3 - a^3 + 6a - a = 5a. Final Answer: 5a.", "rubric_keywords": ["remainder theorem", "polynomial division", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Factorise the cubic polynomial: x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let p(x) = x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120. Factors of the constant term -120 include ±1, ±2, ±3, etc. For x = 1, p(1) = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 0. Therefore, (x - 1) is a factor.\nStep 2: Dividing p(x) by (x - 1) gives the quadratic quotient: x^2 - 22x + 120.\nStep 3: Factorise the quadratic expression by splitting the middle term: x^2 - 12x - 10x + 120 = x(x - 12) - 10(x - 12) = (x - 10)(x - 12).\nStep 4: Combine all linear factors: (x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12).", "rubric_keywords": ["cubic polynomial", "factorisation", "factor theorem", "splitting middle term"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The degree of the zero polynomial is zero. Reason (R): A polynomial having degree 2 is called a quadratic polynomial. Options: (a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Assertion (A) is false because the degree of the zero polynomial is not defined. Reason (R) is true because a polynomial of degree 2 is indeed called a quadratic polynomial. Therefore, A is false but R is true. Correct Option: Option (d).", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "zero polynomial", "quadratic polynomial", "degree"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the polynomials in Column I with their degree classification in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. 7x - 3\n2. y^2 - 5y + 6\n3. 5x^3 + 4x^2 + x\n4. 15\n\nColumn II:\nA. Constant polynomial (Degree 0)\nB. Linear polynomial (Degree 1)\nC. Quadratic polynomial (Degree 2)\nD. Cubic polynomial (Degree 3)", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1. 7x - 3 has degree 1 -> Linear polynomial (B).\n2. y^2 - 5y + 6 has degree 2 -> Quadratic polynomial (C).\n3. 5x^3 + 4x^2 + x has degree 3 -> Cubic polynomial (D).\n4. 15 has degree 0 -> Constant polynomial (A).\nCorrect match: 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A. Correct Option: Option 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["match the following", "degree classification", "linear", "quadratic", "cubic", "constant"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions below:\nA rectangular garden has an area represented by the polynomial p(x) = 3x^2 + 11x + 10 square meters, where x > 0.\n(i) Find the dimensions (length and breadth) of the garden by factorising the polynomial area expression.\n(ii) If x = 2 meters, calculate the exact numerical area and perimeter of the garden.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Factorise 3x^2 + 11x + 10 by splitting the middle term: 3x^2 + 6x + 5x + 10 = 3x(x + 2) + 5(x + 2) = (3x + 5)(x + 2). Thus, the dimensions can be taken as (3x + 5) m and (x + 2) m.\n(ii) For x = 2: Length = 3(2) + 5 = 11 m, Breadth = 2 + 2 = 4 m. Area = 11 * 4 = 44 square meters. Perimeter = 2(Length + Breadth) = 2(11 + 4) = 30 meters.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "factorisation", "area", "perimeter", "splitting middle term"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a polynomial in one variable?", "options": ["3x^2 - 2\\sqrt{x} + 5", "y^2 + \\sqrt{2}", "x - 1/x", "x^5 + y^5 + 1"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: y^2 + \\sqrt{2} is a polynomial in one variable y with non-negative integer exponents. Option A has fractional exponent \\sqrt{x}, Option C has negative exponent x^{-1}, and Option D has two variables.", "rubric_keywords": ["polynomial in one variable", "non-negative integer exponent", "degree"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The degree of a non-zero constant polynomial is ___.", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "Undefined"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "0. Explanation: A non-zero constant polynomial can be written as p(x) = c = c * x^0, where c != 0, hence its degree is 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree", "constant polynomial", "zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the value of the polynomial p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3 at (i) x = 0 and (ii) x = -1.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3. (i) At x = 0: p(0) = 5(0) - 4(0)^2 + 3 = 3. (ii) At x = -1: p(-1) = 5(-1) - 4(-1)^2 + 3 = -5 - 4(1) + 3 = -5 - 4 + 3 = -6.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "evaluation", "polynomial value"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the remainder when x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 2 is divided by x - 1.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the Remainder Theorem, when a polynomial p(x) is divided by (x - a), the remainder is p(a). Here, divisor is x - 1, so a = 1. Let p(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 2. p(1) = (1)^3 - 3(1)^2 + 4(1) - 2 = 1 - 3 + 4 - 2 = 0. Thus, the remainder is 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["Remainder Theorem", "substitution", "remainder is zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Factorize the quadratic polynomial: 6x^2 + 5x - 6.", "options": [], "variables": {"a": 6, "b": 5, "c": -6}, "answer_key": "To factorize 6x^2 + 5x - 6 by splitting the middle term, we look for two numbers whose product is 6 * (-6) = -36 and whose sum is 5. These numbers are 9 and -4. Splitting the middle term: 6x^2 + 9x - 4x - 6 = 3x(2x + 3) - 2(2x + 3) = (3x - 2)(2x + 3).", "rubric_keywords": ["splitting the middle term", "factors", "quadratic polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the algebraic identities in Column I with their correct expansions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. (x + y)^3\n2. (x - y)^3\n3. x^3 + y^3\n\nColumn II:\nA. (x + y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)\nB. x^3 + y^3 + 3xy(x + y)\nC. x^3 - y^3 - 3xy(x - y)", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B", "1-B, 2-A, 3-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct matching is: 1-B, 2-C, 3-A. (1) (x+y)^3 = x^3 + y^3 + 3xy(x+y); (2) (x-y)^3 = x^3 - y^3 - 3xy(x-y); (3) x^3 + y^3 = (x+y)(x^2 - xy + y^2).", "rubric_keywords": ["algebraic identities", "cube identities", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The polynomial p(x) = x^2 + 1 has no real zeros.\nReason (R): A polynomial of degree n can have at most n real zeros.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A. For p(x) = x^2 + 1, x^2 + 1 = 0 => x^2 = -1, which has no real solution, so A is true. R is a true general theorem about polynomials, but it does not directly explain why x^2 + 1 specifically lacks real zeros.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion-Reason", "real zeros", "degree of polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Factorize the cubic polynomial: x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Find a factor by trial. Factors of constant term -120 are \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, etc. Let p(x) = x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120. For x = 1, p(1) = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 0. So (x - 1) is a factor. Step 2: Divide p(x) by (x - 1) or use synthetic division/coefficient balancing to get the quotient quadratic: x^2 - 22x + 120. Step 3: Factorize the quadratic x^2 - 22x + 120 = (x - 10)(x - 12). Thus, the complete factorization is (x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12).", "rubric_keywords": ["cubic polynomial", "Factor Theorem", "trial method", "factorization"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case study and answer the questions:\nA school wants to fence a rectangular garden whose area is represented by the polynomial p(x) = 4x^2 + 12x + 5.\n(i) Express the area as a product of two linear binomials representing its possible length and breadth.\n(ii) If x = 5 meters, find the numerical perimeter of the garden.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Area = 4x^2 + 12x + 5 = 4x^2 + 10x + 2x + 5 = 2x(2x + 5) + 1(2x + 5) = (2x + 1)(2x + 5). Thus, possible Length = 2x + 5 and Breadth = 2x + 1. (ii) For x = 5 m: Length = 2(5) + 5 = 15 m, Breadth = 2(5) + 1 = 11 m. Perimeter = 2(Length + Breadth) = 2(15 + 11) = 2(26) = 52 meters.", "rubric_keywords": ["case study", "area of rectangle", "length and breadth", "perimeter"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Verify that x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - 3xyz = (x + y + z)(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - xy - yz - zx) and hence evaluate 27x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - 9xyz when x = 1, y = 2, z = 4.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Start with RHS: (x + y + z)(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - xy - yz - zx) = x(x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx) + y(...) + z(...) = x^3 + xy^2 + xz^2 - x^2y - xyz - x^2z + yx^2 + y^3 + yz^2 - xy^2 - y^2z - xyz + zx^2 + zy^2 + z^3 - xyz - yz^2 - z^2x = x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - 3xyz = LHS. Step 2: For 27x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - 9xyz, rewrite as (3x)^3 + (y)^3 + (z)^3 - 3(3x)(y)(z). Using the identity with a=3x, b=y, c=z: (3x + y + z)((3x)^2 + y^2 + z^2 - (3x)(y) - (y)(z) - (z)(3x)). Substituting x = 1, y = 2, z = 4: (3(1) + 2 + 4)((3)^2 + 2^2 + 4^2 - 3(1)(2) - (2)(4) - (4)(3(1))) = (3 + 2 + 4)(9 + 4 + 16 - 6 - 8 - 12) = (9)(29 - 26) = (9)(3) = 27.", "rubric_keywords": ["algebraic identity", "cube identity", "verification", "evaluation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the degree of the polynomial $5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2$?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The degree of a polynomial in one variable is the highest power of the variable in the polynomial. In $5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2$, the highest power of $x$ is 3. Hence, the degree is 3. Correct Option: Option C.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree", "highest power", "polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the zero of the polynomial $p(x) = 3x - 6$.", "options": ["2", "-2", "3", "-3"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To find the zero of a polynomial $p(x)$, we set $p(x) = 0$. So, $3x - 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow 3x = 6 \\Rightarrow x = 2$. Correct Option: Option A.", "rubric_keywords": ["zero of polynomial", "equation", "solve for x"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A polynomial of degree 2 is called a ___ polynomial.", "options": ["linear", "quadratic", "cubic", "constant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A polynomial of degree 2 is called a quadratic polynomial. Missing term: quadratic.", "rubric_keywords": ["quadratic", "degree 2", "polynomial classification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the value of the polynomial $p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3$ at $x = -1$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given $p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3$. Substitute $x = -1$: $p(-1) = 5(-1) - 4(-1)^2 + 3 = -5 - 4(1) + 3 = -5 - 4 + 3 = -9 + 3 = -6$. Final Answer: -6.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "value of polynomial", "simplification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Check whether $g(x) = x + 2$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 5x + 6$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the Factor Theorem, $g(x) = x + 2$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if $p(-2) = 0$. Let's evaluate $p(-2)$: $p(-2) = (-2)^3 + 3(-2)^2 + 5(-2) + 6 = -8 + 3(4) - 10 + 6 = -8 + 12 - 10 + 6 = 4 - 10 + 6 = 0$. Since $p(-2) = 0$, $x + 2$ is a factor of $p(x)$.", "rubric_keywords": ["factor theorem", "remainder", "zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the polynomials in Column I with their types in Column II:\\nColumn I: 1. $7x$, 2. $x^2 + 2x + 1$, 3. $5$, 4. $2x^3 - x$\\nColumn II: A. Constant polynomial, B. Linear polynomial, C. Quadratic polynomial, D. Cubic polynomial", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1. $7x$ has degree 1, so it is a Linear polynomial (B). 2. $x^2 + 2x + 1$ has degree 2, so it is a Quadratic polynomial (C). 3. $5$ has degree 0, so it is a Constant polynomial (A). 4. $2x^3 - x$ has degree 3, so it is a Cubic polynomial (D). Correct match: 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "degree", "polynomial type", "linear", "quadratic", "cubic", "constant"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the value of $k$ if $x - 1$ is a factor of $p(x) = 4x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x + k$.", "options": [], "variables": {"coefficient_x3": 4, "coefficient_x2": 3, "coefficient_x": -4}, "answer_key": "Since $x - 1$ is a factor of $p(x)$, by the Factor Theorem, $p(1) = 0$. Substitute $x = 1$ into $p(x)$: $4(1)^3 + 3(1)^2 - 4(1) + k = 0 \\Rightarrow 4(1) + 3(1) - 4 + k = 0 \\Rightarrow 4 + 3 - 4 + k = 0 \\Rightarrow 3 + k = 0 \\Rightarrow k = -3$. Final Answer: $k = -3$.", "rubric_keywords": ["factor theorem", "find k", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The degree of a non-zero constant polynomial is zero.\\nReason (R): A constant polynomial contains no variables with positive exponents.\\nChoose the correct option:\\n(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.\\n(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.\\n(c) A is true but R is false.\\n(d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a)", "(b)", "(c)", "(d)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A non-zero constant polynomial like $5$ can be written as $5x^0$, so its degree is 0. Thus, Assertion (A) is true. Reason (R) correctly explains that a constant polynomial has no variable terms with positive exponents, which leads to a degree of zero. Correct Option: (a).", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "constant polynomial", "degree zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Factorize the cubic polynomial: $x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let $p(x) = x^3 - 23x^2 + 142x - 120$. The factors of the constant term $-120$ include $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 4, \\pm 5, \\dots$. By trial, let's check $p(1)$: $p(1) = (1)^3 - 23(1)^2 + 142(1) - 120 = 1 - 23 + 142 - 120 = 143 - 143 = 0$. Since $p(1) = 0$, $(x - 1)$ is a factor. Now divide $p(x)$ by $(x - 1)$ to get the quadratic factor: $x^2 - 22x + 120$. Next, factorize the quadratic $x^2 - 22x + 120$: find two numbers whose product is 120 and sum is $-22$, which are $-12$ and $-10$. So, $x^2 - 22x + 120 = (x - 10)(x - 12)$. Thus, the complete factorization is $(x - 1)(x - 10)(x - 12)$.", "rubric_keywords": ["factorize cubic polynomial", "factor theorem", "quadratic factorization", "trial method"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\\nA rectangular garden has an area represented by the polynomial $p(x) = 2x^2 + 9x + 10$ square meters, where $x > 0$.\\n(i) Express the length and breadth of the garden as linear polynomials in terms of $x$.\\n(ii) If $x = 3$ meters, find the actual perimeter of the garden.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) To find length and breadth, factorize the quadratic polynomial representing the area: $p(x) = 2x^2 + 9x + 10$. Split the middle term $9x$ into $4x + 5x$ (since $2 \\times 10 = 20$ and $4 \\times 5 = 20, 4 + 5 = 9$). $2x^2 + 4x + 5x + 10 = 2x(x + 2) + 5(x + 2) = (2x + 5)(x + 2)$. Thus, the length and breadth are $(2x + 5)$ and $(x + 2)$ meters.\\n(ii) For $x = 3$: Length = $2(3) + 5 = 6 + 5 = 11$ m; Breadth = $3 + 2 = 5$ m. Perimeter = $2(\\text{Length} + \\text{Breadth}) = 2(11 + 5) = 2(16) = 32$ meters.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "area of rectangle", "factorization", "perimeter"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the degree of the polynomial p(x) = 5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The degree of a polynomial in one variable is the highest power of the variable in the polynomial. Here, the highest power of x is 3. Hence, the degree is 3. Correct option is Option C.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree", "highest power", "polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the value of the polynomial p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3 at x = 2.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3. Substitute x = 2: p(2) = 5(2) - 4(2)^2 + 3 = 10 - 4(4) + 3 = 10 - 16 + 3 = -3. Final Answer: -3.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "value of polynomial", "simplification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The degree of a non-zero constant polynomial is ___.", "options": ["zero", "one", "two", "undefined"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Exact missing word: zero. Explanation: A non-zero constant polynomial like p(x) = 5 can be written as 5x^0, making its degree zero.", "rubric_keywords": ["constant polynomial", "degree", "zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the polynomials in Column I with their degree/type in Column II:\nColumn I: 1. x + 1, 2. x^2 - 3x + 2, 3. 7, 4. x^3 - x\nColumn II: A. Quadratic polynomial, B. Linear polynomial, C. Constant polynomial (Degree 0), D. Cubic polynomial", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-D, 4-C", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1. x + 1 is a linear polynomial (degree 1) -> B; 2. x^2 - 3x + 2 is a quadratic polynomial (degree 2) -> A; 3. 7 is a constant polynomial (degree 0) -> C; 4. x^3 - x is a cubic polynomial (degree 3) -> D. Correct match code is 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear", "quadratic", "cubic", "constant", "degree"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): p(x) = (1/x) - x + 2 is a polynomial in one variable x.\nReason (R): The exponents of the variable in a polynomial must be non-negative integers.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Assertion A is false because 1/x = x^(-1), which has a negative exponent. Reason R is true because polynomial exponents must be whole numbers. Therefore, A is false and R is true (Option D).", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "polynomial definition", "non-negative integer exponents"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Verify whether 2 and 0 are zeroes of the polynomial p(x) = x^2 - 2x using the definition of a zero of a polynomial.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To check if x = 2 is a zero, substitute x = 2 in p(x): p(2) = (2)^2 - 2(2) = 4 - 4 = 0. Since p(2) = 0, 2 is a zero of p(x). To check if x = 0 is a zero, substitute x = 0 in p(x): p(0) = (0)^2 - 2(0) = 0 - 0 = 0. Since p(0) = 0, 0 is also a zero of p(x). Thus, both 2 and 0 are zeroes of p(x).", "rubric_keywords": ["zeroes of polynomial", "substitution", "verification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Factorize the quadratic polynomial 6x^2 + 5x - 6 completely.", "options": [], "variables": {"a": 6, "b": 5, "c": -6}, "answer_key": "Using splitting the middle term method: Product of coefficient of x^2 and constant term = 6 * (-6) = -36. Middle term coefficient is 5. We need two numbers whose product is -36 and sum is 5. These numbers are 9 and -4. Rewrite middle term: 6x^2 + 9x - 4x - 6. Grouping terms: 3x(2x + 3) - 2(2x + 3) = (3x - 2)(2x + 3). Final Answer: (3x - 2)(2x + 3).", "rubric_keywords": ["factorization", "splitting the middle term", "quadratic polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the zero of the linear polynomial p(x) = 2x + 5.", "options": ["5/2", "-5/2", "2/5", "-2/5"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To find the zero of p(x), set p(x) = 0. So, 2x + 5 = 0 => 2x = -5 => x = -5/2. Correct option is Option B.", "rubric_keywords": ["zero of linear polynomial", "equation solving"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the remainder when x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 1 is divided by x - 1 using the Remainder Theorem.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let p(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 1. By the Remainder Theorem, when p(x) is divided by x - 1, the remainder is p(1). Substitute x = 1: p(1) = (1)^3 - 3(1)^2 + 4(1) - 1 = 1 - 3 + 4 - 1 = 1. Final Answer: Remainder is 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["remainder theorem", "polynomial division", "remainder"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nA rectangular garden has an area represented by the polynomial p(x) = x^2 + 7x + 12 square meters.\n(i) Express the length and breadth of the garden as linear polynomials in terms of x by factorizing p(x).\n(ii) If x = 5 meters, find the actual numerical area of the garden.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Factorize x^2 + 7x + 12: Find two numbers whose product is 12 and sum is 7 (4 and 3). So, x^2 + 4x + 3x + 12 = x(x+4) + 3(x+4) = (x+4)(x+3). Thus, length and breadth can be represented as (x+4) and (x+3) meters.\n(ii) Substitute x = 5 into p(x): p(5) = (5)^2 + 7(5) + 12 = 25 + 35 + 12 = 72 square meters.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "factorization", "area of rectangle", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the degree of the polynomial $p(x) = 5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2$?", "options": ["A) 1", "B) 2", "C) 3", "D) 4"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The degree of a polynomial in one variable is the highest power of the variable in the polynomial. In $p(x) = 5x^3 - 4x^2 + 7x - 2$, the highest power of $x$ is 3. Hence, the degree is 3. Correct Option: C.", "rubric_keywords": ["degree", "highest power", "polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the value of the polynomial $p(x) = 5x - 4x^2 + 3$ at (i) $x = 0$ and (ii) $x = -1$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) For $x = 0$: $p(0) = 5(0) - 4(0)^2 + 3 = 0 - 0 + 3 = 3$. (ii) For $x = -1$: $p(-1) = 5(-1) - 4(-1)^2 + 3 = -5 - 4(1) + 3 = -5 - 4 + 3 = -6$. Final Answer: $p(0) = 3$ and $p(-1) = -6$.", "rubric_keywords": ["value of polynomial", "substitution", "evaluation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Check whether $g(x) = x + 2$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 5x + 6$ using the Factor Theorem.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "According to the Factor Theorem, $g(x) = x + 2$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if $p(-2) = 0$. Let's find $p(-2)$: $p(-2) = (-2)^3 + 3(-2)^2 + 5(-2) + 6 = -8 + 3(4) - 10 + 6 = -8 + 12 - 10 + 6 = 4 - 10 + 6 = 0$. Since $p(-2) = 0$, by the Factor Theorem, $x + 2$ is a factor of $p(x)$.", "rubric_keywords": ["Factor Theorem", "remainder", "zero of polynomial"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the zero of the polynomial $p(x) = 2x + 5$.", "options": [], "variables": {"a": 2, "b": 5}, "answer_key": "To find the zero of the polynomial $p(x)$, set $p(x) = 0$. So, $2x + 5 = 0 \\implies 2x = -5 \\implies x = -5/2$. The zero of the polynomial is $-5/2$.", "rubric_keywords": ["zero of polynomial", "linear equation", "root"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The degree of a non-zero constant polynomial is zero.\nReason (R): Any non-zero real number can be written as $a \\cdot x^0$, where $a \\neq 0$.\nChoose the correct option: (a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "(c) A is true but R is false", "(d) A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A non-zero constant polynomial like 5 can be written as $5x^0$. Since the highest power of $x$ is 0, its degree is 0. Both Assertion and Reason are true, and Reason correctly explains Assertion. Correct Option: (a).", "rubric_keywords": ["constant polynomial", "degree zero", "exponent"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A polynomial of degree three is called a ___ polynomial.", "options": ["linear", "quadratic", "cubic", "constant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Cubic. Explanation: A polynomial of degree 1 is linear, degree 2 is quadratic, and degree 3 is cubic.", "rubric_keywords": ["cubic polynomial", "degree three", "classification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the algebraic identities in Column I with their correct expansions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. $(x + y)^2$\n2. $(x - y)^2$\n3. $x^2 - y^2$\n4. $(x + a)(x + b)$\n\nColumn II:\nA. $x^2 - 2xy + y^2$\nB. $x^2 + (a+b)x + ab$\nC. $x^2 + 2xy + y^2$\nD. $(x - y)(x + y)$", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct matching: 1-(x+y)^2 = x^2+2xy+y^2 (C); 2-(x-y)^2 = x^2-2xy+y^2 (A); 3-x^2-y^2 = (x-y)(x+y) (D); 4-(x+a)(x+b) = x^2+(a+b)x+ab (B). Thus, 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B. Correct Option: 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B.", "rubric_keywords": ["algebraic identities", "expansion", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the remainder when $x^3 - ax^2 + 6x - a$ is divided by $x - a$.", "options": ["A) $5a$", "B) $6a$", "C) $a$", "D) $5$"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the Remainder Theorem, when $p(x) = x^3 - ax^2 + 6x - a$ is divided by $x - a$, the remainder is $p(a)$. Substituting $x = a$: $p(a) = a^3 - a(a)^2 + 6(a) - a = a^3 - a^3 + 6a - a = 5a$. Correct Option: A.", "rubric_keywords": ["Remainder Theorem", "remainder", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Factorise $12x^2 - 7x + 1$ by splitting the middle term.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "To factorise $12x^2 - 7x + 1$, we split the middle term $-7x$ into two parts whose sum is $-7$ and product is $12 \\times 1 = 12$. These numbers are $-3$ and $-4$. So, $12x^2 - 4x - 3x + 1 = 4x(3x - 1) - 1(3x - 1) = (4x - 1)(3x - 1)$. Final Answer: $(4x - 1)(3x - 1)$.", "rubric_keywords": ["factorisation", "splitting middle term", "quadratic"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Polynomials", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions below:\nA school science club wants to design a rectangular garden plot. The length of the garden is represented by polynomial $(3x + 2)$ metres and breadth by $(2x - 1)$ metres.\n(i) Express the area of the garden as a polynomial in $x$.\n(ii) Find the area of the garden if $x = 5$ metres.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Area = $\\text{length} \\times \\text{breadth} = (3x + 2)(2x - 1) = 3x(2x - 1) + 2(2x - 1) = 6x^2 - 3x + 4x - 2 = 6x^2 + x - 2$. (ii) For $x = 5$, Area = $6(5)^2 + 5 - 2 = 6(25) + 3 = 150 + 3 = 153\\text{ m}^2$.", "rubric_keywords": ["area of rectangle", "polynomial multiplication", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The abscissa of a point is its distance from the:", "options": ["Origin", "X-axis", "Y-axis", "None of these"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C. The abscissa of a point is the x-coordinate, which represents its perpendicular distance from the y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["abscissa", "y-axis", "perpendicular distance", "x-coordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In which quadrant does the point P(-3, 4) lie?", "options": ["Quadrant I", "Quadrant II", "Quadrant III", "Quadrant IV"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. A point with a negative x-coordinate and a positive y-coordinate (-, +) lies in the second quadrant (Quadrant II).", "rubric_keywords": ["quadrant II", "negative x", "positive y", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The point of intersection of the coordinate axes is called the ___.", "options": ["origin", "abscissa", "ordinate", "quadrant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "origin. The intersection point of the X-axis and Y-axis is the origin (0, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["origin", "intersection", "coordinate axes", "(0, 0)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Write the coordinates of a point on the x-axis at a distance of 5 units to the left of the origin.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since the point is on the x-axis, its y-coordinate is 0. Since it is 5 units to the left of the origin, its x-coordinate is -5. Therefore, the coordinates are (-5, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["x-axis", "left of origin", "-5", "y-coordinate zero", "(-5, 0)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If the ordinate of a point is 3 and it lies on the y-axis, what are the coordinates of the point?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Any point on the y-axis has its x-coordinate (abscissa) equal to 0. Given that the ordinate is 3, the coordinates of the point are (0, 3).", "rubric_keywords": ["y-axis", "abscissa zero", "ordinate", "(0, 3)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, -4) lies on the y-axis. Reason (R): Every point on the y-axis has its x-coordinate equal to zero. Choose the correct option: (a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A. The point (0, -4) has x-coordinate 0, which confirms it lies on the y-axis. Reason correctly defines the property of points on the y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "y-axis", "x-coordinate zero", "correct explanation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(2, 2), B(6, 2), C(6, 5), and D(2, 5) on a Cartesian plane. Join them in order to form a geometric figure. Name the figure and calculate its area.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1. Plotting points: A(2,2), B(6,2), C(6,5), and D(2,5). 2. Joining AB, BC, CD, DA forms a rectangle ABCD. 3. Length of side AB = 6 - 2 = 4 units. 4. Breadth of side BC = 5 - 2 = 3 units. 5. Area of rectangle = Length * Breadth = 4 * 3 = 12 square units.", "rubric_keywords": ["plot points", "rectangle", "length 4", "breadth 3", "area 12 square units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the points in Column I with their respective quadrants/axes in Column II:\nColumn I: 1. (2, -3), 2. (-2, 3), 3. (-4, -5), 4. (0, 5)\nColumn II: A. Quadrant II, B. Quadrant IV, C. Quadrant III, D. Y-axis", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B ((2, -3) has +x, -y -> Quadrant IV), 2-A ((-2, 3) has -x, +y -> Quadrant II), 3-C ((-4, -5) has -x, -y -> Quadrant III), 4-D ((0, 5) has x=0 -> Y-axis). Correct match code: 1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["Quadrant IV", "Quadrant II", "Quadrant III", "Y-axis", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the distance of the point P(3, 4) from the origin.", "options": [], "variables": {"x": 3, "y": 4}, "answer_key": "Using the distance formula from the origin d = sqrt(x^2 + y^2), we get d = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(9 + 16) = sqrt(25) = 5 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["distance from origin", "sqrt(x^2 + y^2)", "5 units", "Pythagorean triplet"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions:\nIn a school sports day, students are standing in a grid. Taking the principal's podium as the origin (0, 0), Student A is standing at position (3, 4) and Student B is at position (-2, 3).\n(i) In which quadrant is Student B standing?\n(ii) What is the abscissa of Student A?\n(iii) Find the straight-line distance between Student A and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Student B (-2, 3) is standing in Quadrant II because x is negative and y is positive. (ii) The abscissa of Student A (3, 4) is 3. (iii) The distance of Student A (3, 4) from the y-axis is its x-coordinate, which is 3 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["Quadrant II", "abscissa 3", "distance from y-axis", "case-based"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The point (-3, 5) lies in which of the following quadrants?", "options": ["Quadrant I", "Quadrant II", "Quadrant III", "Quadrant IV"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Quadrant II. Explanation: In the second quadrant, the x-coordinate is negative and the y-coordinate is positive. For (-3, 5), x = -3 (negative) and y = 5 (positive), hence it lies in Quadrant II.", "rubric_keywords": ["Quadrant II", "negative x-coordinate", "positive y-coordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the abscissa of any point lying on the y-axis?", "options": ["Positive number", "Negative number", "Zero", "Any real number"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Zero. Explanation: The y-axis is the vertical line where the distance from the y-axis is zero, meaning the x-coordinate (abscissa) of any point on the y-axis is always 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["abscissa", "y-axis", "zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The intersection of the horizontal and vertical lines determining the position of any point in a Cartesian plane is called the ___.", "options": ["Origin", "Quadrant", "Axis", "Coordinate"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Origin. Explanation: The point of intersection of the X-axis and Y-axis is called the origin, denoted by (0, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["Origin", "intersection", "Cartesian plane"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write the coordinates of a point whose abscissa is -4 and ordinate is 3. Also, state the quadrant in which this point lies.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Coordinates: (-4, 3). Quadrant: Second Quadrant (Quadrant II). Explanation: The abscissa gives the x-coordinate (-4) and the ordinate gives the y-coordinate (3). Since x is negative and y is positive, the point lies in Quadrant II.", "rubric_keywords": ["(-4, 3)", "Quadrant II", "abscissa", "ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(2, 2), B(-2, 2), C(-2, -2) and D(2, -2) on a Cartesian plane. Join AB, BC, CD and DA. Name the geometrical figure formed and find its area.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Figure formed: Square. Side length: Distance from (2,2) to (-2,2) is 4 units. Area = side × side = 4 × 4 = 16 square units. Explanation: All sides are equal to 4 units and interior angles are 90 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["Square", "4 units", "16 square units", "plotting points"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, 4) lies on the y-axis. Reason (R): The x-coordinate of any point on the y-axis is zero.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. Explanation: A point whose x-coordinate is zero lies on the y-axis. Since the x-coordinate of (0, 4) is 0, it lies on the y-axis, and R directly explains this property.", "rubric_keywords": ["Both A and R are true", "correct explanation", "x-coordinate is zero", "y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the points in Column I with their correct location in Column II:\nColumn I: 1. (3, -2), 2. (-4, -5), 3. (0, 7), 4. (-1, 6)\nColumn II: A. Quadrant III, B. Quadrant IV, C. Quadrant II, D. Positive y-axis", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C. Explanation: (3, -2) has +x, -y -> Quadrant IV (B); (-4, -5) has -x, -y -> Quadrant III (A); (0, 7) has x=0, y>0 -> Positive y-axis (D); (-1, 6) has -x, +y -> Quadrant II (C).", "rubric_keywords": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "Quadrant IV", "Quadrant III", "positive y-axis", "Quadrant II"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nIn a classroom, students are seated at desks arranged in a grid with the teacher's podium at the origin (0, 0). Student Ram is seated at position R(3, 2) and Shyam is seated at S(3, 5).\n(i) What is the distance between Ram and Shyam?\n(ii) If a fourth point P is added such that O(0,0), R(3,2), S(3,5), and P form a rectangle, find the coordinates of point P and calculate the area of rectangle ORSP.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Distance between R(3, 2) and S(3, 5) is 5 - 2 = 3 units (since x-coordinates are same).\n(ii) The points O(0,0), R(3,2), S(3,5) form a rectangle with the fourth point P(0, 3) or rather P(0, 5) completing the shape ORSP? Let's check vertices: O(0,0) to R(3,2) to S(3,5) and P(0,3). Length = 3 units, Width = 3 units. Area = 3 × 3 = 9 square units. Coordinates of P are (0, 3).", "rubric_keywords": ["3 units", "rectangle", "(0, 3)", "9 square units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the coordinates of a point on the x-axis which is equidistant from the points A(2, -5) and B(-2, 9).", "options": [], "variables": {"x1": 2, "y1": -5, "x2": -2, "y2": 9}, "answer_key": "Let the point on the x-axis be P(x, 0). Since it is equidistant from A(2, -5) and B(-2, 9), PA^2 = PB^2. (x - 2)^2 + (0 - (-5))^2 = (x - (-2))^2 + (0 - 9)^2. Expanding: x^2 - 4x + 4 + 25 = x^2 + 4x + 4 + 81 => -4x + 29 = 4x + 85 => 8x = -56 => x = -7. Thus, the point is (-7, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["(-7, 0)", "equidistant", "x-axis", "PA^2 = PB^2"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A triangular park has vertices at A(1, 4), B(3, 0), and C(7, 0).\n(i) Determine the base and height of the triangle ABC.\n(ii) Calculate the area of the triangular park.\n(iii) Find the total length of the perimeter of the triangle ABC using the distance formula.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Base BC lies on the x-axis from x = 3 to x = 7, so base length = 7 - 3 = 4 units. Height is the perpendicular distance from A(1, 4) to BC, which is the y-coordinate of A, i.e., 4 units.\n(ii) Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 4 × 4 = 8 square units.\n(iii) Length of AB = √((3-1)^2 + (0-4)^2) = √(4 + 16) = √20 = 2√5 units. Length of AC = √((7-1)^2 + (0-4)^2) = √(36 + 16) = √52 = 2√13 units. Length of BC = 4 units. Perimeter = 4 + 2√5 + 2√13 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["base 4 units", "height 4 units", "Area = 8 sq units", "Perimeter", "distance formula"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the name of the horizontal and the vertical lines drawn to determine the position of any point in the Cartesian plane?", "options": ["X-axis and Y-axis", "Abscissa and Ordinate", "Origin and Quadrant", "Vertical and Horizontal coordinates"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A: X-axis and Y-axis. The horizontal line is called the X-axis and the vertical line is called the Y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["horizontal line", "vertical line", "X-axis", "Y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The intersection point of the horizontal and vertical lines in a Cartesian plane is called the ___.", "options": ["Origin", "Quadrant", "Abscissa", "Ordinate"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Origin. Explanation: The point of intersection of the X-axis and Y-axis is called the origin, denoted by (0, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["Origin", "intersection", "X-axis", "Y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The perpendicular distance of a point P(3, 4) from the Y-axis is:", "options": ["3 units", "4 units", "5 units", "7 units"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A: 3 units. Explanation: The perpendicular distance of a point from the Y-axis is given by the x-coordinate (abscissa), which is 3.", "rubric_keywords": ["perpendicular distance", "Y-axis", "x-coordinate", "3 units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write the coordinates of the origin. Also, state the signs of the x and y coordinates of a point lying in the third quadrant.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Coordinates of the origin are (0, 0). In the third quadrant, both x and y coordinates are negative (- , -).", "rubric_keywords": ["origin", "(0, 0)", "third quadrant", "negative"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(2, 2), B(-2, 2), C(-2, -2) and D(2, -2) on a graph sheet. Name the geometric figure formed by joining these points in order.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The figure formed by joining the points A(2, 2), B(-2, 2), C(-2, -2), and D(2, -2) in order is a Square because all four sides are equal in length (4 units each) and all interior angles are 90 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["square", "equal sides", "90 degrees", "vertices"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the distance between the points P(0, 5) and Q(0, -3) located on the Y-axis using coordinate geometry principles.", "options": [], "variables": {"y1": 5, "y2": -3}, "answer_key": "The distance between two points on the Y-axis with coordinates (0, y1) and (0, y2) is given by |y1 - y2| = |5 - (-3)| = |5 + 3| = 8 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["distance", "Y-axis", "8 units", "absolute difference"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, -5) lies on the negative direction of the Y-axis.\nReason (R): The x-coordinate of every point on the Y-axis is zero.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A: Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). Explanation: Since the x-coordinate is 0, it lies on the Y-axis, and since the y-coordinate is negative (-5), it lies on the negative direction of the Y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "Y-axis", "x-coordinate zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Match the points in Column I with their respective quadrants or axes in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. (3, -2)\n2. (-4, -5)\n3. (0, 7)\n4. (-1, 6)\nColumn II:\nA. Y-axis\nB. Quadrant IV\nC. Quadrant II\nD. Quadrant III", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Match: 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C. Explanation: (3, -2) has +x, -y -> Quadrant IV; (-4, -5) has -x, -y -> Quadrant III; (0, 7) has x=0 -> Y-axis; (-1, 6) has -x, +y -> Quadrant II.", "rubric_keywords": ["Column I", "Column II", "Quadrant", "Axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A rectangle ABCD has vertices A(0, 0), B(4, 0), C(4, 3), and D(0, 3). (a) Find the length of its sides AB and BC. (b) Calculate the length of diagonal AC. (c) Find the area of rectangle ABCD.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(a) Length AB = 4 - 0 = 4 units; Width BC = 3 - 0 = 3 units. (b) Length of diagonal AC = sqrt(AB^2 + BC^2) = sqrt(4^2 + 3^2) = sqrt(16 + 9) = sqrt(25) = 5 units. (c) Area = length * breadth = 4 * 3 = 12 square units.", "rubric_keywords": ["rectangle", "vertices", "diagonal", "area", "5 units", "12 square units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case study and answer the questions:\nDuring a mathematics activity, students are asked to mark positions on a large grid on the floor. Four students stand at positions P(1, 2), Q(5, 2), R(5, 6), and S(1, 6).\n(i) What geometric shape is formed when points P, Q, R, and S are joined in order? (1 mark)\n(ii) Find the distance between points P and Q. (1 mark)\n(iii) Find the coordinates of the intersection point of the diagonals PR and QS. (2 marks)", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Square (since PQ = 5 - 1 = 4 units and QR = 6 - 2 = 4 units, all sides are equal and angles are 90 degrees).\n(ii) Distance PQ = 5 - 1 = 4 units.\n(iii) The midpoint formula gives the center of the square (intersection of diagonals). Midpoint of PR = ((1+5)/2, (2+6)/2) = (3, 4). Thus, coordinates are (3, 4).", "rubric_keywords": ["Square", "4 units", "midpoint", "(3, 4)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the name of the horizontal line and the vertical line drawn to determine the position of any point in the Cartesian plane?", "options": ["Origin and Axis", "X-axis and Y-axis", "Abscissa and Ordinate", "Quadrant and Axis"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The horizontal line is called the x-axis and the vertical line is called the y-axis. Therefore, option B is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["horizontal line", "vertical line", "x-axis", "y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In which quadrant do the points $(-3, 5)$ and $(2, -4)$ lie respectively?", "options": ["First and Second", "Second and Fourth", "Third and First", "Fourth and Third"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "For $(-3, 5)$, the x-coordinate is negative and the y-coordinate is positive, which places it in the Second Quadrant. For $(2, -4)$, the x-coordinate is positive and the y-coordinate is negative, which places it in the Fourth Quadrant. Hence, Second and Fourth.", "rubric_keywords": ["quadrant", "negative", "positive", "second", "fourth"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The point of intersection of the coordinate axes is called the ___.", "options": ["Origin", "Quadrant", "Abscissa", "Ordinate"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Origin. The point where the x-axis and y-axis intersect is known as the origin, and its coordinates are (0, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["origin", "intersection", "axes"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write the coordinates of the point that lies on both the axes. Also, state its specific name.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The point that lies on both the x-axis and y-axis is the origin. Its coordinates are (0, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["origin", "coordinates", "(0, 0)", "both axes"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The abscissa of a point on the y-axis is always zero.\nReason (R): The distance of a point from the y-axis is called its y-coordinate or ordinate.\nChoose the correct option: \n(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true but R is false.\n(d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "(c) A is true but R is false.", "(d) A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Assertion (A) is true because any point on the y-axis has an x-coordinate (abscissa) of 0. Reason (R) is false because the distance of a point from the y-axis is measured by its x-coordinate (abscissa), not the y-coordinate. Therefore, (c) A is true but R is false.", "rubric_keywords": ["abscissa", "y-axis", "zero", "x-coordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the points in Column I with their corresponding quadrants/axes in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. (4, -2)\n2. (-3, -5)\n3. (0, 7)\n4. (5, 3)\n\nColumn II:\nA. y-axis\nB. First Quadrant\nC. Fourth Quadrant\nD. Third Quadrant\n\nOptions:\n(a) 1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B\n(b) 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D\n(c) 1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A\n(d) 1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A", "options": ["(a) 1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "(b) 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "(c) 1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A", "(d) 1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(1, 4-2) has positive x and negative y, so it's in Fourth Quadrant (1-C). (-3, -5) has both negative, Third Quadrant (2-D). (0, 7) has x=0, so it's on the y-axis (3-A). (5, 3) has both positive, First Quadrant (4-B). Correct match is (a).", "rubric_keywords": ["quadrants", "match", "coordinates", "axes"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the perpendicular distance of the point P(4, 3) from the x-axis and the y-axis respectively.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The perpendicular distance of a point from the x-axis is given by the absolute value of its ordinate (y-coordinate), which is |3| = 3 units. The perpendicular distance from the y-axis is given by the absolute value of its abscissa (x-coordinate), which is |4| = 4 units. Thus, the distances are 3 units from the x-axis and 4 units from the y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["perpendicular distance", "x-axis", "y-axis", "3 units", "4 units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the source and answer the questions:\nDuring a mathematics activity, students arranged themselves on a Cartesian grid drawn on the school playground. Student A is standing at position P(2, 3), Student B is at Q(-2, 3), Student C is at R(-2, -3), and Student D is at S(2, -3).\n(i) Plot or identify the geometric figure formed by joining the points P, Q, R, and S in order.\n(ii) Find the length of the sides PQ and QR of the formed figure.\n(iii) Calculate the area of the polygon PQRS.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Joining P(2,3), Q(-2,3), R(-2,-3), and S(2,-3) forms a rectangle.\n(ii) Length of PQ = distance along x-axis from -2 to 2 = 4 units. Length of QR = distance along y-axis from -3 to 3 = 6 units.\n(iii) Area = length * breadth = 4 * 6 = 24 square units.", "rubric_keywords": ["rectangle", "side length", "area", "24 square units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write whether the given statement is true or false: 'The coordinates of a point whose ordinate is -4 and abscissa is 3 are (-4, 3).' Justify your answer.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "False. The abscissa is the x-coordinate (written first) and the ordinate is the y-coordinate (written second). Given abscissa = 3 and ordinate = -4, the correct coordinates are (3, -4), not (-4, 3).", "rubric_keywords": ["false", "abscissa", "ordinate", "(3, -4)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(1, 1), B(4, 1), and C(1, 5) on a graph sheet. \n(i) Join AB, BC, and CA to form a geometric shape. Name the shape formed.\n(ii) Find the coordinates of the mid-point of the hypotenuse if applicable, or find the area of the triangle ABC.\n(iii) State the property of coordinates for points lying on the line parallel to the y-axis passing through point A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Joining A(1,1), B(4,1), and C(1,5) forms a right-angled triangle at A.\n(ii) Base AB = 4 - 1 = 3 units. Height AC = 5 - 1 = 4 units. Area = 1/2 * base * height = 1/2 * 3 * 4 = 6 square units. The hypotenuse is BC, and its midpoint coordinates can be found using the distance concept or inspection, but primarily the area is 6 square units.\n(iii) Any point lying on the line parallel to the y-axis passing through A(1,1) will have a constant x-coordinate equal to 1 (i.e., abscissa = 1).", "rubric_keywords": ["right-angled triangle", "area", "6 square units", "x-coordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the horizontal line drawn in the Cartesian plane to determine the position of a point called?", "options": ["Y-axis", "X-axis", "Origin", "Quadrant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The horizontal line is called the X-axis, and the vertical line is called the Y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["X-axis", "horizontal line", "Cartesian plane"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In which quadrant does the point (-2, 4) lie?", "options": ["First quadrant", "Second quadrant", "Third quadrant", "Fourth quadrant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since the x-coordinate is negative (-2) and the y-coordinate is positive (4), the point lies in the second quadrant.", "rubric_keywords": ["second quadrant", "negative x", "positive y"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The intersection of the horizontal and vertical lines determining the position of a point in a Cartesian plane is called the ___ .", "options": ["Origin", "Quadrant", "Axis", "Coordinate"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Origin. Explanation: The point of intersection of the X-axis and Y-axis is known as the origin, and its coordinates are (0, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["Origin", "intersection", "X-axis", "Y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Write the answer to each of the following questions: (i) What is the name of the horizontal and the vertical lines drawn to determine the position of any point in the Cartesian plane? (ii) What is the name of each part of the plane formed by these two lines?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) The horizontal line is the X-axis and the vertical line is the Y-axis. (ii) Each part of the plane is called a quadrant.", "rubric_keywords": ["X-axis", "Y-axis", "quadrant"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Write the coordinates of a point whose ordinate is -5 and which lies on the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since the point lies on the y-axis, its x-coordinate (abscissa) is 0. Given that the ordinate is -5, the coordinates of the point are (0, -5).", "rubric_keywords": ["y-axis", "abscissa", "0", "-5"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the points in Column I with their respective quadrants or axes in Column II. Column I: 1. (3, -2), 2. (-4, -4), 3. (0, 5), 4. (-2, 3). Column II: A. Y-axis, B. Fourth quadrant, C. Second quadrant, D. Third quadrant.", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-(3, -2) has positive x and negative y, so it is in the Fourth quadrant (B). 2-(-4, -4) has both negative coordinates, so it is in the Third quadrant (D). 3-(0, 5) has x = 0, so it lies on the Y-axis (A). 4-(-2, 3) has negative x and positive y, so it is in the Second quadrant (C). Thus, the correct match is 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["Fourth quadrant", "Third quadrant", "Y-axis", "Second quadrant"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, -7) lies on the y-axis. Reason (R): The x-coordinate of any point on the y-axis is zero.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. Since the x-coordinate is 0, the point lies on the y-axis by definition.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "y-axis", "x-coordinate zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(2, 4), B(2, -3), and C(-2, -3) on graph paper. Join AB, BC, and CA. What is the geometric figure formed, and what are its base and height?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Plotting A(2, 4), B(2, -3), and C(-2, -3): AB is a vertical segment of length 4 - (-3) = 7 units along x = 2. BC is a horizontal segment of length 2 - (-2) = 4 units along y = -3. AC connects (-2, -3) to (2, 4). The figure formed is a right-angled triangle ABC with base BC = 4 units and height AB = 7 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["right-angled triangle", "base", "height", "plotting"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The abscissa of a point is its perpendicular distance from the:", "options": ["Origin", "X-axis", "Y-axis", "None of these"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The abscissa (x-coordinate) of a point is its perpendicular distance from the Y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["abscissa", "perpendicular distance", "Y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If the coordinates of a point are (a, b) such that a > 0 and b < 0, in which quadrant does the point lie? Justify your answer.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The point lies in the fourth quadrant. Justification: In the fourth quadrant, the x-coordinate (abscissa 'a') is positive (> 0) and the y-coordinate (ordinate 'b') is negative (< 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["fourth quadrant", "positive abscissa", "negative ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The point of intersection of the X-axis and the Y-axis in a Cartesian plane is called:", "options": ["A) Quadrant", "B) Origin", "C) Abscissa", "D) Ordinate"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. Explanation: The X-axis and Y-axis intersect perpendicularly at the point (0,0), which is known as the origin.", "rubric_keywords": ["origin", "intersection", "X-axis", "Y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If the coordinates of a point are (-3, 4), then the point lies in which quadrant?", "options": ["A) First Quadrant", "B) Second Quadrant", "C) Third Quadrant", "D) Fourth Quadrant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. Explanation: In the second quadrant, the x-coordinate is negative and the y-coordinate is positive (-3, 4).", "rubric_keywords": ["second quadrant", "negative abscissa", "positive ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The abscissa of any point on the Y-axis is always ___.", "options": ["0", "1", "-1", "Undefined"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "0. Explanation: Every point on the Y-axis has a horizontal distance of zero from the Y-axis, meaning its x-coordinate (abscissa) is 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["abscissa", "Y-axis", "zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Write the coordinates of a point whose ordinate is -5 and abscissa is 3. Also, state in which quadrant this point lies.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Coordinates: (3, -5). Since the abscissa is positive and the ordinate is negative, the point lies in the Fourth Quadrant.", "rubric_keywords": ["coordinates", "(3, -5)", "fourth quadrant", "abscissa", "ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the location of points in Column I with their corresponding signs of coordinates in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. First Quadrant\n2. Second Quadrant\n3. Third Quadrant\n4. Fourth Quadrant\nColumn II:\nA) (-x, -y)\nB) (+x, -y)\nC) (+x, +y)\nD) (-x, +y)", "options": ["1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Match: 1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B. Explanation: First quadrant has both coordinates positive (+x, +y), second has negative x and positive y (-x, +y), third has both negative (-x, -y), and fourth has positive x and negative y (+x, -y).", "rubric_keywords": ["quadrant signs", "positive", "negative", "cartesian plane"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, -4) lies on the negative Y-axis.\nReason (R): Every point on the X-axis has its y-coordinate equal to zero.\nChoose the correct option: \n(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true but R is false.\n(d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a)", "(b)", "(c)", "(d)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. Explanation: Assertion is true because x=0 and y=-4 lies on the negative Y-axis. Reason is also true as points on the X-axis have y=0, but it does not explain why (0,-4) lies on the negative Y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "Y-axis", "X-axis", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the perpendicular distance of the point P(4, 3) from the X-axis and the Y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Perpendicular distance from the X-axis is equal to the absolute value of its ordinate, which is 3 units. Perpendicular distance from the Y-axis is equal to the absolute value of its abscissa, which is 4 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["perpendicular distance", "X-axis", "Y-axis", "abscissa", "ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(2, 2), B(-2, 2), C(-2, -2), and D(2, -2) on a Cartesian plane. Join them in order. What geometric figure is formed, and what is its area?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Figure formed: Square. Side length of the square = 4 units (from x = -2 to 2 or y = -2 to 2). Area = side × side = 4 × 4 = 16 square units.", "rubric_keywords": ["square", "area", "16 square units", "plotting points", "side length"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The mirror image of the point (3, 5) along the X-axis is:", "options": ["A) (-3, 5)", "B) (3, -5)", "C) (-3, -5)", "D) (5, 3)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. Explanation: The mirror image of a point (x, y) across the X-axis has the same x-coordinate and negated y-coordinate, resulting in (3, -5).", "rubric_keywords": ["mirror image", "X-axis", "(3, -5)", "negated ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw a triangle ABC whose vertices are A(0, 4), B(0, 0), and C(3, 0). Find the base, height, and area of the triangle ABC using coordinate geometry concepts.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Base AB (or BC along axes): Vertices B(0,0) and C(3,0) lie on the X-axis, so base BC = 3 units. Height: Vertex A(0,4) is at a perpendicular height of 4 units along the Y-axis from the origin. Area of triangle = (1/2) × base × height = (1/2) × 3 × 4 = 6 square units.", "rubric_keywords": ["triangle", "base", "height", "area", "6 square units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the name of the horizontal and the vertical lines drawn to determine the position of any point in the Cartesian plane?", "options": ["Coordinate axes", "X-axis and Y-axis", "Origin and quadrants", "Both A and B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option D. Explanation: The horizontal line is called the x-axis and the vertical line is called the y-axis. Together they are known as the coordinate axes.", "rubric_keywords": ["horizontal line", "vertical line", "coordinate axes", "x-axis", "y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The point (-3, 5) lies in which quadrant?", "options": ["First quadrant", "Second quadrant", "Third quadrant", "Fourth quadrant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. Explanation: In the second quadrant, the x-coordinate (abscissa) is negative and the y-coordinate (ordinate) is positive. Therefore, (-3, 5) lies in the second quadrant.", "rubric_keywords": ["second quadrant", "negative x", "positive y", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The abscissa of a point on the y-axis is ___.", "options": ["0", "1", "-1", "any real number"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "0. Explanation: Every point on the y-axis has no horizontal distance from the y-axis, meaning its x-coordinate (abscissa) is always 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["abscissa", "y-axis", "zero", "0"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write the coordinates of the origin. Also, state the signs of the abscissa and ordinate of any point lying in the third quadrant.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Coordinates of the origin are (0, 0). In the third quadrant, both the abscissa (x-coordinate) and the ordinate (y-coordinate) are negative (i.e., -, -).", "rubric_keywords": ["origin", "(0, 0)", "third quadrant", "negative", "abscissa", "ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(2, 3), B(-2, 3), C(-2, -3) and D(2, -3) on a Cartesian plane. What is the geometrical figure formed by joining these points in order?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The points are plotted in their respective quadrants. By joining AB, BC, CD, and DA, the distance AB = 4 units and BC = 6 units. Since adjacent sides are unequal and all interior angles are 90 degrees, the geometrical figure formed is a rectangle.", "rubric_keywords": ["rectangle", "plot points", "sides", "90 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the points in Column I with their correct location in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. (0, -4)\n2. (3, -2)\n3. (-5, -2)\n4. (4, 0)\nColumn II:\nA. Fourth quadrant\nB. y-axis (negative direction)\nC. x-axis (positive direction)\nD. Third quadrant", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Match: 1-B (0, -4 lies on negative y-axis), 2-A (3, -2 lies in fourth quadrant), 3-D (-5, -2 lies in third quadrant), 4-C (4, 0 lies on positive x-axis).", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "column I", "column II", "quadrants", "axes"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the distance of the point P(3, 4) from the origin using coordinate geometry concepts.", "options": [], "variables": {"x": 3, "y": 4}, "answer_key": "Distance from origin O(0,0) to P(3,4) is given by sqrt(x^2 + y^2) = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = sqrt(9 + 16) = sqrt(25) = 5 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["distance", "origin", "sqrt", "5 units", "Pythagoras"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, 4) lies on the y-axis.\nReason (R): The x-coordinate of every point on the y-axis is zero.", "options": ["Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).", "Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of Assertion (A).", "Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.", "Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A. Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and Reason (R) correctly explains why the point (0, 4) lies on the y-axis (since its x-coordinate is 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "y-axis", "x-coordinate zero"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A botanical garden map is set on a coordinate plane where the main entrance is at the origin (0, 0). The rose garden is located at R(3, 4), the fountain at F(-2, 3), and the greenhouse at G(-4, -3).\n(i) In which quadrant does the greenhouse G(-4, -3) lie?\n(ii) What is the abscissa of the rose garden R(3, 4)?\n(iii) Find the distance between the origin and the rose garden R(3, 4).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) The greenhouse G(-4, -3) lies in the third quadrant because both coordinates are negative. (ii) The abscissa (x-coordinate) of R(3, 4) is 3. (iii) Distance from origin to R(3, 4) = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["third quadrant", "abscissa", "distance", "origin", "rose garden"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(5, 0), B(0, 5), C(-5, 0), and D(0, -5) on a Cartesian plane.\n(i) Join AB, BC, CD, and DA. Name the geometrical figure formed.\n(ii) Find the coordinates of the point where the diagonals AC and BD intersect.\n(iii) Calculate the length of side AB.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) The figure formed is a square because all four sides are equal (sqrt(5^2+5^2) = 5sqrt(2) units) and diagonals bisect each other at right angles. (ii) The diagonals intersect at the origin (0, 0). (iii) Length of side AB = sqrt((0-5)^2 + (5-0)^2) = sqrt(25 + 25) = sqrt(50) = 5sqrt(2) units.", "rubric_keywords": ["square", "diagonals", "origin", "5sqrt(2)", "length"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the name of the horizontal and the vertical lines drawn to determine the position of any point in the Cartesian plane?", "options": ["The abscissa and ordinate", "The x-axis and y-axis", "Quadrant and origin", "Horizontal and vertical lines"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The correct option is 'The x-axis and y-axis'. In a Cartesian plane, the horizontal line is called the x-axis and the vertical line is called the y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["Cartesian plane", "x-axis", "y-axis", "horizontal", "vertical"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The intersection of the horizontal line (x-axis) and the vertical line (y-axis) in a Cartesian plane is called the ___.", "options": ["Origin", "Quadrant", "Abscissa", "Ordinate"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Origin. The point of intersection of the x-axis and y-axis is called the origin, denoted by (0, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["Origin", "intersection", "x-axis", "y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If the coordinates of a point are (-3, 0), then it lies on:", "options": ["The x-axis", "The y-axis", "First quadrant", "Third quadrant"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The correct option is 'The x-axis'. Since the ordinate (y-coordinate) is 0, the point lies on the x-axis (specifically on the negative side of the x-axis).", "rubric_keywords": ["x-axis", "ordinate zero", "-3, 0"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "What is the abscissa of any point lying on the y-axis? Give the general coordinates of a point on the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The abscissa of any point on the y-axis is 0. The general coordinates of a point on the y-axis are given by (0, y), where y is the ordinate.", "rubric_keywords": ["abscissa", "0", "y-axis", "coordinates (0, y)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In which quadrants do the points (-2, 4) and (3, -5) lie? Justify your answer based on the signs of their coordinates.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The point (-2, 4) has a negative abscissa and a positive ordinate (-ive, +ive), so it lies in the Second Quadrant. The point (3, -5) has a positive abscissa and a negative ordinate (+ive, -ive), so it lies in the Fourth Quadrant.", "rubric_keywords": ["Second Quadrant", "Fourth Quadrant", "signs", "abscissa", "ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Plot the points A(2, 2), B(-2, 2), C(-2, -2), and D(2, -2) on a Cartesian plane. Join them in order. Identify the geometric figure formed and calculate its area.", "options": [], "variables": {"x": 2, "y": 2}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Plot the points A(2,2), B(-2,2), C(-2,-2), D(2,-2). Step 2: Joining the points forms a square because all four sides are equal in length (length = 4 units) and interior angles are 90 degrees. Step 3: Area of the square = side * side = 4 * 4 = 16 square units.", "rubric_keywords": ["square", "side length", "4 units", "area", "16 square units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A rectangle PQRS has vertices P(1, 2), Q(5, 2), and R(5, 5). (a) Find the coordinates of the fourth vertex S. (b) Find the length of the diagonal PR.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(a) In rectangle PQRS, opposite sides are equal and parallel. Since P(1,2) and Q(5,2) are on the horizontal line y=2 (length = 4 units), and QR is vertical at x=5 (width = 3 units from y=2 to y=5), vertex S must have the same x-coordinate as P (x=1) and the same y-coordinate as R (y=5). Thus, S = (1, 5). (b) The diagonal PR connects P(1, 2) and R(5, 5). Using the distance formula: Distance = sqrt((5-1)^2 + (5-2)^2) = sqrt(4^2 + 3^2) = sqrt(16 + 9) = sqrt(25) = 5 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertex S", "(1, 5)", "diagonal", "distance formula", "5 units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, -7) lies on the negative direction of the y-axis.\nReason (R): Any point with zero abscissa and negative ordinate lies on the y-axis below the origin.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). The point (0, -7) has x = 0 (zero abscissa) and y = -7 (negative ordinate), placing it on the negative y-axis.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "negative y-axis", "zero abscissa", "negative ordinate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the points in Column I with their correct location in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. (4, -3)\n2. (-2, -5)\n3. (-5, 2)\n4. (3, 3)\nColumn II:\nA. Second Quadrant\nB. Fourth Quadrant\nC. First Quadrant\nD. Third Quadrant", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (4, -3 is in Fourth Quadrant), 2-D (-2, -5 is in Third Quadrant), 3-A (-5, 2 is in Second Quadrant), 4-C (3, 3 is in First Quadrant). Correct option: 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["Match", "Quadrants", "signs", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Case Study: In a school mathematics lab, students are seated on a grid floor. Student A sits at position (2, 3), Student B sits at (5, 3), and Student C sits at (5, 7).\n(i) What is the distance between Student A and Student B?\n(ii) What is the distance between Student B and Student C?\n(iii) If these three seats form three vertices of a rectangle ABCD, what are the coordinates of Student D?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Distance between A(2,3) and B(5,3) along the horizontal line y=3 is |5 - 2| = 3 units. (ii) Distance between B(5,3) and C(5,7) along the vertical line x=5 is |7 - 3| = 4 units. (iii) For rectangle ABCD with A(2,3), B(5,3), C(5,7), the fourth vertex D must align with x of A (x=2) and y of C (y=7), so D has coordinates (2, 7).", "rubric_keywords": ["Case Study", "distance", "3 units", "4 units", "vertex D (2, 7)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a linear equation in two variables?", "options": ["x^2 + y = 5", "2x + 3y = 7", "xy + 2 = 6", "3x - 5 = 0"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: 2x + 3y = 7. Rationale: A linear equation in two variables is of the form ax + by + c = 0, where a, b, and c are real numbers, and a and b are not both zero, with the highest degree of variables being 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "two variables", "degree one", "ax + by + c = 0"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find four different solutions for the equation 2x + y = 7.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Express y in terms of x: y = 7 - 2x. Step 2: Substitute values for x. For x = 0, y = 7 - 0 = 7 -> Solution (0, 7). For x = 1, y = 7 - 2(1) = 5 -> Solution (1, 5). For x = 2, y = 7 - 2(2) = 3 -> Solution (2, 3). For x = 3, y = 7 - 2(3) = 1 -> Solution (3, 1). Final Answer: (0, 7), (1, 5), (2, 3), and (3, 1).", "rubric_keywords": ["solutions", "substitution", "y = 7 - 2x", "coordinate pairs"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The graph of the linear equation 2x + 3y = 6 cuts the x-axis at the point:", "options": ["(0, 2)", "(3, 0)", "(2, 0)", "(0, 3)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: (3, 0). Rationale: To find where the graph cuts the x-axis, put y = 0 in the equation. 2x + 3(0) = 6 => 2x = 6 => x = 3. Thus, the point is (3, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["x-axis", "y = 0", "intercept", "(3, 0)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The graph of every linear equation in two variables is a ___.", "options": ["straight line", "parabola", "circle", "point"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Exact missing word: straight line. Explanation: Every solution of a linear equation in two variables represents a point on the Cartesian plane, and all such points lie on a straight line.", "rubric_keywords": ["straight line", "graph", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Check whether x = 2, y = 1 is a solution of the equation 2x - 3y = 1.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Substitute x = 2 and y = 1 into the LHS of the equation 2x - 3y. Step 2: LHS = 2(2) - 3(1) = 4 - 3 = 1. Step 3: Since LHS = RHS (1 = 1), x = 2, y = 1 is a solution of the given equation.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "LHS = RHS", "solution check"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation x + y = 4. Find the coordinates of the points where the graph intersects the x-axis and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Find points for the equation x + y = 4. If x = 0, y = 4 (0, 4). If x = 4, y = 0 (4, 0). If x = 2, y = 2 (2, 2). Step 2: Plot these points on the Cartesian plane and join them with a straight line. Step 3: The line intersects the x-axis where y = 0, giving point (4, 0). It intersects the y-axis where x = 0, giving point (0, 4). Final Answer: Intersects x-axis at (4, 0) and y-axis at (0, 4).", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "x-axis intercept", "y-axis intercept", "(4, 0)", "(0, 4)"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): x = 2, y = 1 is a solution of 2x + 3y = 7. Reason (R): Every linear equation in two variables has infinitely many solutions.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. Rationale: Checking A: 2(2) + 3(1) = 4 + 3 = 7, so A is true. R is also a true mathematical statement about linear equations in two variables, but it does not explain why (2, 1) specifically satisfies this equation.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "infinitely many solutions", "verification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If the point (3, 4) lies on the graph of the equation 3y = ax + 7, find the value of a.", "options": [], "variables": {"x_val": 3, "y_val": 4, "constant": 7}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Since the point (3, 4) lies on the graph, it satisfies the equation. Substitute x = 3 and y = 4 into 3y = ax + 7. Step 2: 3(4) = a(3) + 7. Step 3: 12 = 3a + 7. Step 4: 3a = 12 - 7 => 3a = 5 => a = 5/3. Final Answer: a = 5/3.", "rubric_keywords": ["point lies on graph", "substitution", "value of a", "5/3"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A taxi fare in a city is as follows: For the first kilometer, the fare is Rs 8 and for the subsequent distance it is Rs 5 per km. Taking the distance covered as x km and total fare as y Rs, write a linear equation for this information, and find the total fare for a journey of 5 km.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Fare for the first km = Rs 8. Distance remaining after 1 km = (x - 1) km. Fare for the remaining distance = 5 * (x - 1). Step 2: Total fare y = 8 + 5(x - 1) => y = 8 + 5x - 5 => y = 5x + 3. Step 3: For a journey of 5 km, substitute x = 5 into the equation. y = 5(5) + 3 = 25 + 3 = 28. Final Answer: Equation is y = 5x + 3, and total fare for 5 km is Rs 28.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "taxi fare", "y = 5x + 3", "subsequent distance", "Rs 28"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Match the linear equations in Column I with their corresponding solutions in Column II:\nColumn I (Equations):\n1. x + y = 5\n2. 2x - y = 3\n3. x - y = 1\n4. 3x + y = 10\n\nColumn II (Solutions):\nA. (3, 2)\nB. (2, 1)\nC. (4, 1)\nD. (3, 1)", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A: 1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D. Explanation: 1. x + y = 5 is satisfied by (4, 1) -> 4+1=5 (C). 2. 2x - y = 3 is satisfied by (3, 2) -> 2(3)-2=4? Wait, let's check 2(3)-2 = 4 (not 3). Let's check 2(2)-1 = 3 -> (2, 1) is B. Let's re-verify: 3. x - y = 1 is satisfied by (2, 1)? 2-1=1 -> (2, 1). Let's check options carefully. Correct matching: 1. x + y = 5 -> (4, 1) [C]; 2. 2x - y = 3 -> (3, 1) [2(3)-1=5, wait. If x=2, y=1: 2(2)-1=3 -> 2-B. Let's verify 4. 3x + y = 10 -> 3(3)+1 = 10 -> (3, 1) [4-D]. Correct match mapping: 1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["Match the following", "solutions", "pairing", "verification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a linear equation in two variables?", "options": ["x^2 + y = 5", "2x + 3y = 6", "xy + 2 = 0", "x + (1/y) = 3"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: 2x + 3y = 6 is a linear equation in two variables because the highest degree of variables x and y is 1 and it can be expressed in the general form ax + by + c = 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "degree one", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find four different solutions of the equation 2x + y = 7.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Express y in terms of x: y = 7 - 2x. Step 2: Substitute values for x. For x = 0, y = 7 - 0 = 7 (Solution: (0, 7)). For x = 1, y = 7 - 2 = 5 (Solution: (1, 5)). For x = 2, y = 7 - 4 = 3 (Solution: (2, 3)). For x = 3, y = 7 - 6 = 1 (Solution: (3, 1)). Final Answer: (0, 7), (1, 5), (2, 3), (3, 1).", "rubric_keywords": ["solutions", "substitution", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If (2, 0) is a solution of the linear equation 2x + 3y = k, then the value of k is:", "options": ["2", "4", "6", "8"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: Substitute x = 2 and y = 0 in 2x + 3y = k. We get 2(2) + 3(0) = k, which gives 4 + 0 = k, so k = 4.", "rubric_keywords": ["solution", "substitution", "value of k"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Write the equations of two lines passing through the point (2, 14). How many more such lines are there, and why?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Two equations passing through (2, 14) can be x + y = 16 (since 2 + 14 = 16) and 7x - y = 0 (since 7(2) - 14 = 0). Step 2: There are infinitely many such lines because infinitely many lines can pass through a single given point.", "rubric_keywords": ["infinite lines", "point coordinates", "satisfy equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation x + y = 4. Also, find the coordinates of the points where the line meets the x-axis and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Find solutions for x + y = 4. If x = 0, y = 4; if x = 4, y = 0; if x = 2, y = 2. Step 2: Plot the points (0, 4), (4, 0), and (2, 2) on a Cartesian plane and join them with a straight line. Step 3: The line meets the x-axis at (4, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 4).", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "intercepts", "cartesian plane", "solutions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A linear equation in two variables has ___ solutions.", "options": ["unique", "two", "infinitely many", "zero"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Exact missing term: 'infinitely many'. Explanation: A linear equation in two variables represents a straight line, and every line consists of infinitely many points, each representing a solution.", "rubric_keywords": ["infinitely many", "solutions", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the linear equations in Column I with their corresponding solutions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. x - y = 2\n2. 2x + y = 6\n3. 3x - 2y = 4\n4. x + 2y = 5\nColumn II:\nA. (3, 0)\nB. (4, 2)\nC. (2, 2)\nD. (2, 1)", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct match: 1-B (4 - 2 = 2), 2-C (2(2) + 2 = 6), 3-D (3(2) - 2(1) = 4), 4-A (3 + 2(0) = 3 -> Wait, for 4. x + 2y = 5, if A is (3,0) then 3+0=3 which is not 5. Let's use correct pair for 4: if y=1, x=3 then (3,1). Let's adjust matching reasoning: 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, and matching 4 with (3,1).", "rubric_keywords": ["matching", "satisfies equation", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The cost of a notebook is twice the cost of a pen. Write a linear equation in two variables to represent this statement. If the cost of a pen is ₹15, find the cost of the notebook.", "options": [], "variables": {"cost_pen": 15, "multiplier": 2}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let the cost of a notebook be x and the cost of a pen be y. Step 2: According to the question, x = 2y or x - 2y = 0. Step 3: Given cost of pen (y) = ₹15. Step 4: Cost of notebook (x) = 2 * 15 = ₹30. Final Answer: Linear equation is x - 2y = 0, and cost of the notebook is ₹30.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "word problem", "cost", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): x = 2, y = 1 is a solution of the linear equation 2x + 3y = 7.\nReason (R): A linear equation in two variables has infinitely many solutions.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: Both (A) and (R) are true statements. Checking (A): 2(2) + 3(1) = 4 + 3 = 7, so (2, 1) is a valid solution. Reason (R) is also true, but it does not explain why this specific pair satisfies the equation.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "solution", "infinitely many solutions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions:\nA taxi in a city charges ₹20 for the first kilometer and ₹10 per km for the subsequent distance. Taking the total distance covered as x km and total fare as ₹y:\n(i) Write a linear equation for this statement.\n(ii) If the total distance covered is 5 km, what is the total fare?\n(iii) If the total fare is ₹70, find the total distance covered.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: For the first km, fare = ₹20. For the remaining (x - 1) km, fare = 10(x - 1). Total fare y = 20 + 10(x - 1) = 10x + 10. Step 2: For x = 5 km, y = 10(5) + 10 = ₹60. Step 3: For y = 70, 70 = 10x + 10 => 60 = 10x => x = 6 km.", "rubric_keywords": ["case study", "taxi fare", "linear equation", "distance and cost"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a linear equation in two variables?", "options": ["x^2 + y = 4", "2x + 3y = 5", "xy + x = 2", "1/x + 1/y = 2"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B is correct because 2x + 3y = 5 is of the form ax + by + c = 0 where the highest degree of variables x and y is 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "two variables", "degree one"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "How many solutions does a linear equation in two variables have?", "options": ["Only one solution", "Two solutions", "Infinitely many solutions", "No solution"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C is correct. A linear equation in two variables has infinitely many solutions because every point on the line representing the equation is a solution.", "rubric_keywords": ["infinitely many solutions", "linear equation", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The graph of every linear equation in two variables is a _____.", "options": ["straight line", "parabola", "circle", "point"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "straight line. Explanation: Every solution (x, y) of a linear equation in two variables represents a point on the Cartesian plane, and when all such points are joined, they form a straight line.", "rubric_keywords": ["straight line", "graph", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Express the linear equation 2x + 3y = 9.35 in the form ax + by + c = 0 and indicate the values of a, b and c.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given equation: 2x + 3y = 9.35. Rewriting in the standard form ax + by + c = 0: 2x + 3y - 9.35 = 0. Comparing with ax + by + c = 0, we get a = 2, b = 3, and c = -9.35.", "rubric_keywords": ["standard form", "a=2", "b=3", "c=-9.35"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Check whether (4, 0) is a solution of the equation x - 2y = 4.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Substitute x = 4 and y = 0 in the given equation x - 2y = 4. LHS = 4 - 2(0) = 4 - 0 = 4. Since LHS = RHS (4 = 4), (4, 0) is a solution of the given linear equation.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "LHS equals RHS", "solution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the value of k, if x = 2 and y = 1 is a solution of the equation 2x + 3y = k.", "options": [], "variables": {"x": 2, "y": 1}, "answer_key": "Given equation: 2x + 3y = k. Given that x = 2 and y = 1 is a solution, substitute these values into the equation: 2(2) + 3(1) = k. => 4 + 3 = k. => k = 7.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitute", "k = 7", "solution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (3, 2) lies on the graph of the linear equation 2x + 3y = 12. Reason (R): Every point that satisfies a linear equation is a solution of the equation.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A is correct. Let's check (3, 2) in 2x + 3y = 12: 2(3) + 3(2) = 6 + 6 = 12 (True). Thus, Assertion is true. Reason explains why satisfying points are solutions.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion and Reason", "correct explanation", "satisfies equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I (Linear Equations) with Column II (Properties of their graphs):\nColumn I:\n1. x = 0\n2. y = 0\n3. x - y = 0\nColumn II:\nA. Equation of the x-axis\nB. Equation of the y-axis\nC. Passes through the origin", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (x = 0 is y-axis), 2-A (y = 0 is x-axis), 3-C (x - y = 0 passes through origin (0,0)). Thus correct option is 1-B, 2-A, 3-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["y-axis", "x-axis", "origin", "match"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation x + y = 4. Find the coordinates of the points where the graph intersects the x-axis and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "For equation x + y = 4: When x = 0, y = 4 (Point A: (0, 4)). When y = 0, x = 4 (Point B: (4, 0)). When x = 2, y = 2 (Point C: (2, 2)). Plot these points on the Cartesian plane and join them with a straight line. The line intersects the x-axis at (4, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 4).", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "x-axis intersection", "y-axis intersection", "straight line"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions:\nA taxi fare in a city is as follows: For the first kilometre, the fare is ₹8 and for the subsequent distance it is ₹5 per km. Taking the distance covered as x km and total fare as ₹y:\n(i) Write a linear equation representing this statement.\n(ii) If the total distance covered is 4 km, find the total fare.\n(iii) If the total fare is ₹33, find the distance covered.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Fare for 1st km = ₹8. Remaining distance = (x - 1) km. Fare for remaining distance = 5(x - 1). Total fare y = 8 + 5(x - 1) => y = 5x + 3.\n(ii) For x = 4 km, y = 5(4) + 3 = 20 + 3 = ₹23.\n(iii) For y = 33, 33 = 5x + 3 => 5x = 30 => x = 6 km.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "y = 5x + 3", "fare calculation", "distance"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The linear equation 2x + 3y = 6 has:", "options": ["A unique solution", "Only two solutions", "Infinitely many solutions", "No solution"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Infinitely many solutions because every linear equation in two variables has infinitely many solutions corresponding to each real value assigned to one variable.", "rubric_keywords": ["infinitely many solutions", "linear equation", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If (2, 0) is a solution of the linear equation 2x + 3y = k, then the value of k is:", "options": ["4", "6", "2", "0"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Substitute x = 2 and y = 0 into 2x + 3y = k. We get 2(2) + 3(0) = k, which gives k = 4. Therefore, option A is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["solution", "substitute", "value of k"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write the linear equation 3x + 2y = 9 in the standard form ax + by + c = 0 and indicate the values of a, b and c.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The given equation is 3x + 2y = 9. Transposing 9 to the L.H.S., we get 3x + 2y - 9 = 0. Comparing this with ax + by + c = 0, we get a = 3, b = 2, and c = -9.", "rubric_keywords": ["standard form", "a = 3", "b = 2", "c = -9"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find four different solutions of the equation x + 2y = 6.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1. Put x = 0, then 0 + 2y = 6 => y = 3. Solution: (0, 3).\n2. Put y = 0, then x + 2(0) = 6 => x = 6. Solution: (6, 0).\n3. Put x = 2, then 2 + 2y = 6 => 2y = 4 => y = 2. Solution: (2, 2).\n4. Put x = 4, then 4 + 2y = 6 => 2y = 2 => y = 1. Solution: (4, 1).\nThus, four solutions are (0, 3), (6, 0), (2, 2), and (4, 1).", "rubric_keywords": ["solutions", "substitute", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation x + y = 4. From the graph, find the value of y when x = 3.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "For x + y = 4: When x = 0, y = 4; When x = 4, y = 0; When x = 2, y = 2. Plotting these points (0, 4), (4, 0), and (2, 2) on a graph and joining them gives a straight line. From the graph, when x = 3, the corresponding value of y is 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "straight line", "x = 3", "y = 1"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The graph of every linear equation in two variables is a ___ line.", "options": ["straight", "curved", "parabolic", "circular"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "straight. Every linear equation in two variables geometrically represents a straight line.", "rubric_keywords": ["straight line", "graph", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the linear equations in Column I with their corresponding solutions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. 2x + y = 7\n2. x - y = 2\n3. 3x + 2y = 12\n4. x + 2y = 8\n\nColumn II:\nA) (4, 0)\nB) (2, 3)\nC) (4, 2)\nD) (2, 3)", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Verification: 1. 2x + y = 7 satisfied by (2, 3) -> 1-B; 2. x - y = 2 satisfied by (4, 2) -> 2-C; 3. 3x + 2y = 12 satisfied by (4, 0) -> 3-A; 4. x + 2y = 8 satisfied by (2, 3) -> 4-D. Correct combination is 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "solution", "substitution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The equation x = 5 can be written as a linear equation in two variables as 1x + 0y - 5 = 0.\nReason (R): A linear equation in two variables is of the form ax + by + c = 0, where a, b, c are real numbers and a^2 + b^2 not equal to 0.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true, and R correctly explains why x = 5 can be expressed with y having a coefficient of 0 in the standard form ax + by + c = 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "standard form", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nIn a school, a charity fund is collected where Class IX students contribute. Two students, Amit and Rohit, together contribute Rs. 100 towards the relief fund.\n(i) Express this situation as a linear equation in two variables.\n(ii) If Rohit contributes Rs. 40, how much does Amit contribute?\n(iii) Write one solution where both contribute equally.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Let Amit's contribution be x and Rohit's contribution be y. Equation: x + y = 100.\n(ii) If y = 40, then x + 40 = 100 => x = 60. Amit contributes Rs. 60.\n(iii) If both contribute equally, x = y. Since x + x = 100 => 2x = 100 => x = 50, the solution is (50, 50).", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "contribution", "x + y = 100", "50, 50"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Find the points where the graph of the equation 3x + 4y = 12 cuts the x-axis and the y-axis. Also, find the area of the triangle formed by this line and the coordinate axes.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "For x-axis, put y = 0: 3x + 4(0) = 12 => 3x = 12 => x = 4. Point on x-axis is (4, 0). For y-axis, put x = 0: 3(0) + 4y = 12 => 4y = 12 => y = 3. Point on y-axis is (0, 3). The triangle formed with coordinate axes has base along x-axis (b = 4 units) and height along y-axis (h = 3 units). Area = 1/2 * base * height = 1/2 * 4 * 3 = 6 square units.", "rubric_keywords": ["x-axis", "y-axis", "intercepts", "area of triangle", "6 square units"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a linear equation in two variables?", "options": ["x^2 + 2y = 5", "2x + 3y = 7", "xy + 2 = 6", "3x + y^2 = 4"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B is correct. A linear equation in two variables is of degree 1 and contains two variables. Here, 2x + 3y = 7 is of degree 1 with variables x and y.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "degree 1", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The graph of every linear equation in two variables is a _____", "options": ["straight line", "parabola", "circle", "ellipse"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "straight line. Explanation: Every solution of a linear equation in two variables corresponds to a point on the Cartesian plane, and all such points lie on a straight line.", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "straight line", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find four distinct solutions for the equation 2x + y = 7.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Express y in terms of x: y = 7 - 2x. Step 2: Substitute values for x. For x = 0, y = 7 - 0 = 7 (Solution: (0, 7)). For x = 1, y = 7 - 2 = 5 (Solution: (1, 5)). For x = 2, y = 7 - 4 = 3 (Solution: (2, 3)). For x = 3, y = 7 - 6 = 1 (Solution: (3, 1)). Thus, four solutions are (0, 7), (1, 5), (2, 3), and (3, 1).", "rubric_keywords": ["solutions", "substitution", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Check whether x = 2 and y = 1 is a solution of the equation 2x + 3y = 7.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Substitute x = 2 and y = 1 into the LHS of the equation 2x + 3y = 7. Step 2: LHS = 2(2) + 3(1) = 4 + 3 = 7. Step 3: Since LHS = RHS (7 = 7), x = 2, y = 1 is a solution of the given equation.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "LHS equals RHS", "solution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express the linear equation 2x + 3y = 9.35 (with bar over 5) in the form ax + by + c = 0 and indicate the values of a, b, and c.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Bring all terms to the left-hand side to get the standard form ax + by + c = 0. 2x + 3y - 9.35 (bar on 5) = 0. Step 2: Comparing with ax + by + c = 0, we get a = 2, b = 3, and c = -9.35 (bar on 5).", "rubric_keywords": ["standard form", "coefficients", "a, b, c"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "How many solutions does the linear equation y = 3x + 5 have?", "options": ["Unique solution", "Two solutions", "Infinitely many solutions", "No solution"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C is correct. A linear equation in two variables has infinitely many solutions because for every value of x, there corresponds a unique value of y, and vice versa.", "rubric_keywords": ["infinitely many solutions", "linear equation", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation x + y = 4. Find the coordinates of the points where the graph intersects the x-axis and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Find points on the line. When x = 0, y = 4 (Point (0, 4) on y-axis). When y = 0, x = 4 (Point (4, 0) on x-axis). When x = 2, y = 2 (Point (2, 2)). Step 2: Plot these points on the Cartesian plane and join them with a straight line. Step 3: The graph intersects the x-axis at (4, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 4).", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "x-intercept", "y-intercept", "straight line"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The point (0, 3) lies on the graph of the linear equation 3x + 4y = 12. Reason (R): Every point satisfying the linear equation is a solution of the equation.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A is correct. Checking Assertion: Substituting x = 0, y = 3 in 3(0) + 4(3) = 12 gives 12 = 12, which is true. Reason is also true and correctly explains why the point lies on the graph.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "satisfies equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Match Column I (Linear Equation) with Column II (Solution passing through it):\nColumn I: 1. x + y = 5, 2. 2x - y = 4, 3. 3x + 2y = 12, 4. x - 2y = 0\nColumn II: A. (4, 0), B. (2, 3), C. (2, 1), D. (3, 2)", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A is correct. Verification: 1. x + y = 5 -> for (2, 3), 2 + 3 = 5 (1-B). 2. 2x - y = 4 -> for (3, 2), 2(3) - 2 = 4 (2-D). 3. 3x + 2y = 12 -> for (4, 0), 3(4) + 2(0) = 12 (3-A). 4. x - 2y = 0 -> for (2, 1), 2 - 2(1) = 0 (4-C).", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "linear equation", "solutions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A taxi fare in a city is as follows: For the first kilometer, the fare is Rs 8 and for the subsequent distance it is Rs 5 per km. Taking the distance covered as x km and total fare as Rs y, write a linear equation for this information and draw its graph.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Total distance = x km. Distance for subsequent km = (x - 1) km. Fare for first km = Rs 8. Fare for subsequent distance = 5(x - 1). Total fare y = 8 + 5(x - 1) => y = 5x + 3. Step 2: Standard form is 5x - y + 3 = 0. Step 3: To draw the graph, find points: for x = 0, y = 3; for x = 1, y = 8; for x = -0.6, y = 0. Plot these points and draw the straight line.", "rubric_keywords": ["case study", "word problem", "linear equation", "graph"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The linear equation 2x + 3y = 6 has:", "options": ["A unique solution", "Only two solutions", "Infinitely many solutions", "No solution"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Infinitely many solutions. A linear equation in two variables has infinitely many solutions because every point on the line represents a solution.", "rubric_keywords": ["infinitely many solutions", "linear equation", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If (2, 0) is a solution of the linear equation 2x + 3y = k, then the value of k is:", "options": ["2", "4", "6", "0"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "4. Substituting x = 2 and y = 0 in the equation 2x + 3y = k, we get 2(2) + 3(0) = k, which gives k = 4.", "rubric_keywords": ["solution", "substitute", "value of k"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Express the linear equation 3x + 2y = 9 in the form ax + by + c = 0 and indicate the values of a, b and c.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The given equation is 3x + 2y = 9. Transposing 9 to the LHS, we get 3x + 2y - 9 = 0. Comparing this with the general form ax + by + c = 0, we get a = 3, b = 2, and c = -9.", "rubric_keywords": ["ax + by + c = 0", "a = 3", "b = 2", "c = -9"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write any two solutions for the linear equation 4x + 3y = 12.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Put x = 0, then 3y = 12 implies y = 4. So, (0, 4) is a solution. Put y = 0, then 4x = 12 implies x = 3. So, (3, 0) is a solution. Thus, two solutions are (0, 4) and (3, 0).", "rubric_keywords": ["solutions", "substitute", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation x + y = 4. Find the coordinates of the points where the graph intersects the x-axis and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "For x + y = 4: If x = 0, y = 4 (point (0, 4) on y-axis). If y = 0, x = 4 (point (4, 0) on x-axis). Plotting these points and joining them forms a straight line. The graph intersects the x-axis at (4, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 4).", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "x-axis intersection", "y-axis intersection", "straight line"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The graph of every linear equation in two variables is a ___.", "options": ["straight line", "circle", "parabola", "curve"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "straight line. Every linear equation in two variables geometrically represents a straight line in a Cartesian plane.", "rubric_keywords": ["straight line", "geometric representation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The equation x = 5 can be written as a linear equation in two variables as 1x + 0y - 5 = 0. Reason (R): A linear equation in two variables is represented in the general form ax + by + c = 0.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. Explanation: x = 5 implies x - 5 = 0, which can be expressed in two variables as 1x + 0y - 5 = 0, satisfying the general form ax + by + c = 0.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "general form", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A taxi fare in a city is as follows: For the first kilometer, the fare is Rs 20 and for the subsequent distance it is Rs 6 per km. Taking the distance covered as x km and total fare as Rs y, write a linear equation for this information and draw its graph.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Fare for the first km = Rs 20. Remaining distance = (x - 1) km. Fare for remaining distance = 6(x - 1). Total fare y = 20 + 6(x - 1) = 20 + 6x - 6 = 6x + 14. Linear equation: 6x - y + 14 = 0. For graph plotting, solutions can be (0, 14), (-1, 8), (-2, 2).", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "taxi fare", "graph", "word problem"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the linear equations in Column I with their respective properties in Column II.\nColumn I:\n1. 2x + y = 7\n2. x - y = 2\n3. y = 4\n4. x = 0\nColumn II:\nA. Equation of y-axis\nB. Passes through (3, 1)\nC. Passes through (4, 2)\nD. Line parallel to x-axis at distance 4 units", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (Since 2(3) + 1 = 7), 2-C (Since 4 - 2 = 2), 3-D (y = 4 is parallel to x-axis), 4-A (x = 0 represents the y-axis). Correct option is 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A.", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "solutions", "properties"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the value of m if x = 2, y = 1 is a solution of the equation 2x + 3y = m. Hence, find one more solution of this equation.", "options": [], "variables": {"x": 2, "y": 1}, "answer_key": "Given equation: 2x + 3y = m. Substitute x = 2 and y = 1: 2(2) + 3(1) = m => 4 + 3 = m => m = 7. The equation is 2x + 3y = 7. Let x = -1, then 2(-1) + 3y = 7 => -2 + 3y = 7 => 3y = 9 => y = 3. Another solution is (-1, 3).", "rubric_keywords": ["value of m", "substitute", "another solution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a linear equation in two variables?", "options": ["x^2 + y = 5", "2x + 3y = 6", "xy + 2 = 0", "x + 2y^2 = 7"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B (2x + 3y = 6). Explanation: A linear equation in two variables is of degree 1 and contains two variables. 2x + 3y = 6 has variables x and y with degree 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "two variables", "degree 1"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The graph of every linear equation in two variables is a ___.", "options": ["straight line", "circle", "parabola", "point"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "straight line. Explanation: Geometrically, every solution of a linear equation in two variables represents a point on the line, and every linear equation in two variables graph is a straight line.", "rubric_keywords": ["straight line", "graph", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Write four solutions for the equation: 2x + y = 7.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Express y in terms of x: y = 7 - 2x. Step 2: Substitute values for x. For x = 0, y = 7 - 2(0) = 7 -> (0, 7). For x = 1, y = 7 - 2(1) = 5 -> (1, 5). For x = 2, y = 7 - 2(2) = 3 -> (2, 3). For x = 3, y = 7 - 2(3) = 1 -> (3, 1). Final Answer: (0, 7), (1, 5), (2, 3), (3, 1).", "rubric_keywords": ["solutions", "substitute", "y = 7 - 2x"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "How many linear equations can be satisfied by x = 2 and y = 3?", "options": ["Only one", "Two", "Infinitely many", "Zero"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C (Infinitely many). Explanation: Infinitely many lines can pass through a single point (2, 3) in a Cartesian plane, meaning infinitely many linear equations can be satisfied by the pair (2, 3).", "rubric_keywords": ["infinitely many", "solutions", "point"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the value of k if x = 2 and y = 1 is a solution of the equation 2x + 3y = k.", "options": [], "variables": {"x": 2, "y": 1}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Given equation is 2x + 3y = k. Step 2: Substitute x = 2 and y = 1 into the equation. 2(2) + 3(1) = k. Step 3: Simplify -> 4 + 3 = k => k = 7. Final Answer: k = 7.", "rubric_keywords": ["value of k", "substitute", "solution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Check whether (0, 2) is a solution of the equation x - 2y = 4.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Given equation is x - 2y = 4 and point is (0, 2) where x = 0, y = 2. Step 2: Substitute LHS: 0 - 2(2) = -4. Step 3: Compare with RHS: LHS (-4) != RHS (4). Final Answer: (0, 2) is not a solution of the given equation.", "rubric_keywords": ["check solution", "LHS", "RHS"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The graph of the linear equation x = 3 is a line parallel to the y-axis. Reason (R): Every equation of the form x = a has a graph parallel to the y-axis at a distance 'a' from it.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A (Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A). Explanation: For x = 3, the x-coordinate is always 3 for any value of y. This forms a vertical line parallel to the y-axis at a distance of 3 units.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel", "y-axis", "x = a"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation x + y = 4. Find the coordinates of the points where the graph intersects the x-axis and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Find solutions. When x = 0, y = 4 -> (0, 4). When y = 0, x = 4 -> (4, 0). When x = 2, y = 2 -> (2, 2). Step 2: Plot points (0,4), (4,0), and (2,2) on Cartesian plane and join them with a straight line. Step 3: The line intersects the x-axis at (4, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 4). Final Answer: Intersects x-axis at (4,0) and y-axis at (0,4).", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "intersects", "x-axis", "y-axis"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I (Linear Equation) with Column II (Solution passing through it): Column I: 1. 2x + y = 5, 2. x - y = 2, 3. 3x + 2y = 12. Column II: A. (4, 0), B. (2, 1), C. (3, 2), D. (3, 1).", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C", "1-C, 2-D, 3-B", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (For 2(2) + 1 = 5), 2-D (For 3 - 1 = 2), 3-A (For 3(4) + 2(0) = 12). Final Answer: 1-B, 2-D, 3-A.", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "solution", "column"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions: In a school welfare drive, two students Asha and Amit together contributed ₹ 100 towards the Prime Minister's Relief Fund. (i) Express this data in the form of a linear equation in two variables. (ii) If Asha contributed ₹ 40, how much did Amit contribute?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let Asha's contribution be ₹ x and Amit's contribution be ₹ y. Step 2: According to the question, x + y = 100 (Linear equation). Step 3: If x = 40, then 40 + y = 100 => y = 100 - 40 = 60. Final Answer: (i) x + y = 100 (ii) Amit contributed ₹ 60.", "rubric_keywords": ["contribution", "linear equation", "x + y = 100"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is a linear equation in two variables?", "options": ["A) $x^2 + y = 5$", "B) $2x + 3y = 7$", "C) $xy + x = 4$", "D) $3x + 2 = 8$"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B) $2x + 3y = 7$. Rationale: A linear equation in two variables is of the form $ax + by + c = 0$, where $a, b, c$ are real numbers and $a \\neq 0, b \\neq 0$, with variables having an exponent of 1.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation", "two variables", "degree one", "standard form"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Check whether $(2, 1)$ is a solution of the linear equation $2x + 3y = 7$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Substitute $x = 2$ and $y = 1$ in the given equation. L.H.S = $2(2) + 3(1) = 4 + 3 = 7$. Since L.H.S = R.H.S, $(2, 1)$ is a solution of the equation $2x + 3y = 7$.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "LHS equals RHS", "solution check"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A linear equation in two variables has ___ solutions.", "options": ["unique", "two", "infinitely many", "zero"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "infinitely many. Rationale: For every value of $x$, there is a corresponding value of $y$ that satisfies a linear equation in two variables, hence it has infinitely many solutions.", "rubric_keywords": ["infinitely many solutions", "linear equation", "two variables"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If $x = 2$ and $y = 1$ is a solution of the equation $2x + 3y = k$, then the value of $k$ is:", "options": ["A) 5", "B) 6", "C) 7", "D) 8"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C) 7. Rationale: Substituting $x = 2$ and $y = 1$ in $2x + 3y = k$, we get $2(2) + 3(1) = 4 + 3 = 7 = k$.", "rubric_keywords": ["value of k", "substitution", "solution"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Write any two solutions for the linear equation $4x + 3y = 12$.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Put $x = 0$, then $4(0) + 3y = 12 \\implies 3y = 12 \\implies y = 4$. So, $(0, 4)$ is a solution.\nStep 2: Put $y = 0$, then $4x + 3(0) = 12 \\implies 4x = 12 \\implies x = 3$. So, $(3, 0)$ is another solution. (Other valid solutions like $(6, -4)$ are also correct).", "rubric_keywords": ["two solutions", "x-intercept", "y-intercept", "coordinates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the equations in Column I with their geometric representations in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. $x = 0$\n2. $y = 0$\n3. $x = 5$\n4. $y = -3$\n\nColumn II:\nA. Equation of x-axis\nB. Equation of y-axis\nC. Line parallel to y-axis at a distance of 5 units\nD. Line parallel to x-axis at a distance of 3 units below x-axis", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D. Rationale: $x = 0$ represents the y-axis, $y = 0$ represents the x-axis, $x = 5$ is a vertical line parallel to the y-axis at $x = 5$, and $y = -3$ is a horizontal line parallel to the x-axis 3 units below it.", "rubric_keywords": ["axis equations", "parallel lines", "geometric representation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Draw the graph of the linear equation $x + y = 4$. Find the coordinates of the points where the graph intersects the x-axis and the y-axis.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Find solutions for $x + y = 4$.\n- If $x = 0$, $y = 4$ \\implies Point $(0, 4)$\n- If $y = 0$, $x = 4$ \\implies Point $(4, 0)$\n- If $x = 2$, $y = 2$ \\implies Point $(2, 2)$\nStep 2: Plot these points $(0, 4)$, $(4, 0)$, and $(2, 2)$ on a Cartesian plane and join them with a straight line.\nStep 3: The graph intersects the x-axis at $(4, 0)$ and the y-axis at $(0, 4)$.", "rubric_keywords": ["graph", "straight line", "x-axis intersection", "y-axis intersection", "cartesian plane"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "A taxi fare in a city is as follows: For the first kilometer, the fare is Rs 8 and for the subsequent distance it is Rs 5 per km. Taking the distance covered as $x$ km and total fare as Rs $y$, write a linear equation for this information, and find the total fare for a distance of 10 km.", "options": [], "variables": {"first_km_fare": 8, "subsequent_km_fare": 5, "distance": 10}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Fare for the first km = Rs 8.\nStep 2: Distance remaining after 1 km = $(x - 1)$ km.\nStep 3: Fare for the remaining distance = $5(x - 1)$.\nStep 4: Total fare $y = 8 + 5(x - 1) = 8 + 5x - 5 = 5x + 3$.\nStep 5: For a distance of 10 km ($x = 10$), total fare $y = 5(10) + 3 = 50 + 3 = \\text{Rs } 53$.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear equation formulation", "taxi fare", "substitution", "calculation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Given below are two statements labeled as Assertion (A) and Reason (R):\nAssertion (A): The graph of every linear equation in two variables is a straight line.\nReason (R): Every solution of a linear equation in two variables represents a point on the graph of the equation.\nChoose the correct option:\n(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).\n(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).\n(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.\n(d) (A) is false but (R) is true.", "options": ["(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "(b) Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(c) (A) is true but (R) is false.", "(d) (A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(a) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). Rationale: Every solution $(x, y)$ of a linear equation corresponds to a point on its graph, and the collection of all such points forms a straight line.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "straight line graph", "solutions as points"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions:\nIn a charity book fair, two friends A and B together contributed Rs 100 towards the Prime Minister's Relief Fund.\n(i) Express this statement as a linear equation in two variables.\n(ii) If friend A contributed Rs 40, how much did friend B contribute?\n(iii) Write two possible solutions for this equation.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Let the contribution of friend A be Rs $x$ and friend B be Rs $y$. Linear equation: $x + y = 100$.\n(ii) If $x = 40$, then $40 + y = 100 \\implies y = 100 - 40 = 60$. Friend B contributed Rs 60.\n(iii) Two possible solutions: $(40, 60)$ and $(50, 50)$ [or any other valid pair summing to 100].", "rubric_keywords": ["case study", "linear equation formulation", "substitution", "solutions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "According to Euclid's axioms, the things which are double of the same thing are:", "options": ["Equal to one another", "Half of the same thing", "Unequal", "Double of one another"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Answer is 'Equal to one another'. According to Euclid's sixth axiom, 'Things which are double of the same things are equal to one another.'", "rubric_keywords": ["Euclid's axiom", "double", "equal to one another"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "According to Euclid's definitions, a ___ is that which has no part.", "options": ["Point", "Line", "Plane", "Surface"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Answer is 'Point'. Euclid defined a point as that which has no part in his Elements.", "rubric_keywords": ["point", "no part", "definition"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "How many lines can pass through a single given point?", "options": ["One", "Two", "Infinite", "Zero"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Answer is 'Infinite'. An infinite number of lines can pass through a single point.", "rubric_keywords": ["single point", "infinite lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "State Euclid's fifth postulate (Parallel Postulate).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid's Fifth Postulate states: If a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles on the same side of it taken together less than two right angles, then the two straight lines, if produced indefinitely, meet on that side on which the sum of angles is less than two right angles.", "rubric_keywords": ["fifth postulate", "interior angles", "two right angles", "meet"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Pyramid is a solid figure whose base is a rectilinear rectilineal figure and whose faces are triangles meeting at a common vertex.\nReason (R): Euclid summarized all previous mathematical knowledge in his book 'Elements'.\nChoose the correct option: (a) Both A and R are true and R is correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Answer is (b). Both statements are independently true facts about Euclid's geometry and historical context, but R does not explain A.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "pyramid", "elements"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I with Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Boundaries of surfaces\n2. Ends of a line\n3. A line with one end point\n4. Surface without breadth\nColumn II:\nA. Points\nB. Curves\nC. Ray\nD. Line", "options": ["1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Match: 1-B (Boundaries of surfaces are curves), 2-A (Ends of a line are points), 3-C (Ray has one endpoint), 4-D (Surface without breadth - note: Euclid defined a straight line as breadthless length, let's match appropriately: boundaries of surfaces are curves/lines, ends are points). Correct pairings: 1-B, 2-A, 3-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["boundaries", "surfaces", "ends of a line", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = (1/2)AB. Explain by drawing the figure.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides: AC + AC = BC + AC. Since BC + AC coincides with AB (as point C lies between A and B), we have 2AC = AB. Therefore, AC = (1/2)AB.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BC", "axiom", "midpoint", "proof"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "In the given line segment AB, if AC = BD, then prove that AB = CD. State the Euclid's axiom used here.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AC = BD. From the figure, AC = AB + BC and BD = BC + CD. Substituting these: AB + BC = BC + CD. Using Euclid's third axiom ('If equals be subtracted from equals, the wholes are equal'), subtracting BC from both sides gives AB = CD.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BD", "Euclid's axiom", "subtracted from equals", "AB = CD"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is true regarding axioms in geometry?", "options": ["Axioms are proved theorems", "Axioms are self-evident assumptions used throughout mathematics", "Axioms are specific to geometry only", "Axioms require rigorous proof"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Answer is 'Axioms are self-evident assumptions used throughout mathematics'. Axioms are assumptions that are accepted without proof across all branches of mathematics, unlike postulates which were specifically geometric.", "rubric_keywords": ["axioms", "self-evident assumptions", "mathematics"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Why is Euclid's fifth postulate considered distinct from his other postulates?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid's first four postulates were simple, self-evident geometric constructions (like joining two points or drawing a circle), whereas the fifth postulate is more complex and involves infinite behavior (lines never intersecting on one side if sum of angles is less than two right angles), leading mathematicians to try proving it as a theorem for centuries.", "rubric_keywords": ["fifth postulate", "complex", "infinite behavior", "distinct"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The number of dimensions, a solid has, is:", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A solid has 3 dimensions: length, breadth, and height. Therefore, the correct option is 3.", "rubric_keywords": ["dimensions", "solid", "three"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "According to Euclid's definition, the boundaries of surfaces are ___.", "options": ["lines", "points", "curves", "surfaces"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "According to Euclid's definitions in his Elements, the boundaries of surfaces are lines or curves. Correct answer: lines.", "rubric_keywords": ["boundaries", "surfaces", "lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Which of the following is true regarding Euclid's axioms?", "options": ["Axioms are proved theorems specific to geometry.", "Axioms are assumptions used throughout mathematics without proof.", "Axioms are only applicable to arithmetic.", "Axioms require experimental verification."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Axioms are self-evident assumptions that are used throughout mathematics, not just in geometry. Therefore, option B is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["axioms", "assumptions", "mathematics"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another. Reason (R): This statement is Euclid's first axiom.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid's first axiom states: 'Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another.' Thus both Assertion and Reason are true, and Reason is the correct explanation. Option A is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["axiom", "equal", "Euclid"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "State Euclid's Fifth Postulate (Parallel Postulate).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid's Fifth Postulate states: If a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles on the same side of it taken together less than two right angles, then the two straight lines, if produced indefinitely, meet on that side on which the sum of angles is less than two right angles.", "rubric_keywords": ["fifth postulate", "straight line", "interior angles", "parallel"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = (1/2)AB. Explain with reasoning.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides: AC + AC = BC + AC => 2AC = AB (since BC + AC = AB, point C lies between A and B). Therefore, AC = (1/2)AB.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BC", "half of AB", "Euclid axiom"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the following Euclid terms with their definitions:\nColumn I:\n1. A point\n2. A line\n3. A surface\nColumn II:\nA. Has length and breadth only\nB. That which has no part\nC. Breadthless length", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B", "1-B, 2-A, 3-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1. A point is that which has no part (B). 2. A line is breadthless length (C). 3. A surface has length and breadth only (A). Correct option is 1-B, 2-C, 3-A.", "rubric_keywords": ["point", "line", "surface", "definitions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "If AC = BD, then prove that AB = CD. State the Euclid's axiom used to prove this.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AC = BD. From the line segment layout, AC = AB + BC and BD = BC + CD. Substituting these gives AB + BC = BC + CD. According to Euclid's third axiom ('If equals be subtracted from equals, the wholes are equal'), subtracting BC from both sides yields AB = CD.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BD", "AB = CD", "subtracted from equals", "axiom"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nEuclid summarized the work of earlier mathematicians and arranged it in his famous treatise called 'Elements'. He divided 'Elements' into 13 chapters, each called a book. His work set a new standard for logical reasoning in geometry.\n(i) How many books did Euclid's 'Elements' contain?\n(ii) Why are axioms and postulates considered fundamental in Euclid's geometry?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Euclid's 'Elements' contained 13 books. (ii) Axioms and postulates are considered fundamental because they are self-evident unproved assumptions upon which all subsequent geometric theorems and logical deductions are built.", "rubric_keywords": ["Elements", "13 books", "axioms", "postulates", "assumptions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In the statement 'A square is a polygon bounded by four equal line segments', check whether this definition is consistent and contains undefined terms. List the undefined terms involved.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Yes, the definition is consistent. Undefined terms involved in this definition include: point, line segment (or line), and plane/surface. According to Euclid, geometric terms like point and line are accepted without definition as fundamental building blocks.", "rubric_keywords": ["consistent", "undefined terms", "point", "line segment"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "How many lines can pass through two distinct points?", "options": ["One and only one", "Infinite", "Two", "None"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A. According to Euclid's postulate 1, a straight line may be drawn from any one point to any other point, and there is one and only one line passing through two distinct points.", "rubric_keywords": ["distinct points", "one and only one line", "Euclid's postulate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The side faces of a pyramid are:", "options": ["Triangles", "Squares", "Rectangles", "Polygons"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A. According to Euclid's definitions in Chapter 5, the boundaries of surfaces are curves or straight lines, and specifically for a pyramid, its side faces are triangles.", "rubric_keywords": ["pyramid", "side faces", "triangles", "surfaces"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The things which are double of the same thing are ___ one another.", "options": ["equal to", "greater than", "less than", "half of"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "equal to. Explanation: This is one of Euclid's Common Notions (Axioms): 'Things which are double of the same things are equal to one another.'", "rubric_keywords": ["double", "same thing", "equal to", "Common Notions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "State Euclid's fifth postulate (Parallel postulate).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid's fifth postulate states: If a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles on the same side of it taken together less than two right angles, then the two straight lines, if produced indefinitely, meet on that side on which the sum of angles is less than two right angles.", "rubric_keywords": ["interior angles", "two right angles", "produced indefinitely", "meet"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In a line segment AC, if B is a point between A and C such that AB = BC, then prove that AC = 2AB. Which axiom of Euclid is used here?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AB = BC. We know AC = AB + BC. Substituting BC with AB, we get AC = AB + AB = 2AB. Euclid's axiom used: 'Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another' and 'If equals be added to equals, the wholes are equal'.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = AB + BC", "substituted", "equals added to equals", "axiom"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Axioms are assumptions that are specific to geometry. Reason (R): Postulates are common notions that are used throughout mathematics without specific reference to geometry.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "Both (A) and (R) are false."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option D. Both Assertion and Reason are false. Axioms (or common notions) refer to assumptions used throughout mathematics, whereas postulates are assumptions specifically related to geometry.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "false", "axioms", "postulates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Explain why Euclid's fifth postulate is considered one of the most important postulates in geometry. How does it relate to the concept of parallel lines?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid's fifth postulate states that if the sum of interior angles on one side is less than 180 degrees (two right angles), lines intersect on that side. As a corollary, if the sum equals 180 degrees, the lines never intersect and are parallel. This postulate led to the historical development and discovery of non-Euclidean geometries when mathematicians attempted to prove it as a theorem.", "rubric_keywords": ["fifth postulate", "parallel lines", "interior angles", "non-Euclidean geometry"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the terms in Column I with their Euclid's definitions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Point\n2. Line\n3. Straight line\n4. Surface\n\nColumn II:\nA. That which has length and breadth only.\nB. A line which lies evenly with the points on itself.\nC. That which has no part.\nD. Breadthless length.", "options": ["1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-A, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A. Explanation: According to Euclid's definitions: A point is that which has no part (1-C). A line is breadthless length (2-D). A straight line is a line which lies evenly with the points on itself (3-B). A surface is that which has length and breadth only (4-A).", "rubric_keywords": ["Point", "Line", "Straight line", "Surface", "definitions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the following scenario and answer the questions:\nTwo railway tracks run alongside each other on a flat plain. Engineers ensure that the perpendicular distance between the two tracks remains constant at all points.\n(i) Which geometric property or postulate does this scenario illustrate?\n(ii) If a transversal line crosses both tracks such that the interior angles on one side sum to 170 degrees, what happens to the tracks if extended indefinitely on that side?\n(iii) State the equivalent Playfair's axiom related to parallel lines.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Parallel lines property derived from Euclid's fifth postulate. (ii) Since the sum of interior angles (170 degrees) is less than 180 degrees (two right angles), the tracks will intersect (meet) on that side when produced indefinitely. (iii) Playfair's axiom: 'Through a given point not on a given line, there can be drawn only one line parallel to the given line.'", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel tracks", "intersect", "less than 180", "Playfair's axiom"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If x + y = 15 and x = y, use Euclid's axiom to find the value of x.", "options": [], "variables": {"sum": 15}, "answer_key": "Given x + y = 15 and x = y. Substituting y with x (since things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another), we get x + x = 15, which gives 2x = 15, so x = 7.5. Euclid's axiom used: 'Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another'.", "rubric_keywords": ["substitution", "2x = 15", "x = 7.5", "Euclid's axiom"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "According to Euclid's axioms, things which are equal to the same thing are:", "options": ["Not equal to one another", "Equal to one another", "Half of the same thing", "Double of the same thing"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal to one another. Axiom 1 states that things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another.", "rubric_keywords": ["Axiom 1", "equal", "same thing"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A point is that which has no ___.", "options": ["length", "breadth", "part", "dimension"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "part. Euclid's first definition states that 'A point is that which has no part.'", "rubric_keywords": ["part", "definition", "point"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "State Euclid's Fifth Postulate (Parallel Postulate).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "If a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles on the same side of it taken together less than two right angles, then the two straight lines, if produced indefinitely, meet on that side on which the sum of angles is less than two right angles.", "rubric_keywords": ["interior angles", "two right angles", "produced indefinitely", "meet"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The boundaries of surfaces are:", "options": ["Curves", "Lines", "Points", "Surfaces"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Lines. According to Euclid's definitions, the boundaries of a surface are lines.", "rubric_keywords": ["boundaries", "surfaces", "lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Postulates are assumptions that are specific to geometry.\nReason (R): Axioms are common notions that are used throughout mathematics and not specifically linked to geometry.\nChoose the correct option: (a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a)", "(b)", "(c)", "(d)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. Euclid used the term 'postulate' for the assumptions specific to geometry, while 'axioms' were common notions applicable across all mathematics.", "rubric_keywords": ["Postulates", "geometry", "Axioms", "common notions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the geometrical objects in Column I with their number of dimensions in Column II:\nColumn I: 1. Solid, 2. Surface, 3. Line, 4. Point\nColumn II: A. 0 dimension, B. 1 dimension, C. 2 dimensions, D. 3 dimensions\nChoose the correct pairing option.", "options": ["1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A. Solid has 3 dimensions (length, breadth, height), surface has 2 dimensions, line has 1 dimension, and point has 0 dimensions.", "rubric_keywords": ["Solid", "Surface", "Line", "Point", "dimensions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In the given figure, if AB = BC and X is the midpoint of AB and Y is the midpoint of BC, show that AX = CY.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AB = BC. Since X is the midpoint of AB, AX = 1/2 AB. Since Y is the midpoint of BC, CY = 1/2 BC. By Euclid's Axiom: 'If equals are divided by equals, the wholes are equal' (or halves of equals are equal), since AB = BC, 1/2 AB = 1/2 BC, hence AX = CY.", "rubric_keywords": ["midpoint", "halves", "equals", "AX = CY"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that two distinct lines cannot have more than one point in common using Euclid's axioms.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Proof by contradiction: Let there be two distinct lines l and m, and let them have two distinct points in common, say P and Q. Thus, through two distinct points P and Q, there pass two lines l and m. But according to Euclid's Postulate 1, 'A straight line may be drawn from any one point to any other point', which implies there is one and only one line passing through two distinct points. This contradicts our assumption that there are two distinct lines passing through P and Q. Therefore, two distinct lines cannot have more than one point in common.", "rubric_keywords": ["contradiction", "Postulate 1", "two distinct points", "one line"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Which of the following statements is true according to Euclid's elements?", "options": ["All right angles are equal to one another.", "If a circle is drawn, its radius is half its diameter.", "A line can be extended infinitely without any rules.", "Postulates and axioms have the exact same definition."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "All right angles are equal to one another. This is Euclid's fourth postulate.", "rubric_keywords": ["right angles", "equal", "Postulate 4"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nEuclid summarized the knowledge of geometry known up to that time in his treatise called 'Elements', dividing it into thirteen chapters called books. He defined basic terms like point, line, and surface and laid down postulates and axioms.\n(i) How many books were there in Euclid's 'Elements'? (1 mark)\n(ii) State Euclid's Postulate 2. (1 mark)\n(iii) If a, b, c are three lines in a plane such that line a is parallel to line b and line b is parallel to line c, state the relation between line a and line c using Euclid's axioms. (2 marks)", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Thirteen books. (ii) Euclid's Postulate 2: 'A terminated line can be produced indefinitely.' (iii) By Euclid's Axiom 'Things which are parallel to the same line are parallel to each other', line a is parallel to line c.", "rubric_keywords": ["thirteen books", "terminated line", "produced indefinitely", "parallel"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Things which are equal to the same thing are:", "options": ["Not equal to one another", "Equal to one another", "Half of the same thing", "Double of the same thing"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: Equal to one another. This is Euclid's first common notion (axiom) stating that quantities equal to the same quantity are equal to each other.", "rubric_keywords": ["axiom", "common notion", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The number of dimensions a surface has is ___.", "options": ["zero", "one", "two", "three"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "two. According to Euclid, a surface has length and breadth only, hence it possesses two dimensions.", "rubric_keywords": ["surface", "dimensions", "two"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "According to Euclid's fifth postulate, if a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles on the same side of it taken together less than two right angles, then the two straight lines, if produced indefinitely, meet on that side on which:", "options": ["The sum of angles is equal to two right angles", "The sum of angles is less than two right angles", "The sum of angles is greater than two right angles", "The lines are perpendicular"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: The sum of angles is less than two right angles. This is the direct statement of Euclid's parallel postulate.", "rubric_keywords": ["fifth postulate", "interior angles", "two right angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = (1/2)AB. Explain using the relevant axiom.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides gives AC + AC = AC + BC. Since AC + BC = AB (coincident parts make up the whole), 2AC = AB, which implies AC = (1/2)AB. This uses Euclid's axiom: 'If equals are added to equals, the wholes are equal'.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BC", "axiom", "equals added to equals", "halves"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Axioms are assumptions that are obvious universal truths and are not proved. Reason (R): Theorems are mathematical statements that require logical proof using definitions, axioms, and previously proved statements.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A: Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). Axioms are self-evident accepted truths without proof, whereas theorems require logical deduction.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion", "reason", "axioms", "theorems"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the geometric terms in Column I with their respective dimensions in Column II:\nColumn I: 1. Solid, 2. Surface, 3. Line, 4. Point\nColumn II: A. 0 dimension, B. 1 dimension, C. 2 dimensions, D. 3 dimensions", "options": ["1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A. Solids have length, breadth, and height (3 dimensions); surfaces have length and breadth (2 dimensions); lines have length only (1 dimension); and points have no length, breadth, or height (0 dimension).", "rubric_keywords": ["solid", "surface", "line", "point", "dimensions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In the given configuration, if AB = PQ and PQ = XY, write the relation between AB and XY and state the Euclid's axiom used.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Relation: AB = XY. Euclid's Axiom used: 'Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another' (Euclid's First Axiom).", "rubric_keywords": ["AB = XY", "First Axiom", "equal to the same thing"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "State Euclid's five postulates clearly. Why was Euclid's fifth postulate considered distinct or more complex than the first four postulates?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Postulates: 1. A straight line may be drawn from any one point to any other point. 2. A terminated line can be produced indefinitely. 3. A circle can be described with any centre and any radius. 4. All right angles are equal to one another. 5. If a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles... The fifth postulate is much more complex and lacks the obvious self-evident nature of the first four, prompting centuries of attempts to prove it from the others which eventually led to non-Euclidean geometries.", "rubric_keywords": ["postulates", "fifth postulate", "self-evident", "non-Euclidean geometry"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "According to Euclid's definitions, the boundaries of surfaces are:", "options": ["Curves or straight lines", "Points", "Surfaces", "Solids"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A: Curves or straight lines. In Book I of Euclid's Elements, it is defined that the boundaries of a surface are lines.", "rubric_keywords": ["boundaries", "surfaces", "lines", "curves"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Give an algebraic or arithmetic example that illustrates Euclid's common notion: 'If equals be subtracted from equals, the remainders are equal.'", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Example: Let $10 = 10$. If equal numbers, say $3$, are subtracted from both sides ($10 - 3 = 10 - 3$), the remainders are equal ($7 = 7$). Geometrically, if equal line segments are cut off from two equal line segments, the remaining segments are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["subtracted from equals", "remainders are equal", "arithmetic example"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The number of dimensions a surface has is:", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C. Explanation: According to Euclid's definitions, a solid has 3 dimensions, a surface has 2 dimensions, a line has 1 dimension, and a point has 0 dimensions.", "rubric_keywords": ["surface", "dimensions", "Euclid's definitions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A ___ is that which has no part.", "options": ["point", "line", "surface", "solid"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "point. Explanation: Euclid's first definition in his Elements states that 'A point is that which has no part'.", "rubric_keywords": ["point", "no part", "definition"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Which of the following is true according to Euclid's axioms?", "options": ["All right angles are equal to one another.", "If equals are added to equals, the wholes are equal.", "A line segment can be produced indefinitely.", "All circles are congruent."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. Explanation: 'If equals are added to equals, the wholes are equal' is one of Euclid's well-known common notions (axioms). Option A is Postulate 4.", "rubric_keywords": ["axioms", "equals added to equals", "wholes are equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "State Euclid's fifth postulate (Parallel Postulate) in your own words.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "If a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles on the same side of it taken together less than two right angles, then the two straight lines, if produced indefinitely, meet on that side on which the sum of angles is less than two right angles.", "rubric_keywords": ["fifth postulate", "interior angles", "less than two right angles", "meet"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = 1/2 AB. Explain with reference to Euclid's axiom.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides, AC + AC = BC + AC. Since BC + AC coincides with AB, we have 2AC = AB. Therefore, AC = 1/2 AB. This uses the axiom: 'If equals are added to equals, the wholes are equal.'", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BC", "2AC = AB", "axiom", "wholes are equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I (Geometric Object) with Column II (Dimension):\nColumn I: 1. Solid, 2. Surface, 3. Line, 4. Point\nColumn II: A. 0, B. 1, C. 2, D. 3", "options": ["1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A. Explanation: Solids have 3 dimensions, surfaces have 2, lines have 1, and points have 0 dimensions.", "rubric_keywords": ["Solid", "Surface", "Line", "Point", "Dimensions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Axioms are assumptions that are specific to geometry.\nReason (R): Postulates are common notions that are used throughout mathematics without specific reference to a particular field.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "Both (A) and (R) are false."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option D. Explanation: Both Assertion and Reason are false. In Euclid's terminology, 'axioms' (or common notions) are assumptions used throughout mathematics generally, whereas 'postulates' are assumptions specific to geometry.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "Axioms", "Postulates"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "In a line segment AB, if AB = BC and BX = BY, show that AX = CY. State the Euclid's axiom used.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: AB = BC and BX = BY. According to Euclid's axiom, 'If equals are subtracted from equals, the remainders are equal.' Subtracting BX from AB and BY from BC: AB - BX = BC - BY. Since AB - BX = AX and BC - BY = CY, we get AX = CY.", "rubric_keywords": ["AB = BC", "BX = BY", "remainders are equal", "AX = CY"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions:\nTwo surveyors are marking straight boundary lines on a large open field. They refer to Euclid's postulates to ensure accuracy. One postulate states that a straight line may be drawn from any one point to any other point.\n(i) How many straight lines can pass through a single given point?\n(ii) Given two distinct points, how many unique straight lines can pass through both of them?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Infinitely many straight lines can pass through a single given point. (ii) Only one unique straight line can pass through two distinct points (Euclid's Postulate 1).", "rubric_keywords": ["surveyors", "single point", "two distinct points", "unique straight line"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a line segment of length L units is divided into two parts such that one part is 3 units longer than the other, find the lengths of both parts using linear equations, and verify how Euclid's axiom 'The whole is greater than the part' applies.", "options": [], "variables": {"L": 15}, "answer_key": "Let the smaller part be x. The other part is x + 3. Total length x + (x + 3) = 15 => 2x + 3 = 15 => 2x = 12 => x = 6. The parts are 6 units and 9 units. Since 6 < 15 and 9 < 15, it verifies Euclid's axiom that 'The whole is greater than the part'.", "rubric_keywords": ["smaller part", "linear equations", "whole is greater than the part"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "According to Euclid's axioms, things which are equal to the same thing are:", "options": ["A) unequal to each other", "B) equal to each other", "C) half of the same thing", "D) double of the same thing"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. According to Euclid's first axiom, things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another.", "rubric_keywords": ["Euclid's axiom", "equal to the same thing", "equal to one another"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The edges of a surface are ___.", "options": ["curves", "lines", "points", "solids"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "lines. The boundaries or edges of a surface are lines.", "rubric_keywords": ["edges of a surface", "lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Which of the following statements needs a proof?", "options": ["A) Axiom", "B) Definition", "C) Postulate", "D) Theorem"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option D. A theorem is a mathematical statement that has been proven true based on axioms, postulates, and previously proven theorems.", "rubric_keywords": ["theorem", "proof", "axiom", "postulate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In a line segment, if AC = BD, then prove that AB = CD. State the Euclid's axiom used.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AC = BD. From the line geometry, AC = AB + BC and BD = BC + CD. Therefore, AB + BC = BC + CD. Using Euclid's third axiom ('If equals are subtracted from equals, the remainders are equal'), subtracting BC from both sides gives AB = CD.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BD", "Euclid's third axiom", "equals subtracted from equals", "AB = CD"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If a straight line falling on two straight lines makes the interior angles on the same side of it taken together less than two right angles, then the two straight lines, if produced indefinitely, meet on that side on which the sum of angles is less than two right angles. Reason (R): This statement is Euclid's fifth postulate.", "options": ["A) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "B) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "C) A is true but R is false", "D) A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A. Both Assertion and Reason are true, and the reason correctly identifies Euclid's fifth postulate.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "Euclid's fifth postulate", "interior angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I (Geometric elements) with Column II (Number of dimensions): Column I: 1. Solid, 2. Surface, 3. Line, 4. Point. Column II: A. 0, B. 1, C. 2, D. 3.", "options": ["1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A. Solid has 3 dimensions (length, breadth, thickness), Surface has 2 dimensions (length, breadth), Line has 1 dimension (length), Point has 0 dimensions.", "rubric_keywords": ["Solid", "Surface", "Line", "Point", "dimensions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "State Playfair's Axiom and mention its significance in relation to Euclid's geometry.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Playfair's Axiom states: 'Through a given point not on a given line, there is one and only one parallel line to the given line.' It is equivalent to Euclid's fifth postulate.", "rubric_keywords": ["Playfair's Axiom", "parallel line", "given point", "Euclid's fifth postulate"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Ram and Shyam have the same weight. If they both gain 2 kg weight, will their new weights be equal? Which axiom of Euclid is used here? Write the statement of that axiom and explain how it applies to this scenario.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Yes, their new weights will be equal. The axiom used is Euclid's second axiom: 'If equals are added to equals, the wholes are equal.' Explanation: Let Ram's initial weight be x and Shyam's be y. Given x = y. Adding 2 kg to both gives x + 2 = y + 2, showing their new weights are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["Euclid's second axiom", "equals are added to equals", "new weights equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A ___ is that which has no part.", "options": ["line", "point", "surface", "solid"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "point. According to Euclid's definition 1, a point is that which has no part.", "rubric_keywords": ["point", "no part", "Euclid's definition"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The number of lines that can pass through a single given point is:", "options": ["A) exactly one", "B) two", "C) infinite", "D) zero"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C. An infinite number of lines can pass through a single point.", "rubric_keywords": ["single point", "infinite lines", "pass through"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following statements is one of Euclid's axioms?", "options": ["Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another.", "All right angles are equal to one another.", "A line is breadthless length.", "A terminated line can be produced indefinitely."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another. (Option A) is an axiom, while options B and D are postulates.", "rubric_keywords": ["axiom", "equal", "Euclid"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Given two distinct points, there is a ___ line that passes through them.", "options": ["unique", "infinite", "two", "zero"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "unique. Axiom 5.1 states that through two distinct points, there is a unique line that passes through them.", "rubric_keywords": ["unique line", "two distinct points", "Axiom 5.1"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Playfair’s Axiom is another version of which of Euclid's postulates?", "options": ["Euclid's First Postulate", "Euclid's Third Postulate", "Euclid's Fourth Postulate", "Euclid's Fifth Postulate"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid's Fifth Postulate (Option D). Playfair's axiom states that through a point not on a given line, exactly one line can be drawn parallel to the given line.", "rubric_keywords": ["Playfair's Axiom", "Fifth Postulate", "parallel lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Distinguish between an axiom and a postulate in Euclid's geometry.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Axioms are assumptions used throughout mathematics generally and not specifically linked to geometry. Postulates are assumptions specific to geometry.", "rubric_keywords": ["axioms", "postulates", "geometry", "general mathematics"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a point C lies between two points A and B such that AC = BC, then prove that AC = (1/2)AB.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AC = BC. Adding AC to both sides gives AC + AC = BC + AC. Since BC + AC = AB (coinciding parts), 2AC = AB, therefore AC = (1/2)AB, using Euclid's axiom that if equals are added to equals, the wholes are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["AC = BC", "equals added to equals", "wholes are equal", "proof"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Pyramid is a solid shape in geometry. Reason (R): A solid has three dimensions: length, breadth, and thickness.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (Option A). Pyramids are 3D geometric objects defined by having length, breadth, and thickness.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "solid", "three dimensions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match Column I with Column II:\nColumn I: 1. A point, 2. A line, 3. First Postulate, 4. Fifth Postulate\nColumn II: A. Breadthless length, B. Has no part, C. A straight line may be drawn from any one point to any other point, D. Parallel lines postulate.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D. 1-B (A point has no part), 2-A (A line is breadthless length), 3-C (First Postulate states a straight line may be drawn from any one point to any other point), 4-D (Fifth Postulate relates to parallel lines).", "rubric_keywords": ["Column I", "Column II", "Match", "Euclid definitions"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "In geometry, why did Euclid need to state axioms and postulates instead of proving every single statement? Explain with examples of how axioms form the foundation of deductive reasoning.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Euclid needed self-evident assumptions (axioms and postulates) because every proof requires a starting point; you cannot prove everything without circular reasoning. Examples include axioms like 'things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another', which form the deductive framework of geometry.", "rubric_keywords": ["self-evident", "axioms", "postulates", "deductive reasoning", "circular reasoning"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If line segments AB = 5 cm and PQ = 5 cm, and PQ = XY, find the length of XY and state the axiom used to find it.", "options": [], "variables": {"length": 5}, "answer_key": "Length of XY = 5 cm. Axiom used: 'Things which are equal to the same thing are equal to one another.' Since AB = PQ and PQ = XY, therefore AB = XY = 5 cm.", "rubric_keywords": ["XY = 5 cm", "things equal to the same thing", "axiom"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Introduction to Euclid's Geometry", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The number of dimensions a surface has is:", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "2 (Option C). According to Euclid's definitions, a surface has length and breadth only, hence two dimensions.", "rubric_keywords": ["surface", "dimensions", "length and breadth"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If a ray stands on a line, then the sum of two adjacent angles so formed is:", "options": ["90°", "180°", "270°", "360°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "180°. According to the Linear Pair Axiom, if a ray stands on a line, then the sum of two adjacent angles so formed is 180°.", "rubric_keywords": ["Linear Pair Axiom", "180 degrees", "adjacent angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, then each pair of corresponding angles is:", "options": ["Equal", "Complementary", "Supplementary", "Unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. According to the Corresponding Angles Axiom, if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of corresponding angles is equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["Corresponding Angles Axiom", "equal", "parallel lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the complement of an angle measuring 35°.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The complement of an angle of measure 35° is 90° - 35° = 55°. Two angles are complementary if their sum is 90°.", "rubric_keywords": ["complementary angles", "90 degrees", "55 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Two supplementary angles are in the ratio 3:2. Find the angles.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x and 2x. Since they are supplementary, 3x + 2x = 180°, so 5x = 180°, which gives x = 36°. Thus, the angles are 3(36°) = 108° and 2(36°) = 72°.", "rubric_keywords": ["supplementary angles", "ratio", "108 degrees", "72 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In a triangle ABC, the ratio of the three angles is 1:2:3. Find the measure of each angle of the triangle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be x, 2x, and 3x. By the Angle Sum Property of a triangle, the sum of all interior angles is 180°. Therefore, x + 2x + 3x = 180° => 6x = 180° => x = 30°. The angles are 30°, 2(30°) = 60°, and 3(30°) = 90°.", "rubric_keywords": ["angle sum property", "30 degrees", "60 degrees", "90 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two lines intersect, then vertically opposite angles are equal. Reason (R): Linear pair of angles are supplementary.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. Both statements are mathematically correct theorems/axioms, but the equality of vertically opposite angles is proved using linear pairs, making R not the direct explanatory definition for A in isolation.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite angles", "linear pair", "supplementary"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The sum of all the angles around a point is ___ degrees.", "options": ["90", "180", "270", "360"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "360. A complete revolution around a point makes an angle of 360°.", "rubric_keywords": ["360 degrees", "complete revolution", "sum of angles around a point"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the terms in Column I with their definitions/properties in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Vertically Opposite Angles\n2. Alternate Interior Angles\n3. Linear Pair\n4. Interior Angles on the same side of transversal\nColumn II:\nA. Equal when lines are parallel\nB. Supplementary when lines are parallel\nC. Always equal when two lines intersect\nD. Adjacent angles whose sum is 180°", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-C (Vertically opposite angles are always equal when two lines intersect), 2-A (Alternate interior angles are equal when lines are parallel), 3-D (Linear pair consists of adjacent angles whose sum is 180°), 4-B (Interior angles on the same side of transversal are supplementary when lines are parallel).", "rubric_keywords": ["Vertically Opposite Angles", "Alternate Interior Angles", "Linear Pair", "co-interior angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of alternate interior angles is equal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: AB || CD and transversal EF intersects them at G and H respectively. To prove: ∠AGH = ∠DHG and ∠BGH = ∠CHG. Proof: Using corresponding angles axiom and vertically opposite angles, we can establish that alternate interior angles are equal. Specifically, ∠EGB = ∠GHD (corresponding angles) and ∠AGH = ∠EGB (vertically opposite angles), hence ∠AGH = ∠GHD.", "rubric_keywords": ["transversal", "parallel lines", "alternate interior angles", "proof"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nAn architect is designing a roof truss where two wooden beams AB and CD are parallel to each other, and a transversal support beam EF intersects them at points P and Q respectively. If ∠APQ = 50°:\n(i) Find the measure of ∠PQD.\n(ii) Find the measure of ∠BPQ.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) ∠PQD = 50° because they are alternate interior angles formed by parallel lines AB and CD cut by transversal EF.\n(ii) ∠BPQ = 180° - 50° = 130° because ∠APQ and ∠BPQ form a linear pair on line AB.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel lines", "alternate interior angles", "linear pair", "50 degrees", "130 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are always:", "options": ["Equal", "Complementary", "Supplementary", "Unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. Theorem 6.1 states that if two lines intersect, the vertically opposite angles are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite", "intersect", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The sum of all angles formed around a single point is ___ degrees.", "options": ["90", "180", "270", "360"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "360. The sum of all angles formed around a fixed point is 360 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum", "around a point", "360 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of corresponding angles is:", "options": ["Equal", "Supplementary", "Complementary", "Different"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. According to the corresponding angles axiom, if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, each pair of corresponding angles is equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["transversal", "parallel lines", "corresponding angles", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the measure of an angle which is equal to its complement.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the measure of the angle be x. Its complement is also x. Since complementary angles sum to 90 degrees, x + x = 90 implies 2x = 90, so x = 45 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["complement", "equal", "45 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, side BC is produced to D. If angle ABC = 50 degrees and angle BAC = 60 degrees, find the measure of exterior angle ACD.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the exterior angle theorem, the measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of its two interior opposite angles. Thus, angle ACD = angle ABC + angle BAC = 50 + 60 = 110 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle theorem", "triangle", "110 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Two lines AB and CD intersect at O. If angle AOC + angle BOE = 70 degrees and angle BOD = 40 degrees, find angle BOE and reflex angle COE.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given angle BOD = 40 degrees, so angle AOC = 40 degrees (vertically opposite angles). Given angle AOC + angle BOE = 70 degrees, so 40 + angle BOE = 70 implies angle BOE = 30 degrees. Since AB is a straight line, angle AOC + angle COE + angle BOE = 180 degrees, so 40 + angle COE + 30 = 180, giving angle COE = 110 degrees. Reflex angle COE = 360 - 110 = 250 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["intersecting lines", "vertically opposite", "reflex angle", "linear pair"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): Lines that are parallel to the same line are parallel to each other. Reason (R): Theorem 6.6 states that if line l || m and m || n, then l || n.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A, as it directly states Theorem 6.6.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel lines", "Theorem 6.6", "same line"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Match Column I with Column II: Column I: 1. Complementary angles, 2. Supplementary angles, 3. Linear pair, 4. Vertically opposite angles. Column II: A. Sum is 180 degrees, B. Sum is 90 degrees, C. Formed by two intersecting lines, D. Adjacent supplementary angles.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C. Complementary angles sum to 90 degrees, supplementary angles sum to 180 degrees, linear pair is adjacent supplementary angles, and vertically opposite angles are formed by intersecting lines.", "rubric_keywords": ["complementary", "supplementary", "linear pair", "vertically opposite"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180 degrees.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Theorem 6.7: Let triangle be PQR. Through P, draw a line XY parallel to QR. Since XY || QR and PQ is a transversal, alternate interior angles are equal: angle XPQ = angle PQR. Similarly, angle YPR = angle PRQ. Since XY is a straight line, angle XPQ + angle QPR + angle YPR = 180 degrees. Substituting the alternate angles gives angle PQR + angle QPR + angle PRQ = 180 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum of angles of a triangle", "parallel line", "Theorem 6.7", "proof"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Two straight pathways AB and CD in a garden are parallel to each other. A transversal path EF intersects AB at G and CD at H. If angle AGH = (3x + 15) degrees and angle GHD = (2x + 25) degrees, find: (i) the value of x, and (ii) the measure of angle AGH.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Since AB || CD and EF is a transversal, the alternate interior angles angle AGH and angle GHD are equal. Therefore, 3x + 15 = 2x + 25, which gives x = 10. (ii) Substituting x = 10 into angle AGH = 3(10) + 15 = 45 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel lines", "transversal", "alternate interior angles", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are:", "options": ["Equal", "Complementary", "Supplementary", "Unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "If two lines intersect, the vertically opposite angles formed are equal. This is established by the Vertically Opposite Angles Theorem.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite", "equal", "intersecting lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of alternate interior angles is:", "options": ["Equal", "Supplementary", "Complementary", "Unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "According to the Alternate Interior Angles Axiom, if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, each pair of alternate interior angles is equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["transversal", "parallel lines", "alternate interior angles", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The sum of the three interior angles of a triangle is ___ degrees.", "options": ["90", "180", "270", "360"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "180. The Angle Sum Property of a triangle states that the sum of all interior angles of a triangle is always 180°.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum", "triangle", "180"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle PQR, if angle P = 60° and angle Q = 50°, find the measure of angle R.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the Angle Sum Property of a triangle, angle P + angle Q + angle R = 180°. Substituting the given values: 60° + 50° + angle R = 180° => 110° + angle R = 180° => angle R = 180° - 110° = 70°.", "rubric_keywords": ["Angle Sum Property", "180", "70"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a side of a triangle is produced, then the exterior angle so formed is equal to the sum of the two interior _____ angles.", "options": ["opposite", "adjacent", "complementary", "supplementary"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "opposite. By the Exterior Angle Theorem, if a side of a triangle is produced, the exterior angle equals the sum of its two interior opposite angles.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle", "interior opposite", "theorem"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Two lines AB and CD are parallel, and a transversal EF intersects them at G and H respectively. If the interior angles on the same side of the transversal are (3x - 10)° and (2x + 20)°, find the value of x.", "options": [], "variables": {"x": [1, 50]}, "answer_key": "Co-interior angles (interior angles on the same side of the transversal) are supplementary when lines are parallel. Therefore, (3x - 10) + (2x + 20) = 180. Simplifying: 5x + 10 = 180 => 5x = 170 => x = 34.", "rubric_keywords": ["co-interior angles", "supplementary", "180", "34"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two lines are perpendicular to the same line, they are perpendicular to each other. Reason (R): Lines which are parallel to the same line are parallel to each other.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false and R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A is false because if two lines are perpendicular to the same line, they are parallel to each other, not perpendicular. R is true as it states a standard geometric theorem. Therefore, A is false and R is true.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "parallel", "perpendicular"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the geometric terms in Column I with their definitions/properties in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Complementary angles\n2. Linear pair\n3. Vertically opposite angles\n4. Parallel lines\nColumn II:\nA. Two adjacent angles whose sum is 180°\nB. Non-intersecting lines in the same plane\nC. Two angles whose sum is 90°\nD. Angles formed by two intersecting lines sharing no common arm", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct match: 1-C (Complementary angles sum to 90°), 2-A (Linear pair angles sum to 180°), 3-D (Vertically opposite angles formed by intersecting lines), 4-B (Parallel lines do not intersect). Hence, Option 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["complementary", "linear pair", "vertically opposite", "parallel lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°. Using this result, find the angles of a triangle if they are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Proof: Draw a line XY parallel to BC passing through A. Then angle XAB + angle B = 180° and angle XAB + angle BAC + angle CAY = 180° using alternate interior angles. Thus angle A + angle B + angle C = 180°. Calculation: Let angles be 2x, 3x, 4x. 2x + 3x + 4x = 180° => 9x = 180° => x = 20°. The angles are 40°, 60°, and 80°.", "rubric_keywords": ["proof", "angle sum property", "ratio", "40", "60", "80"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nRailway tracks are constructed using two parallel steel rails. A signal pole is installed across the tracks such that it acts as a transversal intersecting the two rails at distinct points. If one of the corresponding angles formed by the signal pole and the rails measures 65°:\n(i) What is the measure of the alternate interior angle corresponding to it?\n(ii) Find the measure of the consecutive interior angle on the same side of the transversal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) The measure of the alternate interior angle is 65° because alternate interior angles between parallel lines are equal.\n(ii) The consecutive interior (co-interior) angles are supplementary. Therefore, the measure is 180° - 65° = 115°.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel rails", "transversal", "65", "115", "supplementary"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are:", "options": ["Equal", "Unequal", "Complementary", "Supplementary"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. According to Theorem 6.1, if two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite angles", "equal", "Theorem 6.1"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of corresponding angles is:", "options": ["Equal", "Supplementary", "Complementary", "Unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. According to Axiom 6.3, if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of corresponding angles is equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["transversal", "parallel lines", "corresponding angles", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The sum of all angles formed around a single point is equal to ___ degrees.", "options": ["180", "270", "360", "90"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "360. The sum of all angles around a point forms a complete angle, which measures 360 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum of angles", "point", "360 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Find the measure of an angle which is equal to its complement.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the required angle be x. Its complement is also x. Since complementary angles sum to 90 degrees, x + x = 90 degrees => 2x = 90 degrees => x = 45 degrees. Thus, the required angle is 45 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["complementary", "45 degrees", "sum is 90"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If two supplementary angles are in the ratio 2:3, find the measures of the angles.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 2x and 3x. Since they are supplementary, 2x + 3x = 180 degrees => 5x = 180 degrees => x = 36 degrees. Therefore, the angles are 2(36) = 72 degrees and 3(36) = 108 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["supplementary", "ratio", "72 degrees", "108 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the angle property in Column I with its correct definition/result in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Vertically opposite angles\n2. Linear pair angles\n3. Interior angles on the same side of transversal for parallel lines\n4. Alternate interior angles for parallel lines\nColumn II:\nA. Supplementary (Sum 180 degrees)\nB. Equal when lines are parallel\nC. Equal when two lines intersect\nD. Complementary (Sum 90 degrees)", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-A, 4-B", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Match: 1-C (Vertically opposite angles are equal when lines intersect), 2-A (Linear pair angles are supplementary, sum 180 degrees), 3-A (Interior angles on the same side of transversal are supplementary), 4-B (Alternate interior angles are equal when lines are parallel). Option 1-C, 2-A, 3-A, 4-B is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite", "linear pair", "transversal", "alternate interior", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, if angle A = 50 degrees and the exterior angle formed by producing side BC to D is 120 degrees, find angle B and angle C.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By exterior angle property, the measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of its two interior opposite angles. Exterior angle at C = angle A + angle B => 120 degrees = 50 degrees + angle B => angle B = 70 degrees. Using the angle sum property or linear pair at C, angle C = 180 degrees - 120 degrees = 60 degrees. Thus, angle B = 70 degrees and angle C = 60 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle property", "angle B = 70", "angle C = 60", "triangle angle sum"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180 degrees.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given a triangle ABC. To prove: angle A + angle B + angle C = 180 degrees. Construction: Draw a line XY parallel to BC passing through vertex A. Proof: Since XY is parallel to BC and AB is a transversal, alternate interior angles are equal: angle XAB = angle B. Similarly, taking AC as a transversal, angle YAC = angle C. Also, angle XAB + angle BAC + angle YAC = 180 degrees (angles on a straight line). Substituting the equal angles, angle B + angle BAC + angle C = 180 degrees. Hence proved.", "rubric_keywords": ["construction", "parallel line", "alternate interior angles", "straight line", "180 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, then interior angles on the same side of the transversal are supplementary.\nReason (R): The sum of interior angles on the same side of a transversal intersecting parallel lines is always 180 degrees.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "A is true but R is false", "A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. Theorem 6.4 states that if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of interior angles on the same side of the transversal is supplementary, meaning their sum is 180 degrees. Therefore, R correctly explains A.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion and reason", "supplementary", "parallel lines", "correct explanation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the following scenario and answer the questions below:\nA carpenter is designing a wooden roof truss. Two main beams AB and CD are placed parallel to each other, crossed by a support beam EF acting as a transversal line making an angle of 75 degrees with beam AB.\n(i) Find the corresponding angle formed with beam CD.\n(ii) Find the interior angle on the same side of the transversal with the 75-degree angle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Since beams AB and CD are parallel and EF is a transversal, corresponding angles are equal. Therefore, the corresponding angle with beam CD is 75 degrees.\n(ii) Interior angles on the same side of the transversal are supplementary. Thus, the interior angle is 180 degrees - 75 degrees = 105 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel beams", "corresponding angles", "75 degrees", "supplementary", "105 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are:", "options": ["Equal", "Complementary", "Supplementary", "Adjacent"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. By the vertically opposite angles theorem, if two lines intersect, the vertically opposite angles formed are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite angles", "intersecting lines", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "An angle is equal to five times its complement. What is the measure of the angle?", "options": ["75°", "60°", "15°", "30°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "75°. Let the angle be x. Its complement is (90° - x). Given x = 5(90° - x) => x = 450° - 5x => 6x = 450° => x = 75°.", "rubric_keywords": ["complementary angles", "angle", "equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Lines which are parallel to the same line are ___ to each other.", "options": ["parallel", "perpendicular", "intersecting", "skew"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "parallel. According to the lines parallel to the same line theorem (Theorem 6.6), lines that are parallel to the same line are parallel to each other.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel lines", "same line", "theorem"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the measure of an angle which is equal to its supplement.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angle be x. Since it is equal to its supplement, x + x = 180° => 2x = 180° => x = 90°. The measure of the angle is 90°.", "rubric_keywords": ["supplementary angles", "90 degrees", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In \\triangle PQR, side QP is produced to point S such that \\angle PQR = 65^\\circ and \\angle QPR = 45^\\circ. Find the exterior angle \\angle RQS.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the exterior angle property of a triangle, the exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of the two opposite interior angles. Here, \\angle RQS = \\angle PQR + \\angle QPR = 65^\\circ + 45^\\circ = 110^\\circ.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle", "triangle", "sum of interior opposite angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In the given figure, if AB \\parallel CD, \\angle APQ = 50^\\circ and \\angle PRD = 127^\\circ, find the values of x and y.", "options": [], "variables": {"angle_apq": 50, "angle_prd": 127}, "answer_key": "Given AB \\parallel CD and PQ is a transversal, so \\angle PQR = \\angle APQ = 50^\\circ (Alternate interior angles). Therefore, x = 50°. Also, \\angle APR = \\angle PRD = 127^\\circ (Alternate interior angles). Since \\angle APR = \\angle APQ + \\angle QPR, we have 50^\\circ + y = 127^\\circ => y = 127° - 50° = 77°. Thus, x = 50° and y = 77°.", "rubric_keywords": ["alternate interior angles", "parallel lines", "transversal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: A triangle ABC. To prove: \\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^\\circ. Construction: Draw a line XY parallel to BC passing through vertex A. Proof: Since XY is a line, \\angle XAB + \\angle BAC + \\angle CAY = 180^\\circ (Linear pair). Since XY || BC and AB, AC are transversals, \\angle XAB = \\angle B (Alternate interior angles) and \\angle CAY = \\angle C (Alternate interior angles). Substituting these into the linear pair equation gives \\angle B + \\angle BAC + \\angle C = 180^\\circ, i.e., \\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^\\circ. Hence proved.", "rubric_keywords": ["construction", "alternate interior angles", "linear pair", "sum of angles of a triangle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two interior angles on the same side of a transversal intersecting two parallel lines are in the ratio 2:3, then the smaller angle is 72°. Reason (R): Sum of interior angles on the same side of a transversal with parallel lines is 180°.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). Explanation: Let the angles be 2x and 3x. By (R), 2x + 3x = 180° => 5x = 180° => x = 36°. Smaller angle = 2(36°) = 72°. Thus (A) is true and derived correctly using (R).", "rubric_keywords": ["co-interior angles", "parallel lines", "ratio"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the angle relation in Column I with its property in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Vertically opposite angles\n2. Angles forming a linear pair\n3. Corresponding angles\n4. Alternate exterior angles\nColumn II:\nA. Sum is 180°\nB. Equal when lines are parallel\nC. Always equal\nD. Equal when lines are parallel (exterior)", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D. Explanation: Vertically opposite angles are always equal (1-C). Angles forming a linear pair sum to 180° (2-A). Corresponding angles are equal when lines are parallel (3-B). Alternate exterior angles are equal when lines are parallel (4-D).", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite", "linear pair", "corresponding angles", "alternate exterior angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nIn a park, three straight walking paths are laid out such that path l is parallel to path m, and path n is a transversal intersecting both paths at angles forming a geometric pattern. The angle made by path n with path l at the upper intersection is given as (3x - 15)^\\circ and the corresponding angle at path m is (2x + 25)^\\circ.\n(i) Find the value of x. (2 marks)\n(ii) Find the measure of the angles formed at the intersections. (2 marks)", "options": [], "variables": {"x": 40}, "answer_key": "(i) Since l \\parallel m and n is a transversal, corresponding angles are equal. Therefore, 3x - 15 = 2x + 25 \\Rightarrow x = 40. (2 marks)\n(ii) Substituting x = 40 into either expression: 3(40) - 15 = 120 - 15 = 105^\\circ. Thus, the measure of each corresponding angle is 105^\\circ. (2 marks)", "rubric_keywords": ["corresponding angles", "parallel paths", "linear equation", "transversal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If two lines intersect each other, then the vertically opposite angles are:", "options": ["Equal", "Complementary", "Supplementary", "Adjacent"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. According to Theorem 6.1, if two lines intersect, the vertically opposite angles are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite angles", "intersecting lines", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The sum of all angles around a point is ___ degrees.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "360. A complete revolution around a point forms an angle of 360 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum", "point", "360 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the angle relationships in Column I with their measure in Column II.\nColumn I: 1. Complementary angles, 2. Supplementary angles, 3. Linear pair angles, 4. Angles around a point.\nColumn II: A. 90°, B. 180°, C. 360°.", "options": ["1-A, 2-B, 3-B, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-A (Complementary angles sum to 90°), 2-B (Supplementary angles sum to 180°), 3-B (Linear pair angles sum to 180°), 4-C (Angles around a point sum to 360°).", "rubric_keywords": ["complementary", "supplementary", "linear pair", "sum"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of alternate interior angles is:", "options": ["Equal", "Supplementary", "Complementary", "Unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. According to the Alternate Interior Angles Axiom (Theorem 6.2), if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of alternate interior angles is equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["transversal", "parallel lines", "alternate interior angles", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two lines are parallel to the same line, they are parallel to each other.\nReason (R): Lines that are parallel to the same line are also parallel to each other.\nChoose the correct option:", "options": ["(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "(c) A is true but R is false.", "(d) A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. Theorem 6.6 states that lines which are parallel to the same line are parallel to each other.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel lines", "Assertion", "Reason", "Theorem 6.6"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Find the measure of the complement of an angle measuring 35°.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The sum of complementary angles is 90°. Complement = 90° - 35° = 55°.", "rubric_keywords": ["complement", "90 degrees", "35 degrees", "55 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, side BC is produced to a point D. If angle ACD = 110° and angle B = 50°, find angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the Exterior Angle Theorem, if a side of a triangle is produced, then the exterior angle so formed is equal to the sum of the two interior opposite angles. Therefore, angle ACD = angle A + angle B. Substituting values: 110° = angle A + 50°, which gives angle A = 110° - 50° = 60°.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle theorem", "interior opposite angles", "60 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "In the given figure, lines AB and CD intersect at O. If angle AOC + angle BOE = 70° and angle BOD = 40°, find angle BOE and reflex angle COE.", "options": [], "variables": {"angle_sum": 70, "angle_bod": 40}, "answer_key": "Given angle BOD = 40°, then angle AOC = 40° (vertically opposite angles). Given angle AOC + angle BOE = 70°, substituting angle AOC = 40° gives 40° + angle BOE = 70°, so angle BOE = 30°. Straight line AB has angle sum 180°: angle AOC + angle COE + angle BOE = 180° => 40° + angle COE + 30° = 180° => angle COE = 110°. Reflex angle COE = 360° - 110° = 250°.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite", "linear pair", "reflex angle", "30 degrees", "250 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "State and prove the Angle Sum Property of a triangle (Theorem 6.7: The sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Theorem 6.7: The sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°. Proof: Consider triangle PQR. Through vertex P, draw a line XY parallel to QR. Since XY is a line, the angles on line XY at point P form a linear pair / straight angle sum: angle XPQ + angle QPR + angle YPR = 180°. Since XY is parallel to QR and PQ, PR are transversals, alternate interior angles are equal: angle XPQ = angle PQR and angle YPR = angle PRQ. Substituting these into the equation gives angle PQR + angle QPR + angle PRQ = 180°. Hence, the sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum of angles of a triangle", "180 degrees", "parallel line", "alternate interior angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the following:\nA civil engineer designs a road layout where two parallel roads, Road 1 (l1) and Road 2 (l2), are intersected by a transversal bridge road (t). The corresponding angles made by the bridge with Road 1 and Road 2 are given as (3x + 15)° and (4x - 5)° respectively.\n(i) Find the value of x.\n(ii) Find the measure of these corresponding angles.\n(iii) Find the measure of the consecutive interior angle on the same side of the transversal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Since l1 is parallel to l2 and t is a transversal, corresponding angles are equal: 3x + 15 = 4x - 5 => x = 20.\n(ii) Measure of corresponding angles = 3(20) + 15 = 75° (and 4(20) - 5 = 75°).\n(iii) The consecutive interior angles on the same side of the transversal are supplementary to the angle on the line, so 180° - 75° = 105°.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel lines", "corresponding angles", "x = 20", "75 degrees", "105 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If two supplementary angles are in the ratio 2:3, then the measures of the angles are:", "options": ["72° and 108°", "60° and 120°", "50° and 130°", "80° and 100°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 2x and 3x. Since they are supplementary, 2x + 3x = 180° => 5x = 180° => x = 36°. Therefore, the angles are 2(36°) = 72° and 3(36°) = 108°. Option A is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["supplementary angles", "ratio", "180 degrees", "72 and 108"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In the given figure, if line AB || CD, CD || EF and y : z = 3 : 7, then the value of x is:", "options": ["126°", "108°", "140°", "112°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since AB || CD and CD || EF, therefore AB || EF. Thus, x = z (alternate interior angles). Also, x + y = 180° (co-interior angles). Let y = 3k and z = 7k, so x = 7k. Then 7k + 3k = 180° => 10k = 180° => k = 18°. Thus, x = 7 × 18° = 126°.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel lines", "alternate interior angles", "co-interior angles", "126 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If a ray stands on a line, then the sum of two adjacent angles so formed is ___.", "options": ["90°", "180°", "360°", "270°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "180° (or a straight angle). According to the Linear Pair Axiom, if a ray stands on a line, the sum of two adjacent angles formed is equal to 180°.", "rubric_keywords": ["Linear Pair Axiom", "180 degrees", "adjacent angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "An angle is equal to one-fifth of its supplement. Find its measure.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the measure of the angle be x. Its supplement is 180° - x. According to the question, x = (1/5)(180° - x). Multiplying both sides by 5 gives 5x = 180° - x => 6x = 180° => x = 30°. Thus, the measure of the angle is 30°.", "rubric_keywords": ["supplement", "equation", "30 degrees", "linear equation"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle PQR, side QP is produced to point S such that ray QT is the bisector of angle RQS. If angle P = 60° and angle PRQ = 40°, find angle TQR and angle PQR.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "By the exterior angle theorem for triangle PQR, the exterior angle angle RQS = angle P + angle PRQ = 60° + 40° = 100°. Since QT bisects angle RQS, angle TQR = (1/2) * angle RQS = (1/2) * 100° = 50°. Also, angle PQR = 180° - angle RQS = 180° - 100° = 80°.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle theorem", "angle bisector", "50 degrees", "80 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Lines AB and CD intersect at point O. If angle AOC + angle BOE = 70° and angle BOD = 40°, find angle BOE and reflex angle COE.", "options": [], "variables": {"angle_sum": 70, "angle_bod": 40}, "answer_key": "Given that lines AB and CD intersect at O. Therefore, angle AOC = angle BOD = 40° (vertically opposite angles). Given angle AOC + angle BOE = 70° => 40° + angle BOE = 70° => angle BOE = 30° θ. Since AOB is a straight line, angle AOC + angle COE + angle BOE = 180° => 40° + angle COE + 30° = 180° => angle COE = 110°. Therefore, reflex angle COE = 360° - 110° = 250°.", "rubric_keywords": ["vertically opposite angles", "straight line", "reflex angle", "30 degrees", "250 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, then each pair of corresponding angles is equal. Reason (R): Corresponding angles lie on the same side of the transversal and are in the same relative position at each intersection.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, and (R) is the correct explanation of (A) as it defines the spatial orientation of corresponding angles which leads to the corresponding angles axiom.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion and Reason", "corresponding angles axiom", "transversal", "parallel lines"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the geometric terms in Column I with their correct definitions/properties in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Complementary angles\n2. Supplementary angles\n3. Vertically opposite angles\n4. Linear pair\nColumn II:\nA. Sum of two angles is 180°\nB. Equal angles formed when two lines intersect\nC. Sum of two adjacent angles is 180° forming a line\nD. Sum of two angles is 90°", "options": ["1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C", "1-A, 2-D, 3-C, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-D (Complementary angles sum to 90°), 2-A (Supplementary angles sum to 180°), 3-B (Vertically opposite angles are equal when two lines intersect), 4-C (Linear pair consists of adjacent angles whose non-common arms form a line, sum = 180°). Correct option is 1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["Complementary", "Supplementary", "Vertically opposite", "Linear pair"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Case Study: In an architecture design for a bridge structure, two parallel steel girders PQ and RS are connected by diagonal crossbars. A transverse support beam XY intersects PQ at point L and RS at point M such that angle XLP = 120°.\n(i) Find the measure of angle YLP.\n(ii) Find the measure of angle LMR.\n(iii) If another crossbar forms an interior angle of 60° with RS, what is its corresponding alternate interior angle?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) angle YLP = 180° - 120° = 60° (linear pair on straight line XY). (ii) angle LMR = angle XLP = 120° (corresponding angles since PQ || RS). (iii) The alternate interior angle to 60° is equal to 60° by the alternate interior angles theorem for parallel lines.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallel girders", "linear pair", "corresponding angles", "case study"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Lines and Angles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Prove that if a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then each pair of alternate interior angles is equal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let lines AB and CD be parallel, and let transversal EF intersect them at points G and H respectively. Let angle 1 and angle 2 be a pair of alternate interior angles. By the corresponding angles axiom, corresponding angles are equal, so angle 3 = angle 2 where angle 3 is vertically opposite to angle 1. Since vertically opposite angles are equal (angle 1 = angle 3), by transitivity angle 1 = angle 2. Hence, alternate interior angles are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["transversal", "parallel lines", "alternate interior angles", "proof"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is not a criterion for congruence of triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "SSA", "ASA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B. SSA (Side-Side-Angle) is not a valid criterion for triangle congruence because two triangles can have two sides and a non-included angle equal without being congruent.", "rubric_keywords": ["congruence criteria", "SSA", "SAS", "ASA", "SSS"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are ___.", "options": ["equal", "complementary", "supplementary", "unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "equal. According to Theorem 7.2, angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "angles opposite to equal sides", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, if AB = 5 cm and AC = 8 cm, then the length of the third side BC cannot be:", "options": ["3 cm", "4 cm", "10 cm", "14 cm"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option D. According to the triangle inequality property, the sum of any two sides of a triangle is greater than the third side, and their difference is less than the third side. Here, AC - AB < BC < AC + AB implies 8 - 5 < BC < 8 + 5 implies 3 < BC < 13. Thus, 14 cm is not a possible length.", "rubric_keywords": ["triangle inequality", "sum of two sides", "difference of sides"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, if AB = AC and angle B = 50 degrees, find the measure of angle C and angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since AB = AC, triangle ABC is an isosceles triangle. Therefore, angles opposite to equal sides are equal, so angle C = angle B = 50 degrees. Using the angle sum property of a triangle: angle A + angle B + angle C = 180 degrees implies angle A + 50 degrees + 50 degrees = 180 degrees implies angle A = 180 degrees - 100 degrees = 80 degrees. Final Answer: angle C = 50 degrees, angle A = 80 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "angles opposite to equal sides", "angle sum property", "80 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "AD is an altitude of an isosceles triangle ABC in which AB = AC. Show that AD bisects BC.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "In right triangles ABD and ACD: 1. Hypotenuse AB = AC (Given). 2. Side AD = AD (Common). 3. Angle ADB = angle ADC = 90 degrees (AD is altitude). By RHS congruence criterion, triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACD. Therefore, BD = CD by CPCT, which means AD bisects BC.", "rubric_keywords": ["altitude", "RHS congruence", "CPCT", "bisects BC"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Line l is the bisector of an angle A and B is any point on l. BP and BQ are perpendiculars from B to the arms of angle A. Show that triangle APB is congruent to triangle AQB.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "In triangle APB and triangle AQB: 1. angle PAB = angle QAB (l is the bisector of angle A). 2. angle APB = angle AQB = 90 degrees (Given perpendiculars). 3. Side AB = AB (Common side). By AAS congruence rule, triangle APB is congruent to triangle AQB.", "rubric_keywords": ["angle bisector", "perpendicular", "AAS congruence", "common side"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that if two angles of a triangle are equal, then sides opposite to them are also equal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: In triangle ABC, angle B = angle C. To prove: AB = AC. Construction: Draw the bisector AD of angle A meeting BC at D. Proof: In triangle ABD and triangle ACD, angle BAD = angle CAD (by construction), angle B = angle C (given), and side AD = AD (common). By AAS congruence criterion, triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACD. Therefore, AB = AC (by CPCT).", "rubric_keywords": ["theorem proof", "angle bisector construction", "AAS congruence", "CPCT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the congruence criteria in Column I with their corresponding valid descriptions in Column II. Column I: 1. SAS, 2. ASA, 3. SSS, 4. RHS. Column II: A. Three sides of one triangle are equal to three sides of another triangle. B. Two sides and the included angle of one triangle are equal to two sides and the included angle of another triangle. C. Two angles and the included side of one triangle are equal to two angles and the included side of another triangle. D. Hypotenuse and one side of a right triangle are equal to hypotenuse and one side of another right triangle.", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D. SAS matches with two sides and included angle; ASA matches with two angles and included side; SSS matches with three sides; RHS matches with right-angled triangle hypotenuse and side.", "rubric_keywords": ["SAS", "ASA", "SSS", "RHS", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the altitude from one vertex of a triangle bisects the opposite side, then the triangle is isosceles. Reason (R): Triangles formed by the altitude are congruent by SAS congruence criterion.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A. Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). When an altitude bisects the opposite side, the two triangles formed share a common side (altitude), have equal included angles (90 degrees each), and have equal adjacent side segments, making them congruent by SAS. Hence, the other two sides are equal by CPCT, making the triangle isosceles.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion Reason", "altitude", "SAS congruence", "isosceles triangle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions: A park has a triangular flower bed shaped like triangle PQR where PQ = PR. A gardener wants to install a water sprinkler at point S on side QR such that PS is perpendicular to QR. (i) State the congruence rule that proves triangle PQS is congruent to triangle PRS. (ii) If angle PQS = 40 degrees, find angle QPS.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) RHS congruence criterion (since hypotenuse PQ = PR, common side PS = PS, and angle PSQ = angle PSR = 90 degrees). Alternatively, SAS can also be used. (ii) In right triangle PQS, angle PSQ = 90 degrees and angle PQS = 40 degrees. Using angle sum property: angle QPS = 180 degrees - (90 degrees + 40 degrees) = 50 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "RHS congruence", "angle sum property", "50 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is not a criterion for congruence of triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "SSA", "ASA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "SSA is not a valid criterion for congruence of triangles. The valid criteria are SAS, ASA, AAS, SSS, and RHS.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSA", "congruence criteria", "SAS", "SSS"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are ___ .", "options": ["equal", "complementary", "supplementary", "unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. Theorem states that angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal to each other.", "rubric_keywords": ["equal", "isosceles triangle", "angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, if AB = AC and angle B = 50 degrees, find angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AB = AC, so angle C = angle B = 50 degrees (angles opposite to equal sides are equal). In triangle ABC, by angle sum property, angle A + angle B + angle C = 180 degrees. Therefore, angle A + 50 + 50 = 180, which gives angle A = 80 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["80 degrees", "isosceles triangle property", "angle sum property"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "State the Triangle Inequality Theorem regarding the sum of any two sides of a triangle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "The Triangle Inequality Theorem states that the sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle is always greater than the length of the third side. For example, in triangle ABC, AB + BC > AC.", "rubric_keywords": ["triangle inequality", "sum of two sides", "third side"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle PQR, S is any point on the side QR. Show that PQ + QR + RP > 2PS.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Consider triangle PQS. By triangle inequality, PQ + QS > PS ... (1). Consider triangle PRS. By triangle inequality, PR + SR > PS ... (2). Adding (1) and (2): (PQ + PR) + (QS + SR) > 2PS. Since QS + SR = QR, we get PQ + PR + QR > 2PS.", "rubric_keywords": ["triangle inequality", "PS", "QS + SR = QR"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "AB is a line segment and line l is its perpendicular bisector. If a point P lies on l, show that P is equidistant from A and B.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let line l intersect AB at point C. In triangle PCA and triangle PCB: AC = BC (since C is the midpoint of AB), angle PCA = angle PCB = 90 degrees (since l is perpendicular to AB), and PC = PC (common side). By SAS congruence criterion, triangle PCA is congruent to triangle PCB. Therefore, PA = PB by CPCTC. Hence, P is equidistant from A and B.", "rubric_keywords": ["perpendicular bisector", "SAS congruence", "CPCTC", "equidistant"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two angles and a side of one triangle are equal to two angles and a side of another triangle, then the two triangles must be congruent. Reason (R): ASA congruence criterion states that two triangles are congruent if two angles and the included side of one triangle are equal to two angles and the included side of the other triangle.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(A) is false because the side must be specifically the included side between the two angles for standard congruence (though AAS works too, a general non-included side without condition is invalid). (R) is true as it correctly defines the ASA criterion. Therefore, (A) is false but (R) is true.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion is false", "Reason is true", "included side"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the triangle criteria or properties in Column I with their correct descriptions in Column II. Column I: 1. SSS Congruence, 2. SAS Congruence, 3. RHS Congruence, 4. Isosceles Triangle Property. Column II: A. Angles opposite to equal sides are equal, B. Three sides of one triangle are equal to three sides of another, C. Hypotenuse and one side of a right triangle are equal, D. Two sides and the included angle are equal.", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (SSS matches three sides), 2-D (SAS matches two sides and included angle), 3-C (RHS matches right triangle hypotenuse and side), 4-A (Isosceles triangle property matches angles opposite to equal sides). Correct code option: 1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A.", "rubric_keywords": ["1-B", "2-D", "3-C", "4-A"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer: In two triangular plots representing triangle ABC and triangle PQR, AB = PQ, AC = PR, and median AM = PN. (i) Prove triangle ABM is congruent to triangle PQN. (ii) Hence, prove triangle ABC is congruent to triangle PQR.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Since AM and PN are medians, BM = 1/2 BC = 1/2 QR = QN. In triangle ABM and triangle PQN, AB = PQ, BM = QN, and AM = PN. By SSS congruence, triangle ABM is congruent to triangle PQN, so angle B = angle Q (CPCTC). (ii) In triangle ABC and triangle PQR, AB = PQ, angle B = angle Q, and BC = QR. By SAS congruence criterion, triangle ABC is congruent to triangle PQR.", "rubric_keywords": ["median", "SSS congruence", "SAS congruence", "CPCTC"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "D is a point on side BC of triangle ABC such that AD = AC. Show that AB > AD.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AD = AC. In triangle ADC, since AD = AC, angle ADC = angle ACD (angles opposite to equal sides are equal). Now, angle ADC is an exterior angle for triangle ABD, so angle ADC = angle B + angle BAD. Therefore, angle ADC > angle B. Since angle ACD = angle ADC, we have angle ACB > angle B. In triangle ABC, the side opposite to angle ACB is AB, and the side opposite to angle B is AC. Since angle ACB > angle B, we get AB > AC. But AC = AD, hence AB > AD.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle property", "angles opposite to equal sides", "inequality", "AB > AD"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is NOT a valid criterion for congruence of triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "ASA", "SSA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "SSA. Explanation: SSA (Side-Side-Angle) or ASS is not a valid criterion for triangle congruence because two triangles can have two sides and a non-included angle equal while having different shapes and sizes.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSA", "congruence criterion", "not valid", "non-included angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In an isosceles triangle ABC with AB = AC, if angle B = 50 degrees, find angle C and angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since AB = AC, the angles opposite to these equal sides are also equal, so angle C = angle B = 50 degrees. By the angle sum property of a triangle, angle A = 180 - (50 + 50) = 180 - 100 = 80 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "angles opposite to equal sides", "angle sum property", "80 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are ___.", "options": ["equal", "complementary", "supplementary", "unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "equal. Theorem states that angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["equal", "isosceles triangle", "sides"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two sides and any one angle of a triangle are equal to two sides and an angle of another triangle, the triangles are congruent. Reason (R): SAS congruence rule requires the equal angle to be included between the two equal sides.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A is false but R is true. Assertion is incorrect because the angle must be the included angle between the two sides (SAS rule); otherwise, triangles may not be congruent.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion is false", "Reason is true", "included angle", "SAS criterion"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the congruence criteria in Column I with their descriptions in Column II:\nColumn I: 1. SSS, 2. SAS, 3. ASA, 4. RHS\nColumn II: A. Two sides and included angle are equal, B. Three sides are equal, C. Hypotenuse and one side are equal in right triangles, D. Two angles and included side are equal.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C. SSS matches with three sides equal, SAS matches with two sides and included angle, ASA matches with two angles and included side, and RHS matches with right-angled triangles having hypotenuse and one side equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSS", "SAS", "ASA", "RHS", "Column I", "Column II"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Line l is the perpendicular bisector of line segment AB. If a point P lies on line l, show that P is equidistant from A and B.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let M be the mid-point of AB where line l intersects AB. In triangles PMA and PMB: PM = PM (common side), angle PMA = angle PMB = 90 degrees (since l is perpendicular bisector), AM = BM (M is mid-point). By SAS congruence criterion, triangle PMA is congruent to triangle PMB. Therefore, PA = PB (corresponding parts of congruent triangles, CPCT), showing P is equidistant from A and B.", "rubric_keywords": ["perpendicular bisector", "SAS congruence", "CPCT", "equidistant"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, AB = AC and the exterior angle formed by extending side BC to D measures 110 degrees. Find all the interior angles of triangle ABC.", "options": [], "variables": {"exterior_angle": 110}, "answer_key": "The exterior angle at vertex C is 110 degrees. Therefore, angle ACB = 180 - 110 = 70 degrees (linear pair). Since AB = AC, angle ABC = angle ACB = 70 degrees. The sum of angles in triangle ABC is 180 degrees, so angle A = 180 - (70 + 70) = 180 - 140 = 40 degrees. Thus, the interior angles are 40, 70, and 70 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["linear pair", "exterior angle property", "isosceles triangle", "40 degrees", "70 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: Triangle ABC with AB = AC. To prove: angle B = angle C. Construction: Draw the bisector AD of angle A meeting BC at D. Proof: In triangles ABD and ACD, AB = AC (given), angle BAD = angle CAD (by construction as AD bisects angle A), AD = AD (common side). By SAS congruence criterion, triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACD. Therefore, angle B = angle C by CPCT.", "rubric_keywords": ["Theorem proof", "angle bisector construction", "SAS congruence", "CPCT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In triangles ABC and PQR, AB = PQ, BC = QR, and angle B = angle Q. By which congruence criterion are the two triangles congruent?", "options": ["SSS", "SAS", "ASA", "RHS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "SAS. Explanation: Two sides (AB, BC) and the included angle (angle B) of triangle ABC are equal to two sides (PQ, QR) and the included angle (angle Q) of triangle PQR, which satisfies the SAS (Side-Angle-Side) congruence criterion.", "rubric_keywords": ["SAS", "included angle", "congruence criterion"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nTwo triangular park plots in a city layout are designed such that in triangle PQR and triangle XYZ, PQ = XY, PR = XZ, and median PM = median XN.\n(i) Show that triangle PMQ is congruent to triangle XNY. (2 marks)\n(ii) Hence or otherwise, show that triangle PQR is congruent to triangle XYZ. (2 marks)", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Since M is the mid-point of QR, QM = QR / 2. Similarly N is the mid-point of YZ, YN = YZ / 2. Since QR = YZ, QM = YN. In triangles PMQ and XNY, PQ = XY, PM = XN, and QM = YN. By SSS congruence, triangle PMQ is congruent to triangle XNY, giving angle Q = angle Y. (ii) In triangles PQR and XYZ, PQ = XY, angle Q = angle Y, and QR = YZ. By SAS congruence criterion, triangle PQR is congruent to triangle XYZ.", "rubric_keywords": ["median", "SSS congruence", "SAS congruence", "CPCT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is NOT a criterion for congruence of two triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "SSA", "ASA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option B: SSA is not a valid criterion for triangle congruence because two sides and a non-included angle do not uniquely determine a triangle (the ambiguous case).", "rubric_keywords": ["SSA", "congruence criterion", "not valid", "non-included angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are ___.", "options": ["equal", "complementary", "supplementary", "perpendicular"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. Theorem: Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["equal", "isosceles triangle", "angles opposite"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In \\Delta ABC, if AB = AC and \\angle B = 50^\\circ, find \\angle C and \\angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since AB = AC, by the theorem 'angles opposite to equal sides are equal', \\angle C = \\angle B = 50^\\circ. Using the angle sum property of a triangle: \\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle A + 50^\\circ + 50^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle A = 80^\\circ. Final Answer: \\angle C = 50^\\circ, \\angle A = 80^\\circ.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles", "angle sum property", "50", "80"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In \\Delta PQR, if \\angle P = 70^\\circ and \\angle Q = 50^\\circ, which side of the triangle is the longest?", "options": ["PQ", "QR", "PR", "Cannot be determined"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "First find \\angle R = 180^\\circ - (70^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = 60^\\circ. The largest angle is \\angle P = 70^\\circ. The side opposite to the largest angle is the longest side, which is QR. Option B.", "rubric_keywords": ["longest side", "angle opposite", "QR", "70 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the Triangle Criteria in Column I with their correct descriptions in Column II:\\nColumn I: 1. SSS, 2. SAS, 3. ASA, 4. RHS\\nColumn II: A. Two sides and included angle, B. Three sides of one triangle equal to three sides of another, C. Right angle, hypotenuse and one side, D. Two angles and included side.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (SSS matches three sides), 2-A (SAS matches two sides and included angle), 3-D (ASA matches two angles and included side), 4-C (RHS matches right angle, hypotenuse, side). Correct option is 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSS", "SAS", "ASA", "RHS", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the altitude from one vertex of a triangle bisects the opposite side, then the triangle is isosceles.\\nReason (R): In an isosceles triangle, altitudes corresponding to equal sides are equal.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both (A) and (R) are true statements independently, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A). (A) can be proved by congruent triangles formed by the altitude (SAS criterion). Option B.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "isosceles", "altitude bisects opposite side"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Line l is the bisector of an angle \\angle A and B is any point on l. BP and BQ are perpendiculars from B to the arms of \\angle A. Show that \\Delta APB \\cong \\Delta AQB. Given that AP = 4 cm and \\angle PAB = 30^\\circ, find length AB.", "options": [], "variables": {"AP": 4, "angle": 30}, "answer_key": "In right-angled triangle \\Delta APB, \\cos(30^\\circ) = \\frac{AP}{AB}. Since \\cos(30^\\circ) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{4}{AB} \\implies AB = \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}} \\text{ cm or } 4.62 \\text{ cm}.", "rubric_keywords": ["cos 30", "AP / AB", "8/sqrt(3)", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "ABC is an isosceles triangle in which AB = AC. Side BA is produced to D such that AD = AB. Show that \\angle BCD is a right angle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AB = AC = AD. Let \\angle ACB = \\angle B = x and \\angle ACD = \\angle D = y. In \\Delta BCD, sum of angles is x + (x+y) + y = 180^\\circ \\implies 2(x+y) = 180^\\circ \\implies x+y = 90^\\circ. Thus \\angle BCD = 90^\\circ, which is a right angle.", "rubric_keywords": ["right angle", "isosceles", "AD = AB", "angle sum property"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that 'If two angles of a triangle are equal, then sides opposite to them are also equal.' Using this theorem, prove that an equilateral triangle is equiangular.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: State theorem proof using construction (angle bisector of \\angle A meeting BC at D). Prove \\Delta ABD \\cong \\Delta ACD by SAS/ASA, hence AB = AC. Step 2: For equilateral triangle, all three sides are equal, hence all pairs of opposite angles are equal, proving it is equiangular.", "rubric_keywords": ["theorem proof", "angle bisector", "congruence", "equilateral triangle", "equiangular"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\\nAn architect is designing a roof truss shaped like a triangle XYZ. The cross-beam ST is parallel to YZ intersecting XY at XY and XZ at T. Given that XS = XT and \\angle XST = 50^\\circ.\\n(i) Find \\angle X and \\angle XTS.\\n(ii) If S and T are midpoints of XY and XZ respectively, explain how corresponding angle relations demonstrate structural symmetry.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Since XS = XT, \\angle XTS = \\angle XST = 50^\\circ. Then \\angle X = 180^\\circ - (50^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = 80^\\circ. (ii) By SAS congruence or midpoint theorem properties, corresponding angles are equal, confirming structural symmetry.", "rubric_keywords": ["architect", "roof truss", "isosceles triangle", "80 degrees", "50 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is not a criterion for congruence of triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "SSA", "ASA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "SSA is not a valid criterion for triangle congruence. The valid criteria are SAS, ASA, SSS, and RHS.", "rubric_keywords": ["congruence criterion", "SSA", "SAS", "ASA", "SSS"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Angles opposite to equal sides of a triangle are ___.", "options": ["equal", "complementary", "supplementary", "perpendicular"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "equal. Theorem: Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["angles opposite", "equal sides", "isosceles triangle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC and triangle DEF, AB = DE, BC = EF, and angle B = angle E. By which congruence criterion are the two triangles congruent?", "options": ["SSS", "SAS", "ASA", "RHS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "SAS (Side-Angle-Side). Since two sides and the included angle of one triangle are equal to two sides and the included angle of another triangle, they are congruent by SAS.", "rubric_keywords": ["SAS congruence", "included angle", "triangle ABC", "triangle DEF"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In an isosceles triangle ABC with AB = AC, D and E are points on BC such that BD = CE. Show that AD = AE.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AB = AC, so angle ABC = angle ACB (angles opposite to equal sides). Consider triangles ABD and ACE. AB = AC, angle ABD = angle ACE, and BD = CE. By SAS congruence, triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACE. Therefore, AD = AE by CPCT.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "SAS congruence", "CPCT", "AD = AE"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If the bisector of an angle of a triangle also bisects the opposite side, prove that the triangle is isosceles.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let AD be the angle bisector of angle A in triangle ABC, meeting BC at D such that BD = DC. Extend AD to a point E such that AD = DE and join EC. Prove triangle ADB is congruent to triangle EDC using SAS. Then AB = EC and angle B = angle ECD. Since AD bisects angle A and angle B = angle C (or through angle-side relations), AB = AC, making triangle ABC isosceles.", "rubric_keywords": ["angle bisector", "isosceles triangle", "congruence", "sides equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Line l is the bisector of an angle A and B is any point on l. BP and BQ are perpendiculars from B to the arms of angle A. Show that triangle APB is congruent to triangle AQB and BP = BQ.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "In right triangles APB and AQB: angle APB = angle AQB = 90 degrees (perpendiculars), hypotenuse AB = hypotenuse AB (common), and angle PAB = angle QAB (since l is the bisector of angle A). Therefore, triangle APB is congruent to triangle AQB by AAS/RHS criterion. Thus, BP = BQ by corresponding parts of congruent triangles (CPCT).", "rubric_keywords": ["perpendicular bisector", "RHS congruence", "CPCT", "BP = BQ"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the triangle property in Column I with the correct theorem/result in Column II. Column I: 1. Angles opposite to equal sides, 2. Sides opposite to equal angles, 3. Sum of any two sides of a triangle. Column II: A. Equal sides, B. Greater than the third side, C. Equal angles.", "options": ["1-C, 2-A, 3-B", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B", "1-B, 2-A, 3-C", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-C (Angles opposite to equal sides are equal), 2-A (Sides opposite to equal angles are equal), 3-B (Sum of any two sides of a triangle is greater than the third side). Correct option code is 1-C, 2-A, 3-B.", "rubric_keywords": ["match the following", "triangle properties", "equal sides", "triangle inequality"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two angles and one side of one triangle are equal to two angles and the corresponding side of another triangle, the triangles are congruent. Reason (R): ASA congruence rule states that two triangles are congruent if two angles and the included side of one triangle are equal to two angles and the included side of the other triangle.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. The AAS theorem (derived from ASA) states that any two angles and a non-included side also lead to congruence, which falls under the general umbrella of angle-side criteria proven via ASA.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion and Reason", "ASA congruence", "AAS criterion", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "AB is a line segment and line l is its perpendicular bisector. If a point P lies on l, show that P is equidistant from A and B.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let line l intersect AB at point C. Since l is the perpendicular bisector of AB, AC = CB and angle PCA = angle PCB = 90 degrees. In triangles PCA and PCB: AC = CB (given), angle PCA = angle PCB (90 degrees), and PC = PC (common side). Therefore, triangle PCA is congruent to triangle PCB by SAS congruence criterion. By CPCT, PA = PB. Hence, any point on the perpendicular bisector of a line segment is equidistant from its endpoints.", "rubric_keywords": ["perpendicular bisector", "SAS congruence", "equidistant", "CPCT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the following scenario and answer the questions: In a triangular park ABC, AB = AC. A water fountain is placed at point D on BC such that AD is perpendicular to BC. (i) Name the congruent triangles formed by the altitude AD. (ii) Prove that AD bisects angle A. (iii) State the property used to prove angle B = angle C.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Triangle ABD and triangle ACD. (ii) In right triangles ABD and ACD, hypotenuse AB = hypotenuse AC, side AD = side AD (common), and angle ADB = angle ADC = 90 degrees. By RHS congruence criterion, triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACD. Thus, angle BAD = angle CAD by CPCT, proving AD bisects angle A. (iii) Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "altitude", "RHS congruence", "isosceles triangle properties"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is not a criterion for congruence of triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "SSA", "ASA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "SSA is not a valid criterion for congruence of triangles because two sides and a non-included angle do not uniquely determine a triangle.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSA", "congruence criterion", "non-included angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In \\Delta ABC and \\Delta DEF, AB = DE, BC = EF, and \\Delta ABC \\cong \\Delta DEF by SSS congruence. Which angle is equal to \\angle B?", "options": ["\\angle D", "\\angle E", "\\angle F", "None of these"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "\\angle E because by CPCT (Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles), the angle between sides AB and BC in \\Delta ABC corresponds to the angle between DE and EF in \\Delta DEF, which is \\angle E.", "rubric_keywords": ["CPCT", "angle E", "corresponding parts"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In an isosceles triangle \\Delta ABC with AB = AC, if \\angle B = 50^\\circ, find \\angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since AB = AC, the angles opposite to these sides are equal, so \\angle C = \\angle B = 50^\\circ. The sum of angles in a triangle is 180^\\circ, so \\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle A + 50^\\circ + 50^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle A = 80^\\circ.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "angles opposite to equal sides", "180 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Prove that angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let \\Delta ABC be an isosceles triangle with AB = AC. Draw the bisector of \\angle A intersecting BC at D. In \\Delta ABD and \\Delta ACD, AB = AC (given), \\angle BAD = \\angle CAD (by construction), and AD = AD (common). By SAS congruence, \\Delta ABD \\cong \\Delta ACD. Therefore, \\angle B = \\angle C by CPCT.", "rubric_keywords": ["bisector", "SAS congruence", "CPCT", "isosceles triangle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "AD is an altitude of an isosceles triangle ABC in which AB = AC. Show that (i) AD bisects BC and (ii) AD bisects \\angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "In right triangles \\Delta ADB and \\Delta ADC, hypotenuse AB = AC (given), side AD = AD (common), and \\angle ADB = \\angle ADC = 90^\\circ (since AD is an altitude). By RHS congruence criterion, \\Delta ADB \\cong \\Delta ADC. Hence, BD = CD (so AD bisects BC) and \\angle BAD = \\angle CAD (so AD bisects \\angle A) by CPCT.", "rubric_keywords": ["RHS congruence", "altitude", "bisects BC", "bisects angle A", "CPCT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Sides opposite to equal angles of a triangle are ___.", "options": ["equal", "unequal", "perpendicular", "parallel"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "equal. Theorem: If two angles of a triangle are equal, then the sides opposite to them are also equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["equal", "sides opposite to equal angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the congruence criteria in Column I with their descriptions in Column II.\nColumn I: 1. SSS, 2. SAS, 3. ASA, 4. RHS\nColumn II: A. Two sides and the included angle are equal, B. Three sides of one triangle are equal to three sides of another, C. Two angles and the included side are equal, D. Right angle, hypotenuse, and one side are equal.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D. SSS matches with three sides equal; SAS matches with two sides and included angle; ASA matches with two angles and included side; RHS matches with right-angled triangles having equal hypotenuse and side.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSS", "SAS", "ASA", "RHS", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In \\Delta PQR, if \\angle P = 70^\\circ and \\angle Q = 50^\\circ, arrange the sides of the triangle in ascending order of their lengths.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "First find \\angle R: \\angle R = 180^\\circ - (70^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = 60^\\circ. The angles in ascending order are \\angle Q (50^\\circ), \\angle R (60^\\circ), \\angle P (70^\\circ). Since the side opposite to the smaller angle is smaller, the sides in ascending order are PR (opposite to \\angle Q), PQ (opposite to \\angle R), and QR (opposite to \\angle P).", "rubric_keywords": ["angle R", "angle-side relationship", "ascending order", "PR < PQ < QR"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the altitude from one vertex of a triangle bisects the opposite side, then the triangle is isosceles.\nReason (R): In a triangle, angles opposite to equal sides are equal.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). Explanation: If altitude AD bisects BC, then in \\Delta ABD and \\Delta ACD, BD=CD, \\angle ADB=\\angle ADC=90^\\circ, AD=AD, so \\Delta ABD \\cong \\Delta ACD by SAS. Thus AB=AC, making it isosceles.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "isosceles", "SAS congruence"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Case Study: Two triangular park plots are designed such that in \\Delta ABC and \\Delta PQR, AB = PQ, BC = QR, and median AM = PN. (i) Prove that \\Delta ABM \\cong \\Delta PQN. (ii) Hence, prove that \\Delta ABC \\cong \\Delta PQR.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Since BC = QR, BM = MC = \\frac{1}{2}BC and QN = NR = \\frac{1}{2}QR, so BM = QN. In \\Delta ABM and \\Delta PQN, AB = PQ, BM = QN, and median AM = PN. By SSS congruence, \\Delta ABM \\cong \\Delta PQN. (ii) From (i), \\angle B = \\angle Q (CPCT). Now in \\Delta ABC and \\Delta PQR, AB = PQ, \\angle B = \\angle Q, and BC = QR. By SAS congruence, \\Delta ABC \\cong \\Delta PQR.", "rubric_keywords": ["median", "SSS congruence", "CPCT", "SAS congruence"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is not a criterion for congruence of triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "SSA", "ASA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "SSA is not a valid criterion for congruence of triangles because two sides and a non-included angle do not uniquely determine a triangle. The standard valid criteria are SAS, ASA, AAS, SSS, and RHS.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSA", "congruence criteria", "non-included angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Angles opposite to equal sides of a triangle are ___ .", "options": ["equal", "complementary", "supplementary", "unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "equal. Theorem: Angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["equal", "isosceles triangle", "sides"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the congruence criteria in Column I with their correct definitions in Column II.\nColumn I:\n1. SSS\n2. SAS\n3. ASA\n4. RHS\n\nColumn II:\nA. Two sides and the included angle of one triangle are equal to two sides and the included angle of the other triangle.\nB. Three sides of one triangle are equal to three sides of the other triangle.\nC. Hypotenuse and one side of a right-angled triangle are equal to the hypotenuse and one side of the other right-angled triangle.\nD. Two angles and the included side of one triangle are equal to two angles and the included side of the other triangle.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A", "1-D, 2-C, 3-A, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C. Explanation: SSS matches three sides equality, SAS matches two sides and included angle, ASA matches two angles and included side, and RHS matches right angle, hypotenuse, and side.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSS", "SAS", "ASA", "RHS", "congruence criteria"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the altitude from one vertex of a triangle bisects the opposite side, then the triangle is isosceles.\nReason (R): In an isosceles triangle, the altitude to the base bisects the base.\nOptions:\n(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true but R is false.\n(d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a)", "(b)", "(c)", "(d)"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. Proof: Let altitude AD bisect BC in triangle ABC. Then triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACD by SAS, so AB = AC by CPCTC.", "rubric_keywords": ["altitude", "bisects", "isosceles triangle", "CPCTC"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, if AB = AC and angle B = 50 degrees, find angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AB = AC, so angle C = angle B = 50 degrees (angles opposite to equal sides). By angle sum property, angle A + angle B + angle C = 180 degrees. angle A + 50 + 50 = 180 degrees, so angle A = 80 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles", "angle sum property", "80 degrees", "CPCTC"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Prove that the angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: Triangle ABC with AB = AC. To prove: angle B = angle C. Construction: Draw the bisector AD of angle A meeting BC at D. Proof: In triangle ABD and triangle ACD, AB = AC, angle BAD = angle CAD, and AD = AD (common). Triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACD by SAS criterion. Hence, angle B = angle C by CPCTC.", "rubric_keywords": ["construction", "angle bisector", "SAS criterion", "CPCTC"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In quadrilateral ACBD, AC = AD and AB bisects angle A. Show that triangle ABC is congruent to triangle ABD. What can you say about BC and BD?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "In triangle ABC and triangle ABD, AC = AD (given), angle CAB = angle DAB (AB bisects angle A), and AB = AB (common side). By SAS congruence criterion, triangle ABC is congruent to triangle ABD. By CPCTC, BC = BD.", "rubric_keywords": ["SAS congruence", "common side", "angle bisector", "BC = BD"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "BE and CF are two equal altitudes of a triangle ABC. Using RHS congruence rule, prove that the triangle ABC is isosceles.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given altitudes BE perpendicular to AC and CF perpendicular to AB such that BE = CF. Consider right triangles BEC and CFB. Hypotenuse BC = CB, BE = CF, angle BEC = angle CFB = 90 degrees. Triangle BEC is congruent to triangle CFB by RHS criterion. Hence angle BCE = angle CBF, meaning angle C = angle B. Therefore, AB = AC, so triangle ABC is isosceles.", "rubric_keywords": ["RHS congruence", "altitudes", "hypotenuse", "CPCTC", "isosceles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nA surveyor is mapping a triangular park PQR and measures side PQ = 5 cm and angle PQR = 60 degrees. Another triangular plot XYZ has XY = 5 cm and angle XYZ = 60 degrees.\n(i) Can the surveyor conclude that triangle PQR is congruent to triangle XYZ based only on this information? Justify.\n(ii) What additional information is required to prove congruence using the SAS criterion?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) No, because only one side and one angle are given equal, which is insufficient for congruence. (ii) To prove congruence by SAS, the length of the other side forming the angle 60 degrees (i.e., QR = YZ) must also be equal.", "rubric_keywords": ["insufficient information", "SAS criterion", "included side", "justification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that 'If in two triangles, two angles and the included side of one triangle are equal to two angles and the included side of the other triangle, then the two triangles are congruent' (ASA Congruence Rule).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Theorem (ASA Congruence): Given triangle ABC and triangle DEF with angle B = angle E, angle C = angle F, and BC = EF. To prove triangle ABC congruent to triangle DEF. Proof by contradiction: Assume AB != DE. Let AB < DE. Take point P on DE such that PE = AB. Join PC. Triangle ABC is congruent to triangle PEC by SAS. Hence angle ACB = angle PCE, which implies angle ACB = angle DFE only when P coincides with D. Thus AB = DE, and triangles are congruent by SAS.", "rubric_keywords": ["ASA congruence", "assumption", "SAS criterion", "contradiction", "CPCTC"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Which of the following is not a criterion for congruence of triangles?", "options": ["SAS", "ASA", "SSA", "SSS"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option C is correct. SSA (Side-Side-Angle) is not a valid criterion for triangle congruence because two triangles can have two corresponding sides and a non-included angle equal without being congruent.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSA", "congruence criterion", "not valid", "SAS"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "In a triangle, angles opposite to equal sides are ___.", "options": ["equal", "complementary", "supplementary", "unequal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Equal. Explanation: Theorem states that the angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal to each other.", "rubric_keywords": ["equal", "angles opposite to equal sides", "isosceles triangle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In \\triangle ABC, if AB = AC and \\angle B = 50^\\circ, find the measure of \\angle A.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given AB = AC, so \\triangle ABC is an isosceles triangle. Therefore, \\angle C = \\angle B = 50^\\circ (angles opposite to equal sides). Using the angle sum property of a triangle: \\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C = 180^\\circ. \\angle A + 50^\\circ + 50^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle A + 100^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle A = 80^\\circ.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "angle sum property", "80 degrees", "angles opposite to equal sides"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "2 sides of a triangle are of lengths 5 cm and 1.5 cm. The length of the third side of the triangle is ' x '. What is the range of values for x given that x is a whole number?", "options": [], "variables": {"side1": 5, "side2": 1.5}, "answer_key": "According to the triangle inequality theorem, the sum of any two sides of a triangle is always greater than the third side, and the difference between any two sides is less than the third side. Therefore, 5 - 1.5 < x < 5 + 1.5 \\implies 3.5 < x < 6.5. Since x is a whole number, the possible values for x are 4, 5, and 6.", "rubric_keywords": ["triangle inequality theorem", "sum of two sides", "difference of sides", "4, 5, 6"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If two sides and the included angle of one triangle are equal to two sides and the included angle of another triangle, the two triangles are congruent.\nReason (R): SAS congruence rule states that two triangles are congruent if two sides and the included angle of one triangle are equal to the corresponding two sides and the included angle of the other triangle.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.", "Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Option A is correct. Both Assertion and Reason are true, and the reason correctly defines the SAS congruence criterion which justifies the assertion.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "SAS congruence", "included angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the congruence criteria in Column I with their descriptions in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. SSS\n2. SAS\n3. ASA\n4. RHS\n\nColumn II:\nA. Two sides and the included angle are equal.\nB. Three sides of one triangle are equal to three sides of another.\nC. Right hypotenuse side for right-angled triangles.\nD. Two angles and the included side are equal.", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (SSS: Side-Side-Side), 2-A (SAS: Side-Angle-Side), 3-D (ASA: Angle-Side-Angle), 4-C (RHS: Right-angle-Hypotenuse-Side). Therefore, Option A is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["SSS", "SAS", "ASA", "RHS", "matching"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that angles opposite to equal sides of an isosceles triangle are equal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: An isosceles triangle ABC in which AB = AC.\nTo prove: \\angle B = \\angle C.\nConstruction: Draw the bisector AD of \\angle A meeting BC at D.\nProof: In \\triangle ABD and \\triangle ACD, AB = AC (Given), \\angle BAD = \\angle CAD (By construction), AD = AD (Common side). Therefore, \\triangle ABD \\cong \\triangle ACD by SAS congruence criterion. By CPCT (Corresponding Parts of Congruent Triangles are Equal), \\angle B = \\angle C. Hence proved.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "construction", "bisector", "SAS congruence", "CPCT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In a triangle ABC, if \\angle A = 60^\\circ and \\angle B = 80^\\circ, which side of the triangle is the longest?", "options": ["AB", "BC", "AC", "Cannot be determined"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "First, find \\angle C = 180^\\circ - (60^\\circ + 80^\\circ) = 40^\\circ. The largest angle is \\angle B = 80^\\circ. The side opposite to the largest angle is the longest side. The side opposite to \\angle B is AC. Therefore, AC is the longest side. Option C is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["longest side", "angle opposite to longest side", "80 degrees", "AC"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The exterior angle of a triangle is 110^\\circ and one of its interior opposite angles is 40^\\circ. Find the other interior opposite angle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "According to the exterior angle theorem, the measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of its two interior opposite angles. Let the other interior opposite angle be x. Then, 40^\\circ + x = 110^\\circ \\implies x = 110^\\circ - 40^\\circ = 70^\\circ.", "rubric_keywords": ["exterior angle theorem", "interior opposite angles", "70 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Triangles", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions:\nA farmer has a triangular plot of land PQR where PQ = PR. A line segment ST is drawn parallel to QR intersecting PQ at S and PR at T.\n(i) State the relation between \\angle PQR and \\angle PRQ. (1 mark)\n(ii) If \\angle P = 50^\\circ, find \\angle PQR. (1 mark)\n(iii) Show that \\triangle PST is also an isosceles triangle. (2 marks)", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) \\angle PQR = \\angle PRQ since angles opposite to equal sides (PQ = PR) of triangle PQR are equal.\n(ii) In \\triangle PQR, \\angle P + \\angle PQR + \\angle PRQ = 180^\\circ \\implies 50^\\circ + 2\\angle PQR = 180^\\circ \\implies 2\\angle PQR = 130^\\circ \\implies \\angle PQR = 65^\\circ$.\n(iii) Since ST \\parallel QR, corresponding angles give \\angle PST = \\angle PQR and \\angle PTS = \\angle PRQ. Since \\angle PQR = \\angle PRQ, we have \\angle PST = \\angle PTS. Therefore, sides opposite to equal angles in \\triangle PST are equal (PS = PT), proving \\triangle PST is an isosceles triangle.", "rubric_keywords": ["isosceles triangle", "corresponding angles", "65 degrees", "PS = PT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If the angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3: 5: 7: 9, then the measure of the smallest angle is:", "options": ["36°", "45°", "60°", "84°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x, 5x, 7x, and 9x. The sum of angles in a quadrilateral is 360°. Therefore, 3x + 5x + 7x + 9x = 360° => 24x = 360° => x = 15°. The smallest angle is 3x = 3 * 15° = 45°.", "rubric_keywords": ["ratio", "sum of angles", "360 degrees", "smallest angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Show that the diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular to each other.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let ABCD be a rhombus and its diagonals AC and BD intersect at point O. In triangles AOB and AOD, AB = AD (sides of a rhombus), AO = AO (common side), and BO = DO (diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other). By SSS congruence criterion, triangle AOB is congruent to triangle AOD. Therefore, angle AOB = angle AOD (by CPCTC). Since angle AOB + angle AOD = 180° (linear pair), 2 * angle AOB = 180°, which gives angle AOB = 90°.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus", "congruent triangles", "perpendicular", "diagonals"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The figure formed by joining the mid-points of the consecutive sides of an arbitrary quadrilateral is always a:", "options": ["Rhombus", "Square", "Parallelogram", "Rectangle"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "According to the mid-point theorem, the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half of it. Applying this theorem to triangles formed by the diagonals of any quadrilateral shows that the opposite sides of the inner quadrilateral are parallel and equal, forming a parallelogram.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallelogram", "consecutive sides", "quadrilateral"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The diagonals of a rectangle are equal and bisect each ___.", "options": ["other", "angle", "side", "perpendicularly"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "other; Explanation: Diagonals of a rectangle are equal in length and they bisect each other at the center.", "rubric_keywords": ["rectangle", "diagonals", "bisect", "equal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that a diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent triangles.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Statement: Let ABCD be a parallelogram and AC be its diagonal. Proof: In triangles ABC and CDA, AB = CD (opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal), BC = DA (opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal), and AC = AC (common side for both triangles). By SSS congruence criterion, triangle ABC is congruent to triangle CDA. Hence, diagonal AC divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallelogram", "diagonal", "congruent triangles", "SSS criterion"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then it is a parallelogram. Reason (R): Diagonals of a parallelogram always bisect each other.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). Explanation: The theorem states that if the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then it is a parallelogram, which is justified by the standard property that diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "diagonals bisect", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In a parallelogram ABCD, if angle A = (3x - 10)° and angle C = (2x + 30)°, find the measure of angle A and angle B.", "options": [], "variables": {"x_range": [1, 50]}, "answer_key": "In a parallelogram, opposite angles are equal. Therefore, angle A = angle C => 3x - 10 = 2x + 30 => x = 40. Substituting x = 40 into angle A: angle A = 3(40) - 10 = 110°. Since adjacent angles in a parallelogram are supplementary, angle A + angle B = 180° => 110° + angle B = 180° => angle B = 70°. Final answer: angle A = 110°, angle B = 70°.", "rubric_keywords": ["opposite angles", "supplementary", "parallelogram", "equations"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the quadrilaterals in Column I with their correct diagonal properties in Column II.\nColumn I:\n1. Rhombus\n2. Rectangle\n3. Square\n4. Parallelogram\nColumn II:\nA. Diagonals are equal and bisect at right angles.\nB. Diagonals bisect each other at right angles, but are not equal.\nC. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other, but not at right angles.\nD. Diagonals bisect each other, but are neither equal nor at right angles.", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (Rhombus diagonals bisect at right angles and are unequal), 2-C (Rectangle diagonals are equal and bisect each other, not at right angles), 3-A (Square diagonals are equal and bisect at right angles), 4-D (Parallelogram diagonals bisect each other, but are neither equal nor perpendicular). Correct option is 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["Rhombus", "Rectangle", "Square", "Parallelogram", "diagonals"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions: A triangular park ABC has side lengths AB = 6 cm, BC = 8 cm, and AC = 10 cm. Points D, E, and F are the mid-points of sides AB, BC, and CA respectively.\n(i) What is the length of side DE?\n(ii) What is the perimeter of triangle DEF formed by joining the mid-points?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) By the mid-point theorem, DE is parallel to AC and DE = 1/2 * AC = 1/2 * 10 = 5 cm. (ii) Similarly, EF = 1/2 * AB = 1/2 * 6 = 3 cm, and DF = 1/2 * BC = 1/2 * 8 = 4 cm. Perimeter of triangle DEF = DE + EF + DF = 5 + 3 + 4 = 12 cm.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallel", "triangle", "perimeter"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let ABC be a triangle where D and E are mid-points of AB and AC respectively. Extend DE to F such that DE = EF, and join CF. Triangle ADE is congruent to triangle CFE (by SAS criterion). Thus, AD = CF and angle DAE = angle FCE, which implies CF is parallel to AB (alternate interior angles). Since AD = BD (D is mid-point), BD = CF. Thus DBCF is a parallelogram, making DF parallel to BC, so DE is parallel to BC.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "congruent triangles", "parallel", "triangle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other at right angles, then it is a:", "options": ["Rhombus", "Rectangle", "Trapezium", "Kite"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Rhombus. A quadrilateral whose diagonals bisect each other at right angles is a rhombus.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonals", "bisect", "right angles", "rhombus"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The figure obtained by joining the mid-points of the sides of a quadrilateral taken in order is a:", "options": ["Rhombus", "Rectangle", "Parallelogram", "Square"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Parallelogram. By the Mid-point Theorem, the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side. Extending this to a quadrilateral, the inner figure formed is always a parallelogram.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-points", "sides", "quadrilateral", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent ___ .", "options": ["triangles", "rectangles", "squares", "rhombuses"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "triangles. A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent triangles by SAS congruence criterion.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonal", "parallelogram", "congruent", "triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3:5:9:13. Find all the angles of the quadrilateral.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x, 5x, 9x, and 13x. The sum of angles of a quadrilateral is 360 degrees. 3x + 5x + 9x + 13x = 360 -> 30x = 360 -> x = 12 degrees. The angles are 3(12) = 36 degrees, 5(12) = 60 degrees, 9(12) = 108 degrees, and 13(12) = 156 degrees. Final Answer: 36 degrees, 60 degrees, 108 degrees, 156 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["ratio", "sum of angles", "360 degrees", "36", "60", "108", "156"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Show that the diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular to each other.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let ABCD be a rhombus where diagonals AC and BD intersect at O. In triangles AOB and AOD, OB = OD (diagonals bisect), AO is common, and AB = AD (sides of rhombus). By SSS congruence, triangle AOB is congruent to triangle AOD. Thus, angle AOB = angle AOD. Since angle AOB + angle AOD = 180 degrees (linear pair), angle AOB = 90 degrees. Hence, diagonals are perpendicular.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus", "diagonals", "perpendicular", "congruent triangles", "90 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "ABCD is a rectangle in which diagonal AC bisects angle A as well as angle C. Show that ABCD is a square.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given ABCD is a rectangle, so angle A = angle C = 90 degrees and opposite sides are equal. Since diagonal AC bisects angle A, angle DAC = 45 degrees. In right-angled triangle ADC, angle D = 90 degrees and angle DAC = 45 degrees, so angle DCA = 45 degrees. Since angles opposite to equal sides are equal, AD = CD. Thus, adjacent sides of the rectangle are equal, which implies ABCD is a square.", "rubric_keywords": ["rectangle", "diagonal", "bisects", "adjacent sides equal", "square"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "ABC is a triangle right-angled at C. A line through the mid-point M of hypotenuse AB and parallel to BC intersects AC at D. Show that (i) D is the mid-point of AC, (ii) MD perpendicular to AC, (iii) CM = MA = 1/2 AB.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) In triangle ABC, M is mid-point of AB and MD parallel to BC. By converse of mid-point theorem, D is mid-point of AC. (ii) Since MD parallel to BC and AC is transversal, angle ADM = angle ACB = 90 degrees (corresponding angles). Thus MD perpendicular to AC. (iii) In triangles AMD and CMD, AD = CD, angle ADM = angle CDM = 90 degrees, DM = DM (common). By SAS congruence, triangle AMD is congruent to triangle CMD. Therefore, AM = CM (CPCT). Since M is mid-point of AB, AM = 1/2 AB. Thus CM = MA = 1/2 AB.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallel", "congruent triangles", "perpendicular", "half of hypotenuse"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then it is a parallelogram. Reason (R): Diagonals of a parallelogram always bisect each other.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A). If diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, opposite sides are equal and parallel, making it a parallelogram.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonals", "bisect", "parallelogram", "assertion", "reason"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Match the quadrilateral properties in Column I with their correct names in Column II. Column I: 1. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other at right angles. 2. Only one pair of opposite sides is parallel. 3. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other (not at right angles). 4. All sides are equal, diagonals bisect at right angles but are not equal. Column II: A. Rectangle, B. Square, C. Trapezium, D. Rhombus", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (Square: diagonals equal and bisect at 90 degrees), 2-C (Trapezium: one pair parallel), 3-A (Rectangle: diagonals equal and bisect, not at 90 degrees), 4-D (Rhombus: all sides equal, diagonals bisect at 90 degrees but unequal). Correct option: 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["square", "trapezium", "rectangle", "rhombus", "diagonals"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the passage and answer the questions below: A park has a quadrilateral shape PQRS where angle P = 75 degrees, angle Q = 105 degrees, and angle R = 75 degrees. (i) Find the measure of angle S. (ii) What type of quadrilateral is PQRS and why?", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Sum of all interior angles of a quadrilateral is 360 degrees. angle P + angle Q + angle R + angle S = 360 degrees -> 75 + 105 + 75 + angle S = 360 -> angle S = 360 - 255 = 105 degrees. (ii) Since angle P = angle R = 75 degrees and angle Q = angle S = 105 degrees, opposite angles are equal. Also, adjacent angles angle P + angle Q = 75 + 105 = 180 degrees (co-interior angles supplementary, implying PS parallel to QR), and similarly PQ parallel to SR. Therefore, PQRS is a parallelogram.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum of angles", "360 degrees", "105 degrees", "opposite angles equal", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If the angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3:5:9:13, then the measure of the largest angle is:", "options": ["108°", "140°", "156°", "180°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x, 5x, 9x, and 13x. The sum of angles in a quadrilateral is 360°. Therefore, 3x + 5x + 9x + 13x = 30x = 360°, which gives x = 12°. The largest angle is 13x = 13 * 12° = 156°.", "rubric_keywords": ["ratio", "sum of angles", "360 degrees", "largest angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Which of the following statements is NOT true for a general parallelogram?", "options": ["Opposite sides are equal", "Opposite angles are equal", "Diagonals bisect each other", "Diagonals are equal in length"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "In a general parallelogram, opposite sides are equal, opposite angles are equal, and diagonals bisect each other. However, diagonals are not necessarily equal (that property belongs to rectangles and squares). Hence, 'Diagonals are equal in length' is false.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallelogram properties", "diagonals", "false statement"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent ___.", "options": ["triangles", "squares", "rectangles", "parallelograms"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "triangles. Theorem: A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent triangles by SAS congruence criterion.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonal", "congruent", "triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Two opposite angles of a parallelogram are given as (3x - 10)° and (50 - x)°. Find the measure of each angle of the parallelogram.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal, we have 3x - 10 = 50 - x. Solving this gives 4x = 60, so x = 15. Substituting x = 15, the opposite angles are 3(15) - 10 = 35°. The adjacent angle is 180° - 35° = 145°. Thus, the four angles of the parallelogram are 35°, 145°, 35°, and 145°.", "rubric_keywords": ["opposite angles", "parallelogram", "linear equation", "35 and 145 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The perimeter of a rhombus is 52 cm and one of its diagonals is 24 cm. Find the length of its other diagonal.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Side of the rhombus = Perimeter / 4 = 52 / 4 = 13 cm. The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles (90°). Let half of the first diagonal be 24 / 2 = 12 cm. Using Pythagoras theorem in the right-angled triangle formed by half-diagonals and side: side^2 = (d1/2)^2 + (d2/2)^2 => 13^2 = 12^2 + (d2/2)^2 => 169 = 144 + (d2/2)^2 => (d2/2)^2 = 25 => d2/2 = 5 cm. Therefore, the other diagonal d2 = 10 cm.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus perimeter", "diagonals bisect at right angles", "Pythagoras theorem", "10 cm"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In triangle ABC, D, E, and F are respectively the mid-points of sides AB, BC, and CA. Show that triangle ABC is divided into four congruent triangles by joining D, E, and F.", "options": [], "variables": {"side_lengths": [6, 8, 10]}, "answer_key": "Since D and E are mid-points of AB and BC, by the mid-point theorem, DE || AC. Similarly, DF || BC and EF || AB. Therefore, DECF, BDEF, and DFAE are all parallelograms. Since DF is a diagonal of parallelogram BDEF, triangle BDF is congruent to triangle FED. Similarly, triangle DAE is congruent to triangle FED, and triangle EFC is congruent to triangle FED. Thus, all four smaller triangles are congruent to one another.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallelograms", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the quadrilaterals in Column I with their correct diagonal properties in Column II.\nColumn I: 1. Parallelogram, 2. Rhombus, 3. Rectangle, 4. Square\nColumn II: A. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other at right angles, B. Diagonals bisect each other but not at right angles, C. Diagonals bisect each other at right angles, D. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other.", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A", "1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (Parallelogram diagonals bisect each other), 2-C (Rhombus diagonals bisect at right angles), 3-D (Rectangle diagonals are equal and bisect), 4-A (Square diagonals are equal and bisect at right angles). Correct matching code is 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A.", "rubric_keywords": ["match the following", "diagonals properties", "rhombus", "rectangle", "square"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then it is a parallelogram.\nReason (R): Diagonals of a parallelogram always bisect each other.\nChoose the correct option: (a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "(c) A is true but R is false", "(d) A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both Assertion and Reason are true. The theorem stating that if diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other it is a parallelogram is the converse of the property that diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. Thus, option (a) is correct.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "parallelogram converse theorem", "diagonals bisect"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and is half of it (Mid-point Theorem).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: Triangle ABC where D and E are mid-points of AB and AC respectively. Construction: Draw CF parallel to BA intersecting DE produced to F. Proof: Triangle ADE and Triangle CFE are congruent by ASA criterion. Thus, AD = CF. Since AD = DB, we get BD = CF and BD || CF, making BCFD a parallelogram. Therefore, DF || BC, which implies DE || BC, and DE = 1/2 DF = 1/2 BC.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem proof", "congruent triangles", "parallel", "half of third side"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "ABCD is a rhombus and P, Q, R, and S are the mid-points of the sides AB, BC, CD, and DA respectively. Show that the quadrilateral PQRS is a rectangle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Join diagonals AC and BD of rhombus ABCD. In triangle ABC, P and Q are mid-points of AB and BC, so PQ || AC and PQ = 1/2 AC. In triangle ADC, R and S are mid-points of CD and DA, so SR || AC and SR = 1/2 AC. Thus, PQ || SR and PQ = SR, meaning PQRS is a parallelogram. Since diagonals of a rhombus intersect at 90°, the adjacent sides of PQRS are perpendicular, making PQRS a rectangle.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus mid-points", "rectangle proof", "diagonals at right angles", "mid-point theorem"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other at right angles, then the quadrilateral is a:", "options": ["Parallelogram", "Rectangle", "Rhombus", "Trapezium"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Rhombus. Explanation: A parallelogram whose diagonals bisect each other at right angles is a rhombus.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus", "diagonals", "bisect", "right angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3:5:9:13. Find the measure of the largest angle of the quadrilateral.", "options": ["130°", "156°", "169°", "180°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "156°. Explanation: Sum of angles = 360°. Let angles be 3x, 5x, 9x, 13x. 3x + 5x + 9x + 13x = 30x = 360° implies x = 12°. Largest angle = 13 * 12° = 156°.", "rubric_keywords": ["ratio", "sum of angles", "360 degrees", "largest angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent ___.", "options": ["triangles", "squares", "rectangles", "parallelograms"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "triangles. Explanation: By the ASA or SSS congruence criterion, a diagonal divides a parallelogram into two congruent triangles.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonal", "parallelogram", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Show that the diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular to each other.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let ABCD be a rhombus with diagonals AC and BD intersecting at O. Since a rhombus is a parallelogram, its diagonals bisect each other at O. In triangle AOB and triangle AOD, AB = AD (sides of a rhombus), AO = AO (common), and OB = OD (diagonals bisect). By SSS congruence, triangle AOB is congruent to triangle AOD. Hence, angle AOB = angle AOD. Since angle AOB + angle AOD = 180° (linear pair), each is 90°. Therefore, AC is perpendicular to BD.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus", "diagonals", "perpendicular", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Two opposite angles of a parallelogram are (3x - 2)° and (50 - x)°. Find the measure of each angle of the parallelogram.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal. Therefore, 3x - 2 = 50 - x implies 4x = 52 implies x = 13. Substituting x = 13, the angle is 3(13) - 2 = 37°. The adjacent angle is 180° - 37° = 143°. Thus, the four angles are 37°, 143°, 37°, and 143°.", "rubric_keywords": ["opposite angles", "equal", "parallelogram", "37 degrees", "143 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the properties of quadrilaterals in Column I with their correct names in Column II.\nColumn I:\n1. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other at right angles.\n2. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other (not at right angles).\n3. Only one pair of opposite sides is parallel.\n4. Diagonals bisect each other at right angles (not necessarily equal).\nColumn II:\nA. Rectangle\nB. Square\nC. Trapezium\nD. Rhombus", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (Square has equal diagonals bisecting at right angles), 2-A (Rectangle has equal diagonals bisecting each other), 3-C (Trapezium has one pair of parallel sides), 4-D (Rhombus has diagonals bisecting at right angles).", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "square", "rectangle", "trapezium", "rhombus"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and is half of it (Mid-point Theorem).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let triangle ABC be given with E and F as mid-points of AB and AC respectively. Extend FC to D such that CF = FD, and join BD. In triangle EAF and triangle DCF, AF = CF (F is mid-point of AC), angle EFA = angle DFC (vertically opposite angles), and EF = FD (by construction). By SAS congruence, triangle EAF is congruent to triangle DCF. Therefore, AE = CD and angle AEF = angle CDF. Since alternate interior angles are equal, AB is parallel to CD. Also AE = BE (given), so BE = CD. Thus, BCDE is a parallelogram, which implies EF is parallel to BC and EF = (1/2)BC.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallel", "half", "triangle", "congruence"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then it is a parallelogram. Reason (R): A quadrilateral is a parallelogram if its opposite sides are equal.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A). Explanation: Both statements are standard theorems regarding parallelograms, but the truth of (R) does not explain why diagonals bisecting each other implies a parallelogram.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "diagonals", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "ABCD is a parallelogram in which P and Q are mid-points of opposite sides AB and CD. If AC is a diagonal, show that line segments DP and BQ trisect the diagonal AC.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since ABCD is a parallelogram, AB = CD and AB is parallel to CD. Thus, AP = CQ and AP is parallel to CQ, making APCQ a parallelogram. DP and BQ intersect AC at points F and E respectively. Applying the converse of the mid-point theorem in triangle CQD and triangle APB, it can be proven that AF = FE = EC, which means that DP and BQ trisect AC.", "rubric_keywords": ["trisect", "diagonal", "mid-point", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "A park is in the shape of a quadrilateral ABCD with angle A = 70°, angle B = 80°, and angle C = 120°. (i) Find the measure of angle D. (ii) If the mid-points of sides AB, BC, CD, and DA are joined in order, name the figure so formed and justify your answer using the properties of quadrilaterals.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Sum of all interior angles of a quadrilateral = 360°. angle A + angle B + angle C + angle D = 360° implies 70° + 80° + 120° + angle D = 360° implies 270° + angle D = 360° implies angle D = 90°. (ii) By joining the mid-points of adjacent sides of any quadrilateral in order, the resulting figure is always a parallelogram (Varignon's Theorem), justified using the Mid-point Theorem in triangles formed by drawing a diagonal of the quadrilateral.", "rubric_keywords": ["angle sum property", "90 degrees", "mid-points", "parallelogram", "Varignon"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Three angles of a quadrilateral are 75 degrees, 90 degrees, and 75 degrees. The fourth angle is:", "options": ["120 degrees", "100 degrees", "110 degrees", "130 degrees"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Sum of angles in a quadrilateral = 360 degrees. Fourth angle = 360 - (75 + 90 + 75) = 360 - 240 = 120 degrees. Correct option is 120 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["quadrilateral angle sum", "360 degrees", "subtraction"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent ___.", "options": ["triangles", "quadrilaterals", "sectors", "circles"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Triangles. A diagonal connects opposite vertices and divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles based on SAS congruence criterion.", "rubric_keywords": ["triangles", "congruent", "diagonal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other at right angles, then it is a:", "options": ["Parallelogram", "Rectangle", "Rhombus", "Trapezium"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Rhombus. If diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other at right angles, it is a rhombus.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonals bisect at right angles", "rhombus", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3:5:9:13. Find all the angles of the quadrilateral.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x, 5x, 9x, and 13x. Sum of angles of a quadrilateral = 360 degrees. Therefore, 3x + 5x + 9x + 13x = 360 degrees => 30x = 360 degrees => x = 12 degrees. The angles are: 3(12) = 36 degrees, 5(12) = 60 degrees, 9(12) = 108 degrees, and 13(12) = 156 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["ratio", "360 degrees", "algebraic method", "angle values"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side. Reason (R): This is a direct consequence of the Mid-point Theorem.", "options": ["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true but R is false.", "A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. The statement is the fundamental definition and proof basis of the Mid-point theorem for triangles.", "rubric_keywords": ["Assertion", "Reason", "Mid-point theorem", "parallel"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the quadrilateral property in Column I with the correct quadrilateral type in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other at right angles.\n2. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other (not at 90 degrees).\n3. Diagonals bisect each other at right angles (not necessarily equal).\n4. Only one pair of opposite sides is parallel.\n\nColumn II:\nA. Rectangle\nB. Square\nC. Trapezium\nD. Rhombus", "options": ["1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (Square has equal diagonals and 90 degree bisecting angle), 2-A (Rectangle has equal diagonals but not 90 degrees), 3-D (Rhombus has 90 degree bisecting diagonals but unequal lengths), 4-C (Trapezium has one pair of parallel sides). Correct choice: 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C.", "rubric_keywords": ["Square", "Rectangle", "Rhombus", "Trapezium", "diagonals"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "Can a quadrilateral ABCD be formed if angle A = 50 degrees, angle B = 60 degrees, angle C = 70 degrees, and angle D = 190 degrees? Justify your answer.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "No, a quadrilateral cannot be formed. The sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be exactly 360 degrees. Here, 50 + 60 + 70 + 190 = 370 degrees, which exceeds 360 degrees.", "rubric_keywords": ["angle sum property", "360 degrees", "justification"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "D, E, and F are respectively the mid-points of the sides BC, CA, and AB of triangle ABC. Show that triangle ABC is divided into four congruent triangles by joining D, E, and F.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Since D and E are mid-points of BC and CA, DE is parallel to AB (by Mid-point theorem). Similarly, FE is parallel to BC and DF is parallel to AC. Therefore, AFDE, BDFE, and CDEF are all parallelograms. In parallelogram BDFE, DE is a diagonal which divides it into two congruent triangles FDE and DBE. Similarly, triangle FDE is congruent to triangle EAF and triangle DCF. Thus, all four triangles are congruent to each other.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallelograms", "congruent triangles", "proof"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the scenario and answer the questions: A school garden is in the shape of a quadrilateral PQRS where P(0,0), Q(4,0), R(5,3), and S(1,3) are corner points mapped on a coordinate grid.\n(i) Show that PQRS is a parallelogram.\n(ii) Find the length of diagonal PR.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) Length of PQ = sqrt((4-0)^2 + (0-0)^2) = 4. Length of SR = sqrt((5-1)^2 + (3-3)^2) = 4. Length of PS = sqrt((1-0)^2 + (3-0)^2) = sqrt(10). Length of QR = sqrt((5-4)^2 + (3-0)^2) = sqrt(10). Since opposite sides are equal (PQ = SR and PS = QR), PQRS is a parallelogram.\n(ii) Diagonal PR connects P(0,0) and R(5,3). Length of PR = sqrt((5-0)^2 + (3-0)^2) = sqrt(25 + 9) = sqrt(34) units.", "rubric_keywords": ["distance formula", "opposite sides", "parallelogram", "diagonal length"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and is half of it (Mid-point Theorem).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: Triangle ABC where E and D are mid-points of AB and AC respectively. To prove: (1) ED is parallel to BC, (2) ED = 1/2 of BC. Construction: Draw CF parallel to AB meeting ED produced at F. Proof: Prove triangle AED congruent to triangle CDF using ASA criterion. Then AE = CF = EB, so EB is parallel to CF and EB = CF, making EBCF a parallelogram. Hence EF is parallel to BC (so ED is parallel to BC) and EF = BC which implies ED = 1/2 BC.", "rubric_keywords": ["Mid-point theorem", "construction", "congruent triangles", "parallelogram property"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3 : 5 : 9 : 13. Find the smallest angle of the quadrilateral.", "options": ["27°", "36°", "45°", "54°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x, 5x, 9x, and 13x. By the angle sum property of a quadrilateral, the sum of all interior angles is 360°. So, 3x + 5x + 9x + 13x = 360° => 30x = 360° => x = 12°. The smallest angle is 3x = 3(12°) = 36°. Correct Option: 36°.", "rubric_keywords": ["angle sum property", "360°", "ratio", "smallest angle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other at right angles, then it is necessarily a:", "options": ["Rectangle", "Parallelogram", "Rhombus", "Kite"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "A quadrilateral whose diagonals bisect each other at right angles is a rhombus (and also a square as a special case, but rhombus is the defining general quadrilateral property). Specifically, parallelograms with perpendicular diagonals are rhombuses. Correct Option: Rhombus.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonals bisect", "right angles", "rhombus", "perpendicular"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The line segment joining the mid-points of any two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and is ___ of it.", "options": ["half", "equal", "double", "one-third"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Exact missing word: half. Explanation: According to the Mid-point Theorem, the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and equal to half of it.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "half", "parallel", "third side"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Two opposite angles of a parallelogram are (3x - 2)° and (50 - x)°. Find the measure of each angle of the parallelogram.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: We know that opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal. Therefore, 3x - 2 = 50 - x. Step 2: Solve for x: 3x + x = 50 + 2 => 4x = 52 => x = 13. Step 3: Substitute x back to find the angle measure: 3(13) - 2 = 39 - 2 = 37°. Step 4: The adjacent angle is 180° - 37° = 143°. Thus, the angles of the parallelogram are 37°, 143°, 37°, and 143°.", "rubric_keywords": ["opposite angles equal", "equation", "37°", "143°"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Show that the diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular to each other.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let ABCD be a rhombus where diagonals AC and BD intersect at O. Step 2: Since a rhombus is a parallelogram, its diagonals bisect each other at O (AO = CO and BO = DO). Also, adjacent sides are equal (AB = BC). Step 3: In triangles AOB and COB, AO = CO (diagonals bisect), OB = OB (common), and AB = BC (sides of a rhombus are equal). Step 4: By SSS congruence criterion, triangle AOB is congruent to triangle COB. Step 5: Therefore, ∠AOB = ∠COB (by CPCT). Since ∠AOB + ∠COB = 180° (linear pair), 2∠AOB = 180° => ∠AOB = 90°. Hence, diagonals are perpendicular.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus", "congruent triangles", "SSS", "perpendicular", "90°"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "ABC is a triangle right-angled at C. A line through the mid-point M of hypotenuse AB and parallel to BC intersects AC at D. Show that (i) D is the mid-point of AC, (ii) MD ⊥ AC, (iii) CM = MA = 1/2 AB.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: (i) In ΔABC, M is the mid-point of AB and MD || BC. By the converse of mid-point theorem, since the line drawn through the mid-point of one side parallel to another side bisects the third side, D is the mid-point of AC. Step 2: (ii) Since MD || BC and AC is a transversal, ∠ADM = ∠ACB (corresponding angles). Given ∠ACB = 90°, so ∠ADM = 90°. Therefore, MD ⊥ AC. Step 3: (iii) In ΔAMD and ΔCMD, AD = CD (D is mid-point of AC), ∠ADM = ∠CDM = 90°, and MD = MD (common). By SAS congruence, ΔAMD ≅ ΔCMD. Step 4: Therefore, MA = MC (by CPCT). Since M is the mid-point of AB, MA = 1/2 AB. Thus, CM = MA = 1/2 AB.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "congruence", "perpendicular", "hypotenuse", "CPCT"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): If the diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent triangles, then any parallelogram shares this property. Reason (R): A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two triangles of equal area and congruent shapes.", "options": ["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).", "(A) is true but (R) is false.", "(A) is false but (R) is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Explanation: A diagonal of any parallelogram divides it into two triangles that are congruent by ASA or SSS criteria (e.g., alternate interior angles and common diagonal). Therefore, both Assertion and Reason are true, and Reason correctly explains Assertion. Correct Option: Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "diagonal", "parallelogram", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the quadrilateral in Column I with its specific property in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Rhombus\n2. Rectangle\n3. Square\n4. Parallelogram\nColumn II:\nA. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other at right angles\nB. Diagonals bisect each other at right angles, not necessarily equal\nC. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other, but not at right angles\nD. Diagonals bisect each other", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Detailed Matching:\n1. Rhombus -> B (Diagonals bisect each other at right angles)\n2. Rectangle -> C (Diagonals are equal and bisect each other)\n3. Square -> A (Diagonals are equal and bisect at right angles)\n4. Parallelogram -> D (Diagonals bisect each other)\nCorrect Option: 1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D.", "rubric_keywords": ["match the following", "rhombus", "rectangle", "square", "parallelogram", "diagonals"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In a parallelogram ABCD, the ratio of two adjacent angles is 4 : 5. Find all the angles of the parallelogram.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: Let the adjacent angles be 4x and 5x. Step 2: Adjacent angles of a parallelogram are supplementary (sum to 180°). Therefore, 4x + 5x = 180° => 9x = 180° => x = 20°. Step 3: The angles are 4(20°) = 80° and 5(20°) = 100°. Step 4: Since opposite angles are equal, the four angles of the parallelogram are 80°, 100°, 80°, and 100°.", "rubric_keywords": ["adjacent angles", "supplementary", "80°", "100°"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 5, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "P, Q, R and S are respectively the mid-points of the sides AB, BC, CD and DA of a quadrilateral ABCD and AC is a diagonal. Show that: (i) SR || AC and SR = 1/2 AC, (ii) PQ = SR, (iii) PQRS is a parallelogram.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Step 1: In ΔDAC, S is the mid-point of DA and R is the mid-point of DC. By the mid-point theorem, SR || AC and SR = 1/2 AC. (Part i proved)\nStep 2: Similarly, in ΔBAC, P is the mid-point of AB and Q is the mid-point of BC. By the mid-point theorem, PQ || AC and PQ = 1/2 AC.\nStep 3: From (i) and (ii), SR || PQ and SR = PQ (= 1/2 AC). (Part ii proved)\nStep 4: Since one pair of opposite sides of quadrilateral PQRS is equal and parallel (PQ = SR and PQ || SR), PQRS is a parallelogram. (Part iii proved)", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallel", "congruence", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The sum of all the interior angles of a quadrilateral is:", "options": ["180°", "360°", "270°", "540°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "360°. By the angle sum property of a quadrilateral, dividing it into two triangles gives 2 * 180° = 360°.", "rubric_keywords": ["sum", "quadrilateral", "360 degrees", "angle sum property"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The diagonals of a rhombus always intersect each other at:", "options": ["60°", "45°", "90°", "120°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "90°. The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles (90°).", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus", "diagonals", "intersect", "90 degrees", "perpendicular"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "A diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent ___.", "options": ["triangles", "rectangles", "squares", "parallelograms"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "triangles. Theorem 8.1 states that a diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent triangles.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonal", "parallelogram", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3 : 5 : 9 : 13. Find all the angles of the quadrilateral.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x, 5x, 9x, and 13x. By angle sum property, 3x + 5x + 9x + 13x = 360°. Hence, 30x = 360°, which gives x = 12°. The angles are: 3(12°) = 36°, 5(12°) = 60°, 9(12°) = 108°, and 13(12°) = 156°.", "rubric_keywords": ["ratio", "angle sum property", "36 degrees", "60 degrees", "108 degrees", "156 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "If the diagonals of a parallelogram are equal, show that it is a rectangle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let ABCD be a parallelogram with AC = BD. In triangles ABC and DCB, AB = DC (opposite sides), BC = BC (common), and AC = DB (given). Triangle ABC is congruent to triangle DCB by SSS congruence. Thus, angle ABC = angle DCB. Since ABCD is a parallelogram, consecutive angles are supplementary: angle ABC + angle DCB = 180°, so angle ABC = 90°. Since one angle is 90°, the parallelogram is a rectangle.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonals equal", "parallelogram", "congruent triangles", "rectangle", "90 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Prove that the line segment joining the midpoints of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side (Mid-point Theorem).", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Given: In triangle ABC, E and F are midpoints of AB and AC respectively. To prove: EF || BC and EF = 1/2 BC. Construction: Draw CD parallel to BA meeting EF produced at D. Proof: Triangles AEF and CDF are congruent by ASA criterion. Thus AE = CD and EF = FD. Since AE = BE, BE = CD. Also BE || CD, making BCDE a parallelogram. Therefore ED || BC, which implies EF || BC, and ED = BC => 2EF = BC => EF = 1/2 BC.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallel", "congruent triangles", "construction", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "In a parallelogram ABCD, angle A = 65°. Find the measures of the remaining angles angle B, angle C, and angle D.", "options": [], "variables": {"angle_A": 65}, "answer_key": "Given angle A = 65°. Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal, so angle C = angle A = 65°. Consecutive angles are supplementary, so angle A + angle B = 180°, which gives angle B = 180° - 65° = 115°. Also, angle D = angle B = 115°. Therefore, angle B = 115°, angle C = 65°, and angle D = 115°.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallelogram", "opposite angles", "supplementary", "65 degrees", "115 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the quadrilateral types in Column I with their diagonal properties in Column II:\nColumn I:\n1. Parallelogram\n2. Rhombus\n3. Rectangle\n4. Square\n\nColumn II:\nA. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other at right angles\nB. Diagonals bisect each other but not necessarily at right angles\nC. Diagonals bisect each other at right angles but are not equal\nD. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A", "1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D", "1-C, 2-A, 3-B, 4-D", "1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A. Parallelogram diagonals bisect each other (B). Rhombus diagonals bisect at right angles, unequal (C). Rectangle diagonals are equal and bisect (D). Square diagonals are equal and perpendicular (A).", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "diagonals", "rhombus", "rectangle", "square", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The line segment joining the midpoints of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side.\nReason (R): Equal chords of a circle subtend equal angles at the centre.\nChoose the correct option:\n(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.\n(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.\n(c) A is true but R is false.\n(d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.", "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "(c) A is true but R is false.", "(d) A is false but R is true."], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. Both statements are standard geometric theorems, but R pertains to circles and is unrelated to the mid-point theorem in triangles.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "mid-point theorem", "true", "unrelated"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case study and answer the questions:\nA town planner designs a square residential park ABCD. Points P, Q, R, and S are the midpoints of sides AB, BC, CD, and DA respectively.\n(i) What type of quadrilateral is PQRS formed by joining the consecutive midpoints?\n(ii) If the diagonal AC of square ABCD measures 12 cm, find the length of side PQ.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) PQRS is a rhombus. By applying the mid-point theorem in triangles ABC and ADC, PQ || AC and PQ = 1/2 AC, SR || AC and SR = 1/2 AC. Thus PQ = SR and PQ || SR, making PQRS a parallelogram with adjacent sides equal (since diagonals of square ABCD are equal and perpendicular), hence a rhombus.\n(ii) In triangle ABC, PQ = 1/2 AC = 1/2 * 12 = 6 cm.", "rubric_keywords": ["case study", "midpoint theorem", "rhombus", "6 cm", "diagonal"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "If the angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 3:5:9:13, then the measure of the smallest angle is:", "options": ["36°", "60°", "108°", "156°"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let the angles be 3x, 5x, 9x, and 13x. The sum of angles of a quadrilateral is 360°. So, 3x + 5x + 9x + 13x = 360° => 30x = 360° => x = 12°. Smallest angle = 3x = 3(12°) = 36°.", "rubric_keywords": ["ratio", "360 degrees", "smallest angle", "36 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Prove that a diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent triangles.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Consider parallelogram ABCD with diagonal AC. In triangles ABC and CDA: AB = CD (opposite sides of a parallelogram), BC = DA (opposite sides), and AC = AC (common side). Therefore, by SSS congruence criterion, triangle ABC is congruent to triangle CDA.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonal", "parallelogram", "congruent triangles", "SSS criterion"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Fill_In_The_Blanks", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Easy", "seed_question": "The line segment joining the mid-points of any two sides of a triangle is ___ to the third side.", "options": ["parallel", "perpendicular", "equal", "bisecting"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "parallel. According to the Mid-point Theorem, the line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half of it.", "rubric_keywords": ["mid-point theorem", "parallel", "third side"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "MCQ", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Which of the following is not a property of a parallelogram?", "options": ["Opposite sides are equal", "Opposite angles are equal", "Diagonals bisect each other", "Diagonals are equal"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Diagonals are equal is a property of rectangles and squares, not necessarily of a general parallelogram.", "rubric_keywords": ["parallelogram property", "diagonals are equal", "rectangle"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Long_Answer", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Show that if the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other at right angles, then it is a rhombus.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let ABCD be a quadrilateral whose diagonals AC and BD intersect at O such that OA = OC, OB = OD, and ∠AOB = 90°. In triangles AOB and AOD, OA = OA (common), OB = OD (given), and ∠AOB = ∠AOD = 90°. By SAS congruence, triangle AOB is congruent to triangle AOD, so AB = AD. Similarly, adjacent sides can be shown equal, proving all four sides are equal (AB = BC = CD = DA). Thus, ABCD is a rhombus.", "rubric_keywords": ["diagonals bisect", "right angles", "rhombus", "SAS congruence"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Numerical", "marks_weightage": 3, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "ABCD is a rhombus in which altitude from D to side AB bisects AB. Find the angles of the rhombus.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let DE be the altitude to AB such that AE = EB. In right triangle AED and BED, DE = DE, AE = BE, and ∠AED = ∠BED = 90°. By SAS, triangle AED is congruent to triangle BED, so AD = BD. Since ABCD is a rhombus, AD = AB. Thus AD = AB = BD, meaning triangle ABD is an equilateral triangle. Therefore, ∠A = 60° and ∠B = 120°. Opposite angles of a rhombus are equal, so angles are 60°, 120°, 60°, 120°.", "rubric_keywords": ["rhombus", "altitude", "equilateral triangle", "60 degrees", "120 degrees"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the special quadrilaterals in Column I with their correct diagonal property in Column II.\nColumn I: 1. Parallelogram, 2. Rectangle, 3. Rhombus, 4. Square\nColumn II: A. Diagonals are equal and bisect at right angles, B. Diagonals bisect each other but not necessarily at right angles, C. Diagonals are equal and bisect each other, D. Diagonals bisect each other at right angles but are not equal.", "options": ["1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "1-B (Parallelogram diagonals bisect each other), 2-C (Rectangle diagonals are equal and bisect each other), 3-D (Rhombus diagonals bisect at right angles but are unequal), 4-A (Square diagonals are equal and bisect at right angles).", "rubric_keywords": ["match", "parallelogram", "rectangle", "rhombus", "square", "diagonals"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Assertion_Reason", "marks_weightage": 1, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Assertion (A): The quadrilateral formed by joining the mid-points of the consecutive sides of a quadrilateral is a parallelogram.\nReason (R): The line segment joining the mid-points of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and half of it.\nChoose the correct option: (a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. (b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A. (c) A is true but R is false. (d) A is false but R is true.", "options": ["(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A", "(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A", "(c) A is true but R is false", "(d) A is false but R is true"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A. The mid-point theorem is directly applied to triangles formed by diagonals of the quadrilateral to prove the inner figure is a parallelogram.", "rubric_keywords": ["assertion reason", "mid-point theorem", "parallelogram"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Short_Answer", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "The angle between two altitudes of a parallelogram through the vertex of an obtuse angle of the parallelogram is 60°. Find the angles of the parallelogram.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Let ABCD be a parallelogram with obtuse angle C and acute angle A. Altitudes from vertex D to sides AB and BC form a quadrilateral with the vertex angle. The sum of opposite angles in that quadrilateral gives the angle between altitudes as 60° supplement, so the obtuse angle of parallelogram is 180° - 60° = 120°. The adjacent angle is 60°. Thus, angles are 60°, 120°, 60°, 120°.", "rubric_keywords": ["altitudes", "obtuse angle", "parallelogram angles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Quadrilaterals", "question_type": "Case_Based", "marks_weightage": 4, "difficulty": "Hard", "seed_question": "Read the case and answer the questions:\nA triangular garden has sides 10m, 24m, and 26m. A fence is built by joining the mid-points of the sides of this triangular plot to form an inner triangular pathway.\n(i) What is the perimeter of the inner pathway?\n(ii) Name the geometric shape formed by the four smaller triangles inside the original triangle.", "options": [], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "(i) By mid-point theorem, the sides of the inner triangle are half of the sides of the outer triangle (i.e., 5m, 12m, 13m). Perimeter = 5 + 12 + 13 = 30 m.\n(ii) The four smaller triangles formed are congruent triangles.", "rubric_keywords": ["case based", "mid-point theorem", "perimeter", "congruent triangles"]}
{"board": "KARNATAKA_STATE_BOARD_SSLC", "class": "Class_9", "subject": "Mathematics", "chapter": "Circles", "question_type": "Match_The_Following", "marks_weightage": 2, "difficulty": "Medium", "seed_question": "Match the concepts in Column I with their correct counterparts in Column II for Circles (Laws & Theorems vs Mathematical Equations):\nColumn I:\n  1. First Fundamental Law\n  2. Invariant Relation\n  3. Inverse Square Theorem\n  4. Proportional Limit\nColumn II:\n  A. 1/r^2 dependency\n  B. Direct linear slope\n  C. State conservation\n  D. Bounded ceiling", "options": ["1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A", "1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D", "1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D", "1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B"], "variables": {}, "answer_key": "Correct Match: 1-B, 2-D, 3-C, 4-A. Step-by-step reasoning: Concept 1 strictly maps to (B), Item 2 corresponds to (D), Property 3 aligns with (C), and Metric 4 defines (A).", "rubric_keywords": ["circles", "matching", "column_i", "column_ii"]}
